[Annotator "https://lichess.org/@/rudisco"]

{ THEMES and Things to Look For
- General opening principles
- How to accomplish objectives through making plans and clearing obstacles to those plans
- Keeping the king safe in the opening

Stream footage here: www.youtube.com/watch?v=Ytkf3qZTj74
Game played here: www.chess.com/live/game/5640593191 }
1. e4 { [%c_effect e4;square;e4;type;Book] [%clk 0:03:02] } 1... e5 { [%clk 0:03:01] } 2. Nf3 { [%clk 0:03:02] } 2... Nc6 { [%clk 0:03:01] } 3. d4 { The Scotch game is one of the oldest openings, idea is to immediately try and contest the center.
The first time the moves e4 d4 were ever played was by Napoleon Bonaparte (!!!), at least in Danya's Chessbase database search. The veracity of the games are disputed, according to chess historian Edward Winter Napoleon's games may have been made up as a propaganda tool.
Why is it called the Scotch? In 1824, several games were hosted in the city of Edinburgh by members of the Edinburgh chess club against the London chess club where e4 d4 were played many times. It was common for these types of games to be played. } { [%clk 0:03:03] } 3... exd4 { [%clk 0:02:53] } 4. Nxd4 { Downside of Nxd4 Scotch is that white has now moved the same piece twice in the opening, hindering development } { [%csl Gg1,Gf3][%cal Gg1f3,Gf3d4] [%clk 0:03:04] } 4... Bc5 { Black now gets to develop with tempo by attacking white's hanging piece and getting the dark squared bishop into the center of the board } { [%csl Gf8,Gc5,Rd4][%cal Gf8c5,Rc5d4] [%clk 0:02:41] } 5. Nxc6 { [%clk 0:03:01] } 5... Qf6 { Instead of immediately recapturing on c6, Danya opts to threaten 6.Qxf2 checkmate } { [%cal Rc5f2,Rf6f2] [%clk 0:02:37] } 6. f3 { Downside of f2 weakens the king and white prevents themselves from castling due to the c5 bishop staring down the diagonal. Be careful when moving up pawns on the kingside as it will weaken the kin's position. } { [%cal Rc5g1,Bf2f3,Bg2g3,Bh2h3] [%clk 0:02:57] } (6. Qf3 Qxf3 7. gxf3 dxc6 { This is the line white should have taken and is a "known" endgame. }) 6... dxc6 { Danya: Why am I doubling my pawns?
In the opening development is more important than the pawn structure. } { [%clk 0:02:18] } (6... Qxc6 { Could have recaptured with the Queen to prevent doubled pawns, however this prevents the c8 bishop from being developed. } 7. Nc3 d6 { d6 later may be a tempting move to open up the diagonal for the c8 bishop, but now with the queen on c6, it opens up black for a devastating pin.
TACTIC: when the queen and king are on the same diagonal, look for pins } { [%csl Rc6,Re8][%cal Rf1b5,Rb5e8,Gc8g4] }) 7. Bc4 { [%clk 0:02:52] } 7... Ne7 { [%clk 0:02:14] } (7... Qh4+ { Tempting move because... } 8. Kf1 (8. g3 { However, white can simply block with the pawn. Be careful when going for checks as if they are easily prevented, you waste a tempo. }) 8... Qf2# { ... is mate }) 8. Bf4?? { White tunnel visions here, and hangs the dark squared bishop } { [%cal Rf6f4] [%clk 0:02:41] } (8. Nc3 { White's idea should be to get the king to safety, one way is to castle queenside (long) and that requires all the pieces between the king and a1 rook to be out of the way. } { [%cal Gd1e2,Gc1d2] }) (8. Be3 { The other way for white to castle is kingside (short) and that requires the diagonal that black's c5 bishop is controlling, one way to do that is be3, but that doesn't work because white does not have another piece defending e3 } { [%cal Be1g1,Rc5g1] }) (8. Qe2 O-O { Can be3 be played now? No, that would leave b2 hanging. Therefore the c3 square needs to occupied to block black's queen from attacking it. } { [%cal Rf6b2] } 9. c3 { Blocking black's queen access to the b2 pawn and finally allowing white to play be3 and castle. kc3 also would work to block black's queen. }) 8... Qxf4 { [%clk 0:02:04] } 9. Qd2 { Danya: Does black want to trade queens? } { [%clk 0:02:40] } 9... Qe5 { [%clk 0:01:43] } (9... Bf2+ { Idea here is to draw the king defending the queen away... however, this doesn't work because the queen is also defended by the knight } { [%csl Bb1][%cal Bb1d2] } 10. Kxf2 Qxd2+ 11. Nxd2 { and this just leads to black losing the bishop in the exchange }) 10. f4?? { After an opponent moves, scan the board for hanging pieces, in this case f4 and b2 are hanging. Which pawn to take? } { [%csl Rf4,Rb2] [%clk 0:02:29] } 10... Qxb2 { White takes the b2 hanging pawn because this also results in being able to capture the now hanging a8 rook. When you see a good move (10...Qxf4), look for a better one.
TACTIC: note that the white queen and king are on the same diagonal, these are setups for pinning tactics to win white's queen. } { [%csl Ga1,Be1,Bd2][%cal Gb2a1] [%clk 0:01:24] } 11. f5 { [%clk 0:02:15] } 11... Qxa1 { White does not bother to defend the rook with 11.Qc3, so we take the rook and threaten the b1 knight. Note that white cannot castle to get the king out of danger due to moving the f pawn } { [%csl Rb1][%cal Rc5g1,Ra1b1] [%clk 0:01:19] } 12. Ke2 { [%clk 0:02:08] } 12... Qe5 { We don't want the queen getting stuck on a1 and retreat it back to the center, also pinning the e4 pawn to the king.
TAKEAWAYS
- Be careful about pawn moves in front of your king
- Development is more important than pawn structure especially in the opening
- When we make plans, we try to understand what we want to accomplish, what the obstacles are, and how to remove those obstacles. Solving the obstacles leads to a plan, a plan allows us to accomplish the objective. } { [%cal Re5e2] } 0-1


