[Annotator "https://lichess.org/@/rudisco"]

{ THEMES and Things to Look For
- Converting games with material advantages by developing pieces, opening up the position, and making easy trades
- Pinning tactics

Stream footage here: www.youtube.com/watch?v=HiCeU8tIh0U
Game played here: www.chess.com/live/game/5640642915 }
1. e4 { [%clk 0:03:02] } 1... e5 { [%clk 0:03:00] } 2. Qh5 { Very common move at the beginner level and attacks the f7 pawn. } { [%clk 0:03:03] } 2... Nc6 { [%clk 0:02:51] } 3. Bc4 { SCHOLARS MATE ALERT } { [%csl Rf7][%cal Rc4f7,Rh5f7] [%clk 0:03:03] } 3... g6 { [%clk 0:02:38] } (3... Qe7 { Inaccurate because it blocks the development of the f8 bishop } { [%csl Bf8] } 4. Nc3 { Also allows white to develop the knight into the center with tempo. We avoid all of this by playing 3...g6 } { [%csl Re7][%cal Gc3d5,Rd5e7] }) (3... Qf6 { Qf6 is also inaccurate because it also exposes the queen and blocks the knight even if it allows the bishop to develop } { [%csl Bg8][%cal Bg8f6] }) 4. Qf3 { Threatening mate once again. } { [%csl Rf7][%cal Rf3f7,Rc4f7] [%clk 0:03:02] } 4... Nf6 { Develop the knight while also stopping checkmate. The black knight is supported by the black queen. } { [%csl Gf6][%cal Rf3f6,Gd8f6] [%clk 0:02:35] } 5. d3 { [%clk 0:02:59] } 5... Nd4 { The queen is annoying and we would like to stop the possibility of scholar's mate by chasing it away. kd4 accomplishes this while also giving the knight a central position.
Yes, this does result in black moving the knight twice in the opening which is against general opening principles, but all principles have exceptions. In this case, white has already moved the queen twice so black can afford to delay their development as well to get rid of the queen. } { [%clk 0:02:25] } (5... Bg7 { Danya does note that in an actual classical game, he would have played this. }) 6. Qe3 { Whenever there is a knight on d4 look for... } { [%csl Rc2][%cal Rd4c2,Rc2e1,Rc2a1,Rc2e3] [%clk 0:02:34] } (6. Qd1 { This is the only move for white. } 6... Bg7 { White would like to chase the knight away from d4 } { [%csl Rd4][%cal Rc3d4,Gc2c3] } 7. c3 (7. Nf3 { This is also possible, but... } 7... Nxf3+ 8. Qxf3 { ... black has now moved the queen three times and it is, once again, exposed to attack. })) 6... Nxc2+ { ... the fork. Don't take the rook, look for the better move. The king will also have to move and will not be allowed to castle. } { [%csl Rc2][%cal Rc2a1,Rc2e3,Rc2e1] [%clk 0:02:22] } 7. Ke2 { [%clk 0:02:06] } 7... Nxe3 { [%clk 0:02:20] } 8. Bxe3 { When you're up a queen and your opponent's king is out in the open, your goal is to open up the center to allow you to leverage your queen material advantage. } { [%clk 0:02:08] } 8... d5 { Two defenders, two attackers, d5 is successfully defended.
Danya: How does one decide when to trade when you are up material?
It depends. There is no hard rule for when one should trade when up material.
Danya then sets up a sample position here: https://lichess.org/study/rsvXy6Pu/rNEPRocm } { [%csl Rd5][%cal Rd8d5,Rf6d5,Rc4d5,Re4d5] [%clk 0:01:57] } (8... Bg7 { Also fine to further development by fianchettoing first then castling } { [%cal Ge8g8] }) (8... c6 { Can also prepare e5 with c6, but it's unnecessary since d5 is already under black's control }) 9. exd5 { [%clk 0:02:06] } 9... Nxd5 { [%clk 0:01:58] } 10. Nf3 { Knight attacks the hanging pawn. } { [%cal Rf3e5] [%clk 0:02:06] } 10... Bg7 { Danya chooses not to immediately trade and instead "fianchetto" which is an Italian word which means to develop a bishop to the long diagonal. This both develops the bishop onto the long diagonal and defends the pawn.
Trading at this moment is unnecessary because the position is open and the knight may be further used to attack the king. } { [%csl Gg7][%cal Gg7e5,Rd5e3] [%clk 0:01:48] } 11. Nc3 { Now white controls d5 } { [%cal Rc3d5,Rc4d5,Rd8d5] [%clk 0:02:03] } 11... Nxc3+ { Now black trades to avoid giving white a tempo by moving the knight off of the white controlled d5 } { [%clk 0:01:41] } 12. bxc3 { [%clk 0:02:05] } 12... O-O { [%clk 0:01:41] } 13. d4 { [%clk 0:02:06] } 13... exd4 { [%clk 0:01:29] } 14. cxd4 { Are there open files (files missing pawns) that can be used to develop a piece? } { [%cal Be8e1] [%clk 0:02:05] } 14... Re8 { Yes, e8 is a great place for the rook to x-ray the king and pin the bishop. This opens the center and gets our pieces to active squares. } { [%csl Re3][%cal Re8e1] [%clk 0:01:17] } 15. Rhd1 { [%clk 0:02:04] } 15... Bg4 { [%clk 0:01:17] } 16. h3 { [%clk 0:02:04] } 16... Bxf3+ { [%clk 0:01:18] } 17. gxf3 { The bishop continues to be pinned which means it cannot move, this leaves the d4 pawn hanging since black attacks it more times than it is defended. } { [%csl Re3,Rd4][%cal Re8e2,Rg7d4,Gd1d4,Gd8d4] [%clk 0:02:05] } 17... Bxd4 { So we take it. } { [%clk 0:01:01] } 18. Rab1 { We cannot immediately take the bishop because it is pinned due to the rook has x-raying the queen } { [%cal Rd4e3,Rd1d8] [%clk 0:02:00] } 18... Qf6 { We must first move the queen out of the way while still defending the bishop because it is still under attack by the rook. Now we are ready to take the bishop. } { [%csl Gd4][%cal Gf6d4,Rd1d4] [%clk 0:00:42] } (18... Rb8 { Many beginners would automatically play rb8 to defend the b pawn. In this case, don't be worried about losing a pawn when up a queen. Just go straight for creating threats against white. } { [%csl Rb7][%cal Rb1b7,Rb8b7] }) 19. Rxd4 { We are allowed to re-take with the queen because the white bishop is still pinned by the rook. } { [%csl Re3][%cal Rf6d4,Re8e2] [%clk 0:01:51] } 19... Qxd4 { [%clk 0:00:42] } 20. Rc1 { When converting an advantage, you want to bring all of your pieces into the game. In this case the a8 rook isn't doing anything so... } { [%csl Ba8] [%clk 0:01:50] } 20... Rad8 { we bring it into the center where it can reinforce the open d file. } { [%clk 0:00:34] } (20... Qb2+ { Note that this does not win the rook because... } 21. Kf1 { ... the king un-pins the bishop ... } { [%csl Rc1,Re3][%cal Re8e3,Re3c1] } 21... Qxc1+ { [%cal Re3c1] } (21... Rxe3 { Black can however win the rook in the exchange by removing the bishop defender } 22. fxe3 Qxc1+) 22. Bxc1 { ... and now can defend white's rook }) 21. Kf1 { Now the bishop is no longer pinned and could capture the queen } { [%cal Re3d4] [%clk 0:01:41] } 21... Rxe3 { Sacrificing the rook for the bishop because black is so far ahead in material } { [%clk 0:00:34] } 22. fxe3 { [%clk 0:01:41] } 22... Qxe3 { [%clk 0:00:35] } 23. Re1 { [%clk 0:01:34] } 23... Qxf3+ { [%clk 0:00:35] } 24. Kg1 { We can win the pawn, but there is no need to with such an overwhelming material advantage and checkmate in sight. } { [%cal Rf3h3] [%clk 0:01:32] } 24... Rd2 { [%clk 0:00:24] } 25. Re2 { Another example of black winning through both trading and not trading. } { [%clk 0:01:29] } 25... Rd1+ { [%clk 0:00:23] } (25... Rxe2 { If black trades then... } 26. Bxe2 Qxe2 { ... black can then run the pawn up } { [%cal Gc7c1] }) 26. Kh2 { 0-1 Black wins. } { After not trading black sets up mate in 1 } { [%cal Rf3h1] [%clk 0:01:19] } 0-1


