[StudyName "Sensei Danya Speedrun (Part 1)"]
[ChapterName "Game 3: Ponziani Gambit (Black, ELO 476)"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/dSNQO9Uq/Ld6sb9S8"]
[Annotator "https://lichess.org/@/rudisco"]

{ THEMES and Things to Look For
- Following opening principles will allow you to gain an edge over opponents who play seemingly intimidating moves that ignore those principles.
- Look for better moves! Sometimes you can ignore winning material to go for a mate.
- Watch out for tempo plays. When developing your pieces, be aware of moves that you may allow your opponent to play with tempo.
- It is not mandatory to take the queen if your opponent offers the sacrifice. One can ignore queen sacrifices, especially if taking the queen results in a losing position for the queen taker.

Stream footage here: https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=3knyQ8z7lrQ
Game played here: https://www.chess.com/live/game/5640733433 }
1. e4 { [%c_effect e4;square;e4;type;Book] [%clk 0:05:00] } 1... e5 { [%c_effect e5;square;e5;type;Book] [%clk 0:04:56] } 2. Bc4 { Start of the "bishop's opening" } { [%clk 0:04:59] } 2... Nf6 { Developing the knight and attacking the pawn. } { [%csl Re4][%cal Rf6e4] [%clk 0:04:50] } 3. d4 { White attempts to immediately open the center. } { [%clk 0:04:57] } 3... exd4 { [%clk 0:04:35] } 4. e5 { This is a line in the Ponziani. } { [%clk 0:04:56] } 4... d5 { Black looking to counterattack white's bishop while also developing the pawn. This also opens up black's light squared bishop for development. } { [%csl Rc4,Rf6][%cal Rd5c4,Re5f6,Gc8g4] [%clk 0:04:15] } (4... Ne4 { Simply moving the knight away both violates the opening principle of not moving a piece more than once in the opening and results in... } 5. Qxd4 { ... taking the pawn and attacking the knight with tempo. } { [%csl Re4][%cal Rd4e4] } 5... Nc5 { Forcing black to move the knight once again. } { [%csl Gc5][%cal Gf8c5] }) 5. exf6 { [%clk 0:04:52] } (5. Bb5+ { Does this win a piece for white? } 5... c6 { One possible response from black that continues to attack the bishop. } { [%csl Rb5][%cal Rc6b5] } (5... Nfd7 { The best move for black, blocking the check and moving the knight out of harms way. }) (5... Nc6 { Among the worst moves for black as now white will win material. } 6. exf6 { Black with no compensation for the knight here. })) (5. exd6 Bxd6 { Can white take the seemingly hanging pawn? } { [%csl Rd4][%cal Rd1d4] } 6. Qxd4 Bb4+ { No, black can unleash a nasty discovered attack on the queen with a check and white has no way to prevent the loss of the queen. } { [%csl Rd4,Re1][%cal Rd8d4,Rb4e1] } 7. c3 Qxd4 { White's pawn is pinned and black wins the queen. } { [%csl Re1,Rc3][%cal Rb4e1] }) 5... dxc4 { [%clk 0:04:12] } 6. Qe2+ { [%clk 0:04:50] } 6... Be6 { White looks like they have many threats here, but black maintains an edge here due to better development. } { [%csl Re8,Rg7][%cal Re2e8,Rf6g7] [%clk 0:03:58] } 7. fxg7 { [%clk 0:04:38] } 7... Bxg7 { [%clk 0:03:56] } 8. Qh5 { [%clk 0:04:33] } 8... Nc6 { Ignoring white's attempts at intimidation and simply developing the knight. } { [%clk 0:03:46] } 9. Nf3 { Should black castle kingside or queenside in this position? } { [%clk 0:04:33] } 9... Qd7 { Black should prepare to castle queenside (long) as black's kingside pawn structure is damaged and white has some threats on that side of the board. } { [%csl Gb8,Bg7,Gg5,Rf7][%cal Ge8b8,Gf3g5,Rg5f7,Rh5f7] [%clk 0:03:31] } (9... O-O 10. Ng5 { This position is perfectly fine for black, but why create attacking chances for white? } { [%csl Rh7][%cal Rg5h7,Rh5h7] }) (9... Qe7 { Why not Qe7? } 10. O-O O-O-O 11. Bg5 { To avoid allowing white to play this nasty move pinning the queen on the rook with the dark squared bishop. } { [%csl Re7,Rd8][%cal Rg5d8] } 11... f6 { Yes, black has f6, but this blunts the influence of black's bishop. This is simply a line that black can avoid by playing Qd7 instead. } { [%cal Gg7f6] }) 10. Ng5 { [%clk 0:04:19] } 10... O-O-O { [%clk 0:03:30] } 11. O-O { [%clk 0:04:01] } 11... Bd5 { Further centralizing the bishop, more effectively pointing it at the white's king. } { [%csl Rg2][%cal Rd5g2] [%clk 0:03:27] } 12. a4 { At this point, a hollow queenside attacking threat from white who has no other pieces developed on that side of the board. } { [%clk 0:03:46] } 12... Rdg8 { Taking advantage of the semi open g file. Black prepares to put more pressure on white's king. } { [%csl Rg2][%cal Rg8g2] [%clk 0:03:10] } 13. Nxf7?? { [%clk 0:03:46] } 13... Bxf7 { Black wins the exchange here. } { [%clk 0:03:02] } 14. Qb5 { [%clk 0:03:36] } 14... Bd5 { Putting the bishop back on a central attacking square. } { [%csl Rg2][%cal Rd5g2] [%clk 0:02:46] } 15. a5 { White wants to play a6. } { [%csl Ga6][%cal Ga5a6] [%clk 0:03:25] } 15... a6 { Black stops white from playing a6 by playing it themselves. } { [%clk 0:02:28] } 16. Qc5 { [%clk 0:03:17] } (16. Qa4 { This allows an elegant line from black once black recognizes that if white's g2 pawn were not there, then there would be a strong checkmate idea along the open g file. } { [%csl Bg2,Rg1,Rh1][%cal Rg8g1,Rd5h1] } 16... Qh3 { Effectively forcing black to accept the sacrifice of the queen for mate. } (16... Bxg2 { Danya is correct that this line doesn't work as well because you are sacrificing the light squared bishop that is controlling h1 that allows the mate in the first place. However, there is still a forced mating line here. } 17. Kxg2 (17. Bf4 { Very, very hard move to find that prolongs the game, but mate is still inevitable. } 17... Qh3 18. Qxc4 Bf3 19. Qe6+ Qxe6 20. Nd2 Bh6+ 21. Bg3 Bxd2 22. Ra3 Ne5 23. Rxf3 Nxf3+ 24. Kg2 Nh4+ 25. Kh1 Qe2 26. Ra1 Qf3+ 27. Kg1 Qg2# { Pretty wild line to find, but it exists. }) 17... Qg4+ { Most intuitive move that leads to this mating line. } 18. Kh1 Qf3+ 19. Kg1 Be5+ 20. Bg5 Rxg5#) 17. gxh3 (17. f3 { This line still leads to a forced mate. } 17... Be5 18. Rf2 { Trying to open up space for the king to escape. } { [%csl Gf1][%cal Gg1f1] } 18... Rxg2+ 19. Kf1 Rxf2+ 20. Kxf2 Qxh2+ 21. Kf1 Bxf3 22. b3 Qg2+ 23. Ke1 Qe2# { However it's not enough and this line is more or less forced. }) 17... Bf6+ 18. Bg5 Rxg5#) 16... Bf8 { Trapping white's queen and unleashing a discovered attack } { [%csl Rc5,Rg2][%cal Rf8c5,Rg8g2,Rd5g2] [%clk 0:02:09] } (16... Qh3 { Does Qc5 from white prevent the previous mating idea? } 17. Qxd5 { Yes, white can simply decline to take the hanging queen and, instead, capture the light squared bishop that is making the mating idea possible. } (17. gxh3 Bf6+ 18. Bg5 Rxg5# { If white accepts the Botez gambit, then the same mating idea can be forced. })) 17. Na3 { White ignores both threats. What is the best move for black? } { [%csl Rc5,Rg2][%cal Rf8c5,Rg8g2,Rd5g2] [%clk 0:03:17] } 17... Rxg2+ { [%clk 0:01:54] } 18. Kh1 { A nice mate in 2 pattern here. } { [%clk 0:03:14] } 18... Rg1+ { Sacrificing the rook for... } { [%clk 0:01:31] } (18... Rg4+ { This still leads to mate, but complicates things because white has... } 19. f3) (18... Rxf2+ { This line leads to a windmill checkmating idea. } 19. Kg1 Rg2+ { Moving the rook back. } 20. Kh1 Rg3+ { Then out of the way again to allow the discovered check and checkmate. } 21. Rf3 (21. Qxd5 Qxd5+ 22. Rf3 Qxf3#) 21... Bxf3#) 19. Kxg1 { [%clk 0:03:01] } 19... Qg4# { 0-1 Black wins by checkmate. } { ... checkmate. } { [%clk 0:01:28] } 0-1


