{ THEMES and Things to Look For
- Prophylaxis. Finding moves that prevent your opponent from executing on their ideas.

Stream footage here: https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=aEKSPFwvFoY
Game played here: https://www.chess.com/live/game/5650690441 }
1. f3 { Poor opening move by white as this already weakens the kingside pawn structure. } { [%clk 0:04:53] } 1... e5 { [%clk 0:04:55] } 2. d3 { White plays... a poor choice of opening, how does black respond?
Danya doesn't recommend newer players to use these off beat, weak openings as they do not help develop good fundamentals. } { [%clk 0:04:50] } 2... d5 { Black should take control of the center and support that center by developing minor pieces. } { [%csl Be5,Bd5] [%clk 0:04:39] } 3. e4 { [%clk 0:04:46] } 3... Nf6 { Simply supporting the center pawn. } { [%csl Gd5][%cal Gf6d5] [%clk 0:04:29] } (3... d4? { This is a positional mistake for black.
The reason for this is that is closes the center and doesn't allow black to take full advantage of white's weaknesses. Note that Bf5 no longer prevents white from castling kingside. } { [%csl Gc5][%cal Gf8c5] }) (3... c6 { Another perfectly reasonable move for black to support the center pawns. }) 4. exd5 { [%clk 0:04:44] } 4... Nxd5 { [%clk 0:04:22] } 5. c4 { White continues to play pawn moves that appear to be aggressive, but actually weakens their position in the long run. } { [%clk 0:04:39] } 5... Nf6 { [%clk 0:04:18] } 6. Bg5 { [%clk 0:04:34] } (6. Be3 { This is probably the right square for white's bishop. This stops black from playing Bc5 ideas. } { [%csl Gc5][%cal Ge3c5,Gf8c5] }) 6... Bc5 { Long term idea is to stop white from castling kingside. Note how white's choice of 1. f3 directly allows black to undermine white's king. } { [%csl Rg1,Bf3][%cal Rc5g1] [%clk 0:04:15] } 7. h4 { [%clk 0:04:30] } 7... Nc6 { Black continues to develop, ignoring white's passive moves. } { [%clk 0:04:06] } (7... h5? { An example of poor prophylactic thinking. Black sees h5 as a phantom threat and tries to stop it even though h5 isn't a threat. }) 8. Nc3 { [%clk 0:04:24] } 8... h6 { Forcing white to give up this pin. } { [%csl Rd8,Rg5][%cal Rg5d8,Rh6g5] [%clk 0:03:49] } 9. Bxf6 { [%clk 0:04:22] } 9... Qxf6 { [%clk 0:03:48] } 10. Nd5 { White looks like they've finally got some activity on the board, finding a good square for the knight. Note how much more active black's pieces are where as white's light squared bishop and g1 knight have effectively nowhere to go. } { [%csl Rc7,Bc5,Bc6,Bc8][%cal Rd5c7] [%clk 0:04:19] } (10. Ne4) 10... Qd8 { Defending the pawn and forking square. } { [%csl Rc7,Ra8,Re8][%cal Gd8c7,Rd5c7,Rc7e8,Rc7a8] [%clk 0:03:38] } 11. Rb1 { White with ideas of playing b4. } { [%csl Gb4][%cal Gb2b4] [%clk 0:04:03] } 11... a5 { Danya plays a5 to demonstrate prophylaxis. Black directly stopping white from playing b4. } { [%csl Gb4][%cal Ga5b4,Gb2b4] [%clk 0:03:22] } (11... Be6 { Also fine to just continue developing. } 12. b4) 12. a3 { White continues to try and play b4. } { [%clk 0:04:00] } 12... a4 { Dulling the impact of b4 as now black can meet b4 with axb3 (en passant) . } { [%clk 0:03:10] } 13. b4 { [%clk 0:03:57] } 13... axb3 { [%clk 0:03:10] } 14. Rxb3 { [%clk 0:03:56] } 14... Nd4 { Developing the knight with tempo and opening up c6 for the pawn to dislodge white's knight. } { [%csl Rb3,Gc6][%cal Rd4b3,Gc7c6] [%clk 0:02:46] } 15. Rc3 { [%clk 0:03:50] } 15... c6 { Forcing white's knight back. } { [%clk 0:02:45] } 16. Ne3 { [%csl Ra3][%cal Rc5a3,Ra8a3] [%clk 0:03:34] } 16... Bxa3 { Taking the hanging pawn and threatening a material winning pin. } { [%csl Re1,Rc3][%cal Rb4e1,Ga3b4] [%clk 0:02:24] } 17. g4?? { Completely missing the pin. } { [%clk 0:03:20] } 17... Bb4 { [%clk 0:02:23] } 18. Qb3?? { Hanging the queen. } { [%clk 0:03:10] } 18... Nxb3 { [%clk 0:02:16] } 0-1


