1. e3 { Another game where black should respond by occupying the center. } { [%clk 0:04:54] } 1... e5 { [%clk 0:04:58] } 2. Nc3 { [%clk 0:04:46] } 2... d5 { Following the plan of taking control of the center. } { [%csl Rd5][%cal Gd8d5,Rc3d5] [%clk 0:04:52] } 3. d4 { [%clk 0:04:31] } 3... e4 { Black chooses to close the center. Note white's knight is poorly positioned. White would really like to play c4 to attack the base and undermine black's pawn chain. } { [%csl Bc3] [%clk 0:04:38] } (3... Nc6? { What is the problem with this move? } 4. dxe5 Nxe5 { This hangs the d pawn. } { [%csl Gd5][%cal Rd1d5,Rc3d5,Gd8d5] } 5. Nxd5 { White is now up the exchange. }) 4. Bb5+? { A toothless check from white, allowing black to develop with tempo. } { [%clk 0:04:25] } (4. f3 { In this case, attacking the tip of the pawn chain is also okay with the idea of giving white's light squared bishop a better square to develop to. } { [%csl Gd3][%cal Gf1d3] }) 4... c6 { A nice pawn chain for black. } { [%csl Rb5,Bb7,Bc6,Bd5,Be4][%cal Rc6b5] [%clk 0:04:22] } (4... Bd7 { Offering the trade here is okay, but c6 is just a better move. } 5. Bxd7+ Qxd7) 5. Be2 { [%clk 0:04:21] } 5... Bd6 { [%clk 0:04:06] } 6. Bg4 { [%clk 0:04:16] } (6. b3 { This is a common plan for black in the French. The dark squared bishop is horribly positioned and in this case white is the one which may have ideas of exchanging a bad bishop for a good one by playing a4 then Ba3. } { [%csl Ga3,Ga4][%cal Ga2a4,Gc1a3] }) 6... f5 { No need to trade and allow white to develop the queen. Again black supports the center with tempo. } { [%clk 0:03:49] } (6... Nf6 { Also a fine move. Developing the knight and if white elects to trade light squared bishops... } 7. Bxc8 Qxc8 { ... black has a perfectly pleasant position with strong center control and better developed minor pieces. } { [%csl Bd6,Bf6] }) 7. Bh5+ { Another poor check. } { [%clk 0:04:11] } 7... g6 { [%clk 0:03:45] } 8. Be2 { [%clk 0:04:10] } 8... Nf6 { Black calmly continues development. } { [%clk 0:03:31] } 9. Nh3 { [%clk 0:04:08] } 9... O-O { [%clk 0:03:29] } 10. Ng5 { [%clk 0:04:07] } (10. O-O { White should have castled here. Black would have ideas of starting a pawn storm to attack white's king. g5 is the move to start the pawn storm, but white currently controls that square with the h3 knight. How can black prep g5? } { [%cal Gf5f4,Gg6g5,Gg5g4] } 10... h6 { This strategically violates the principle of not pushing pawns in front of a castled king. Black is allowed to do this here because:
1) white has no threats on the kingside
2) white's king is more vulnerable, black has more threats on the kingside } 11. a3 g5 12. b3 f4 13. exf4 Bxh3 14. gxh3 gxf4) 10... h6 { Chasing the knight away. } { [%clk 0:03:21] } 11. Ne6?? { Blundering the knight. } { [%clk 0:04:06] } 11... Bxe6 { [%clk 0:03:19] } 12. f3 { White offers black a chance to open the center with exf3. Should black accept? } { [%clk 0:04:01] } 12... exf3 { Yes! Black has material advantage and can best leverage that by opening up lanes of attack against white's king. } { [%clk 0:02:51] } 13. Bxf3 { By taking with the pawn, white weakens their control over the e4 square. } { [%csl Be4] [%clk 0:03:58] } 13... Ne4 { So black outposts the knight. } { [%clk 0:02:44] } 14. Bxe4 { Which pawn does black take with? } { [%clk 0:03:56] } 14... fxe4 { The f pawn to stop white from castling. } { [%csl Rf1][%cal Rf8f1] [%clk 0:02:36] } 15. Rf1 { Now that black has opened up the f file, how should black take full advantage of white's king stuck in the center? } { [%clk 0:03:51] } 15... Qh4+ { [%csl Rh2,Re1][%cal Rh4h2,Rh4e1] [%clk 0:02:18] } 16. g3 { Should black immediately take the pawn? } { [%clk 0:03:32] } 16... Rxf1+ { No, black can delay taking the h pawn and draw white's king further into the open. } { [%clk 0:02:09] } 17. Kxf1 { [%clk 0:03:29] } 17... Qxh2 { [%clk 0:02:04] } 18. Ke1 { Black has a simple mate in two. } { [%clk 0:03:09] } 18... Bxg3+ { [%clk 0:01:50] } 19. Kf1 { [%clk 0:03:07] } 19... Bh3# { 0-1 Black wins by checkmate. } { [%clk 0:01:35] } 0-1


