{ THEMES and Things to Look For
- Never premove when you have time unless it is literally the only move possible.
- Playing an open center.
- When an opponent moves a pawn, note the squares in their position that they weaken. Squares where it is impossible for an opponent to control with a pawn are good candidates for outposts or angles of attack!
- When playing white, look for outpost possibilities on d5.

Games referenced
Smyslov - Rudakovsky Moscow 1945 (https://www.chessgames.com/perl/chessgame?gid=1125461)
Kamsky - Naroditsky Saint Louis 2017 (https://www.chessgames.com/perl/chessgame?gid=1870176)

Stream footage here: https://youtu.be/PHAmfkkrSSs
Game played here: https://www.chess.com/live/game/5650922698 }
1. e4 { [%clk 0:05:00] } 1... e5 { [%clk 0:04:59] } 2. Nf3 { [%clk 0:04:54] } 2... Nc6 { [%clk 0:04:57] } 3. Bc4 { This is the Italian opening, emphasizing good opening principles. Making moves that control the center and develop minor pieces. } { [%clk 0:04:54] } 3... d6 { [%clk 0:04:54] } (3... Bc5 { This is the main line. }) (3... Be7 { This is also a line, the Hungarian. }) 4. c3 { Preparing d4. } { [%csl Gd4][%cal Gd2d4] [%clk 0:04:33] } 4... Be6? { Offering this exchange is not good for black because... } { [%clk 0:04:36] } (4... Bg4 { This blunders a pawn and possibly mate if one is not careful. } 5. Qb3 { [%csl Rf7,Rb7][%cal Rc4f7,Rb3b7,Rb3f7] } 5... Bxf3 6. Bxf7+ Ke7 7. Qe6#) 5. Bxe6 { [%clk 0:04:31] } 5... fxe6 { ... after the exchange black's kingside pawn structure is damaged and gives white Qd3. } { [%csl Gb3,Re6,Rb7][%cal Gd1b3,Rb3e6,Rb3b7] [%clk 0:04:34] } 6. d4 { White chooses to follow through with controlling the center with pawns. } { [%clk 0:04:24] } 6... exd4 { [%clk 0:04:32] } 7. cxd4 { [%clk 0:04:23] } 7... e5 { Does white close the center? } { [%clk 0:04:28] } 8. Nc3 { White choosing to allow an open center since they are slightly better developed than black. } { [%clk 0:03:54] } (8. d5 { White would also be okay with closing the center in this position even though it doesn't allow white to fully take advantage of their lead in development. This is okay because white has compensation for closing. } 8... Nce7 9. Ng5 { More than enough compensation for white, that outposted knight will be a (k)nightmare to deal with. } { [%csl Ge6][%cal Gg5e6] } 9... Nf6 10. Ne6 Qd7 { Fully stopping black from castling anytime soon. Note that the light squared bishops were traded making this outposted knight even more difficult to remove. } 11. Nc3 { [%csl Ge4][%cal Gc3e4] } 11... c6 { Black's best try here is probably to try and undermine the pawn chain protecting the outposted knight. }) 8... exd4 { Black chooses to open the center up and trade some material. } { [%clk 0:04:26] } 9. Nxd4 { [%clk 0:03:53] } 9... Nxd4 { [%clk 0:04:19] } 10. Qxd4 { [%clk 0:03:52] } 10... Nf6 { [%clk 0:04:07] } (10... c5? { This is a mistake because it weakens the d5 square. Note that after c6, black has no way to control the d5 square with a pawn. } { [%csl Bd5] } 11. Qc4 Nf6 { White would like to outpost the knight on the weak square black has created. } { [%csl Gd5][%cal Gc3d5] } 12. Bg5 { White should prepare Nd5 by removing black's last defender of the square. } (12. Nd5 { White should not do this immediately as black can simply trade. } 12... Nxd5 13. Qxd5) 12... Be7 13. Bxf6 Bxf6 14. Nd5 { And now white achieves their objective and has a positional advantage even though material is equal. }) 11. O-O { [%clk 0:03:41] } 11... Ng4? { Ng4 looks scary, but really presents no threat. } { [%clk 0:04:01] } 12. Bf4 { White more or less ignores the knight and continues developing. Also nice that the bishop defends h2. } { [%csl Gh2][%cal Gf4h2] [%clk 0:03:15] } 12... g5?? { Black blunders the rook. } { [%csl Rh8][%cal Rd4h8] [%clk 0:03:48] } 13. Qxh8 { [%clk 0:03:00] } 13... gxf4 { [%clk 0:03:47] } 14. Qxh7 { [%clk 0:02:58] } 14... Qf6 { [%clk 0:03:33] } 15. Qh5+ { Boom the final fork. } { [%csl Rg4,Re8][%cal Rh5g4,Rh5e8] [%clk 0:02:46] } 1-0


