{ THEMES and Things to Look For
- Don't forget to develop your pieces when up material. This is critical to converting advantages as it allows you to fully leverage your extra material.
- Taking material (usually pawns) at the cost of development can prove to be deadly.

Games referenced

Stream footage here: https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=hzotV0aslmY
Game played here: https://www.chess.com/live/game/5678422182 }
1. e4 { [%clk 0:04:59] } 1... d5 { Start of the Scandi } { [%clk 0:04:56] } 2. exd5 { [%clk 0:04:56] } 2... Qxd5 { [%clk 0:04:46] } 3. Nc3 { [%csl Rd5][%cal Rc3d5] [%clk 0:04:55] } 3... Qd8 { [%clk 0:04:42] } (3... Qa5 { This is the main line Scandi. }) 4. d4 { White occupying the center. } { [%clk 0:04:46] } 4... Nc6? { Black attacking the pawn as white only has one defender. This is also a known mistake in the scandi opening. } { [%csl Rd4][%cal Rc6d4,Rd8d4,Gd1d4] [%clk 0:04:40] } 5. Nf3 { Defending the pawn and developing. } { [%csl Gd4][%cal Gf3d4,Gd1d4] [%clk 0:04:38] } 5... Nf6 { [%clk 0:04:36] } 6. d5 { Danya violates an opening principle here by moving the same piece twice in the opening. However, this is okay because black also wasted tempos moving the queen around and black has not occupied the center with any pawns.
Black is not able to take the pawn because white has the same number of defenders as attackers. } { [%csl Rc6][%cal Gd1d5,Gc3d5,Rd5c6] [%clk 0:03:58] } 6... Nxd5?? { Black just takes anyway and blunders the knight. } { [%clk 0:04:31] } (6... Nb4 { Nb4 seems like a scary move. } { [%csl Rd5][%cal Rb4d5,Rf6d5,Rd8d5] } 7. Bc4 { Developing the bishop and defending the pawn. } 7... Bf5 { [%csl Rc2][%cal Rf5c2,Rb4c2] } 8. Nd4 { This looks unintuitive as it blocks the queen's defense of the pawn. White can sacrifice the pawn for rapid development. } (8. Bb3 { This is also good. Positioning the bishop to do double defensive duty. Preparing a3 to push the knight out. } { [%cal Gb3c2,Gb3d5,Ga2a3] }) 8... Bg6 9. O-O Nbxd5?? { This is a great example of a poisoned pawn. } 10. Nxd5 Nxd5?? { By retaking black walks into a strong tactic which wins white a lot of material. } 11. Bb5+ c6 12. Nxc6 bxc6 13. Bxc6+ Qd7 14. Bxd7+ { First the queen. } 14... Kxd7 (14... Kd8 { Black can try and avoid losing immediately with this, but down a queen and soon to be down a knight, black's position is hopeless. }) 15. Qxd5+ { Then the knight. } 15... Kc7 16. Qxa8 { And finally the rook. }) 7. Nxd5 { [%clk 0:03:55] } 7... Bg4 { Black pinning the knight to the king. } { [%csl Rd1][%cal Rg4d1] [%clk 0:04:23] } 8. Be2 { Unpinning the knight and developing the light squared bishop. } { [%clk 0:03:46] } 8... e5 { [%clk 0:04:12] } 9. O-O { Sticking to the plan of completing development and getting the king to safety. } { [%clk 0:03:37] } 9... Bc5 { How can white complete development of the dark squared bishop? } { [%csl Bc1] [%clk 0:04:00] } (9... e4 { This is a neat idea for black. } 10. Bf4 { This is the right line for white to take which results in winning a minor piece. White threatens a nasty fork here. } { [%csl Rc7,Re8,Ra8][%cal Rd5c7,Rc7e8,Rc7a8] } (10. Ne1? { This is a mistake as it allows black to overload the queen as the only defender of two pieces. } { [%csl Ge2,Gd5][%cal Gd1e2,Gd1d5] } 10... Bxe2 11. Nxc7+ { Not retaking immediately so white can get as much as it can for the minor piece. } (11. Qxe2 Qxd5 { The straightforward line loses white a full piece. }) 11... Qxc7 12. Qxe2) (10. Nd4 { This hangs the knight and white will lose the exchange here. } { [%csl Rd5][%cal Rd8d5] } 10... Qxd5 11. Bxg4 Nxd4) (10. Ng5 { Again the queen is overloaded here and white loses the exxchange. } { [%csl Gd5,Ge2][%cal Gd1d5,Gd1e2] } 10... Bxe2 11. Qxe2 Qxd5) 10... Bd6 (10... exf3 11. gxf3 { What white should do first is to eliminate the possibility of the pawn fork first. } (11. Nxc7+ { White does have to be careful here, immediately taking with the knight is an error. } 11... Qxc7 12. Bxc7 fxe2 { Black's pawn is setup for a fork here. } { [%csl Rd1,Rf1][%cal Re2d1,Re2f1] }) 11... Bd6 12. fxg4 Bxf4 13. Nxf4 { And here white is up the exchange. }) 11. Nd2 Bxf4 12. Nxf4 Qd6 13. Nxe4 Qxf4 14. Bxg4) 10. Be3 { By blunting black's dark squared bishop and offering an exchange. White is more than happy to trade material as white is up material. } { [%clk 0:03:20] } 10... Bxe3 { What piece should white take with? } { [%clk 0:03:45] } 11. Nxe3 { The knight! White opens up two attacks and, again, isn't afraid to trade into an endgame up material. } { [%csl Rg4,Rd8][%cal Re3g4,Rd1d8] [%clk 0:03:06] } 11... e4 { [%clk 0:03:42] } (11... Bd7 { A better move for black, saving the bishop and preventing the trade. } 12. Qd5 { Possible continuation for white, exploiting black's passive pieces. } { [%csl Re5][%cal Rd5e5,Rf3e5] }) (11... Qc8 { Qc8 is tempting, but runs into a nasty tactic. } 12. Nxg4 Qxg4 { [%csl Rg4][%cal Re2g4] } 13. Nxe5 { Black must move the queen and white will win a pawn in the exchange. } { [%csl Rg4,Rc6][%cal Re5g4,Re5c6,Re2g4] }) 12. Qxd8+ { Trading the queens first. } { [%clk 0:02:47] } 12... Rxd8 { [%clk 0:03:37] } 13. Nxg4 { [%clk 0:02:46] } 13... exf3 { Trading minor pieces. } { [%clk 0:03:34] } 14. Bxf3 { [%clk 0:02:45] } 14... O-O { [%clk 0:03:30] } 15. Bxc6 { Again trading, and ruining black's pawn structure. } { [%clk 0:02:23] } 15... bxc6 { [%clk 0:03:26] } 16. Rad1 { White offers the trade while developing the rook to an open file. } { [%clk 0:02:19] } 16... Rxd1 { [%clk 0:03:22] } 17. Rxd1 { [%clk 0:02:16] } 17... h5 { [%clk 0:03:19] } 18. Ne5 { [%clk 0:02:13] } 18... g5 { Black blunders a pawn. } { [%csl Rc6][%cal Re5c6] [%clk 0:03:16] } 19. Nxc6 { [%clk 0:01:59] } 19... Re8 { [%clk 0:03:12] } 20. Rd8 { Forcing the trade as black's rook is pinned. The backrank mate Re1# isn't possible due to the pin. } { [%csl Rg8,Re8][%cal Rd8g8] [%clk 0:01:41] } (20. Kf1 { Assuming a backrank existed this is preferable to h3 because it stops black from playing Re2 and centralizes the queen. } { [%csl Ge2][%cal Gf1e2,Ge8e2] }) 20... Kf8 { [%clk 0:02:57] } 21. Rxe8+ { [%clk 0:01:40] } 21... Kxe8 { [%clk 0:02:55] } 22. Nxa7 { White continues to grab material. } { [%clk 0:01:40] } 22... f5 { What is white's plan here? } { [%clk 0:02:53] } 23. Kf1 { 1-0 White wins. } { Bringing the king out and then try and promote the passed pawn. } { [%cal Ga2a4,Ga4a5] [%clk 0:01:24] } 1-0


