{ THEMES and Things to Look For
- The player with a large developmental advantage should look to open the position even if it means sacrificing a pawn or ruining their own pawn structure.
- Sometimes pawns are not important.
- Observation and pattern recognition are a key skill in chess.\
- The weakest pieces to defend a piece are the king and the queen. When the queen is attacked, it must move which means it could no longer be acting as a defender.

Games referenced

Stream footage here: https://youtu.be/hzotV0aslmY?t=1075
Game played here: https://www.chess.com/live/game/5678484027 }
1. g3 { [%clk 0:05:00] } 1... e5 { Black's plan is to occupy the center in light of white's passive opening. } { [%csl Gd5][%cal Gd7d5] [%clk 0:04:58] } 2. Bg2 { [%clk 0:04:59] } 2... d5 { [%clk 0:04:57] } 3. e3 { [%clk 0:04:59] } 3... Nf6 { [%clk 0:04:51] } 4. d4 { Should black close the center of the board? } { [%clk 0:04:55] } 4... e4 { Both are okay, in this game black chooses to close. } { [%clk 0:04:25] } (4... Nc6 { Also a reasonable move that could open up the center. }) 5. c4 { White attacks the base of the pawn chain. } { [%clk 0:04:30] } 5... c6 { Black defends by intending to replace it with another pawn. } { [%csl Gd5][%cal Gc6d5] [%clk 0:04:10] } 6. Qc2? { "Aimless" move by white. } { [%clk 0:04:29] } 6... Bd6 { [%clk 0:04:04] } 7. h4 { [%clk 0:04:26] } 7... O-O { Black continues developing in the face of white's passive moves. } { [%clk 0:03:54] } 8. a3 { Where does the light squared bishop belong? } { [%csl Bc8] [%clk 0:04:24] } 8... Bg4 { White no longer has the option to play h3 so black's light squared bishop has free reign over the g4 square. } { [%csl Gh3][%cal Gh2h3] [%clk 0:03:33] } 9. b4 { White continues passive moves at the cost of not developing minor pieces. What should black do? } { [%clk 0:04:21] } 9... dxc4 { Open the center! This breaks the pawn chain and sacrifices a pawn, but black's development provides plenty of compensation. This also prevents white from playing c5. } { [%clk 0:03:01] } (9... Nbd7 { If black continues blindly developing, white can simply force a closed position. } 10. c5) 10. Bxe4? { White now also gives up their most active piece. } { [%clk 0:04:11] } (10. Qxc4 { The better line for white. } 10... a5 { However white will be unable to keep things closed after a5. }) 10... Nxe4 { [%clk 0:02:52] } 11. Qxe4 { Can black defend the light squared bishop with tempo? } { [%csl Rg4][%cal Re4g4] [%clk 0:04:07] } 11... f5 { Yes, while this does move a pawn in front of the king, white has no threats since their pieces are so underdeveloped. } { [%clk 0:02:31] } 12. Qe6+ { [%clk 0:04:01] } 12... Kh8 { [%clk 0:02:30] } 13. Qxc4 { Again, black is okay with giving up the pawn. Literally all of white's minor pieces are sitting on their starting squares! } { [%csl Bg1,Bc1,Bb1,Ba1,Bh1] [%clk 0:04:00] } 13... a5 { Opening up the position, now on the queenside. } { [%clk 0:02:08] } (13... Nd7 { Again, if black continues to blindly develop without exploiting the position, this simply gives white tempi to develop their own pieces and erode black's advantage. }) 14. b5 { [%clk 0:03:51] } 14... cxb5 { [%clk 0:02:06] } 15. Qxb5 { How can black occupy the c file? } { [%clk 0:03:49] } 15... Qc7 { Controlling the c file and creating a battery. } { [%csl Rc1,Rg3][%cal Rc7c1,Rd6g3] [%clk 0:01:50] } 16. Nd2 { [%clk 0:03:40] } 16... Bxg3 { Sacrificing the bishop to fully leverage black's developmental advantage. } { [%clk 0:01:34] } 17. fxg3 { [%clk 0:03:22] } 17... Qxg3+ { [%clk 0:01:33] } 18. Kf1 { [%clk 0:03:08] } 18... f4 { Further attempting to rip open the position. } { [%clk 0:01:26] } 19. Ndf3 { White attempts to bring some defenders to the king, giving back the material. } { [%csl Re3,Rf3][%cal Rf4e3,Rg4f3] [%clk 0:03:06] } (19. e4 { What if white tries to keep f file closed? } 19... f3 { Bringing the pawn into the attack. White's position is lost. }) 19... Bxf3 { [%clk 0:01:03] } 20. Nxf3 { [%clk 0:03:01] } 20... Qxf3+ { [%clk 0:01:02] } 21. Ke1 { Free rook. } { [%csl Rh1][%cal Rf3h1] [%clk 0:02:58] } 21... Qxh1+ { [%clk 0:00:51] } 22. Kd2 { [%clk 0:02:56] } 22... fxe3+ { [%clk 0:00:49] } 23. Kxe3 { [%clk 0:02:55] } 23... Nc6 { Black bringing the last piece into the game, Danya said this is not the best move but it is actually engine approved. Turns out the engine agrees that you should develop to convert winning positions! } { [%clk 0:00:24] } 24. Bb2 { [%clk 0:02:48] } 24... Rae8+ { [%clk 0:00:23] } 25. Kd3 { [%clk 0:02:44] } 25... Qf3+ { [%clk 0:00:22] } 26. Kc4 { Black has a nice tactical idea here to win the queen. } { [%clk 0:02:41] } 26... Qe2+ { First forcing the king onto the same rank as the queen. } { [%clk 0:00:12] } 27. Kc5 { [%clk 0:02:38] } 27... Rf5+ { [%clk 0:00:11] } 28. d5 { [%clk 0:02:36] } 28... Rxd5+ { And then distracting the only defender of the queen. } { [%clk 0:00:10] } 29. Kxd5 { [%clk 0:02:32] } 29... Qxb5+ { [%clk 0:00:10] } 30. Kd6 { [%clk 0:02:30] } 30... Qxb2 { [%clk 0:00:10] } 31. Rd1 { [%clk 0:02:25] } 31... Qe5+ { [%clk 0:00:09] } 32. Kd7 { [%clk 0:02:22] } 32... Qe7# { 0-1 Black wins by checkmate. } { [%clk 0:00:08] } 0-1


