{ THEMES and Things to Look For
- Four ways of defending against threats. 1) Moving the threatened piece out of the way. 2) Defend it with another piece. 3) Intercept one of the attackers by interfering with their control. 4) Responding with an EST - "equal or stronger threat". E.g. when your knight is threatened, you attack their queen.
- Don't blindly follow the move orders for your opening. Critical to contextualize them into what your opponent plays and adjust as appropriate.

Stream footage here: https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=J6MDnL_B83w
Game played here: https://www.chess.com/live/game/5795849182 }
1. d4 { [%clk 0:05:00] } 1... Nf6 { Start of the King's Indian Defense. } { [%clk 0:04:54] } 2. c4 { [%clk 0:04:57] } 2... g6 { Giving up control of the center to develop pieces into positions that attack the center from a distance. } { [%clk 0:04:51] } 3. Nc3 { [%clk 0:04:57] } 3... Bg7 { [%clk 0:04:50] } 4. e4 { [%clk 0:04:56] } 4... d6 { [%clk 0:04:41] } 5. Bg5 { [%clk 0:04:56] } (5. Nf3 O-O 6. Be2 e5 7. dxe5 dxe5 { This is the classical main line of the KID. This does not blunder the e5 pawn because... } 8. Nxe5 { ... if white takes... } 8... Nxe4 { Normally when a piece, white would seek to trade it for as much material as possible. } { [%csl Re5][%cal Rg7e5] } 9. Nxf7 { Seemingly winning a pawn. } 9... Bxc3+ { However, black has an intermediate move. } (9... Rxf7 { And white would be up a pawn if black recaptures. } 10. Nxe4) 10. bxc3 Qxd1+ 11. Bxd1 Rxf7 $15 { This equalizes material. Black's position is comfortably better with slightly more active pieces and black's king castled. }) 5... O-O { [%clk 0:04:34] } 6. Nf3 { How can white threaten the e4 pawn? } { [%csl Gd4][%cal Gf3d4] [%clk 0:04:55] } 6... Bg4 { Attacking the sole defender. Not a typical KID move it does illustrate a key theme. One thing to note is that comparing this to the main line, there is a bishop on g5 vs e2 which creates a potential pin that would materialize if black played e5. } { [%csl Rf3,Gd4,Bg5,Rd8][%cal Rg4f3,Gf3d4,Rf3d1,Rg5d8] [%clk 0:04:04] } (6... e5?? { e5 is not played because it makes the pin real and blunders into a tactical sequence for white. } { [%csl Rd8][%cal Rg5d8] } 7. dxe5 dxe5 8. Qxd8 Rxd8 9. Bxf6 Bxf6 10. Nd5 { White now forking the hanging bishop and threatening the trapped a8 rook. } { [%csl Rf6,Rc7,Ra8][%cal Rd5f6,Rd5c7,Rc7a8] }) 7. Bd3 { How does black continue to build pressure on the center? } { [%clk 0:04:52] } 7... Nc6 { [%csl Rd4][%cal Rc6d4] [%clk 0:03:39] } 8. O-O { White ignores the threat resulting in... } { [%clk 0:04:48] } (8. d5 { What white should have played. } 8... Ne5 { One possible line as black takes full advantage of the pin. } { [%csl Rf3,Rd1][%cal Re5f3,Rg4d1] } 9. Be2 Bxf3 { White forced to take with the g2 pawn because taking with the bishop hangs the c4 pawn. } { [%csl Rc4][%cal Re5c4,Ge2c4,Rg2f3] } 10. gxf3 { Achieving black's goal of ruining white's pawn structure. } (10. Bxf3 Nxc4 { Resulting in the loss of a pawn for white. })) 8... Nxd4 { ... losing the pawn due to the pin on the knight. } { [%csl Rd1][%cal Rg4d1] [%clk 0:03:32] } 9. Bxf6 { Temping to retake here immediately, but there is a better move. } { [%csl Rf6][%cal Rg7f6] [%clk 0:04:41] } 9... Bxf3 { [%clk 0:02:58] } (9... Nxf3+ { Knight takes does not work because the g4 bishop will be hanging. } 10. gxf3 Bxf3 11. Bxe7 Qxe7 12. Qxf3 { Resulting in loss of material for black. }) 10. gxf3 { [%clk 0:04:41] } 10... Bxf6 { Ruining the structure of white's kingside. } { [%csl Bh2,Bf2,Bf3] [%clk 0:02:52] } (10... c6 { Possible ideas of queenside pawn storm. } { [%csl Ga6,Gb5][%cal Gb7b5,Ga7a6] }) 11. Kh1 { [%clk 0:04:32] } 11... Qd7 { Black activating more pieces with ideas of attacking the kingside. } { [%clk 0:02:29] } 12. f4 { [%clk 0:04:30] } 12... Qh3 { [%clk 0:02:26] } 13. f5 { [%clk 0:04:27] } 13... Nf3 { Threatening mate in 1. } { [%csl Rh2][%cal Rh3h2] [%clk 0:02:14] } (13... Be5 { Why is this worse even though the idea seems similar to Nf3? } { [%csl Rh2][%cal Re5h2,Rh3h2] } 14. f4 { White can easily defend by intercepting / interfering with the bishop's control of h2. } { [%cal Re5f4] }) 14. Qxf3 { [%clk 0:04:01] } 14... Qxf3+ { Forcing loss of white's queen. } { [%clk 0:02:14] } 15. Kg1 { 0-1 Black wins. } { [%clk 0:04:00] } 0-1


