1. e4 { [%clk 0:05:00] } 1... c5 { Start of the Sicilian. } { [%clk 0:04:58] } 2. Nf3 { [%clk 0:04:59] } 2... Nc6 { [%clk 0:04:58] } 3. Bc4 { [%clk 0:04:57] } 3... e6 { Ideas of building pressure on the d5 square to challenge the bishop. } { [%csl Rd5][%cal Re6d5] [%clk 0:04:55] } 4. Nc3 { [%clk 0:04:55] } 4... Nf6 { [%cal Rf6d5,Re6d5] [%clk 0:04:53] } 5. O-O { [%clk 0:04:53] } 5... d5 { Executing the idea of attacking the bishop. } { [%clk 0:04:52] } 6. exd5 { [%clk 0:04:52] } 6... exd5 { [%clk 0:04:51] } 7. Re1+ { [%clk 0:04:46] } 7... Be7 { [%clk 0:04:50] } 8. Bb5 { [%clk 0:04:43] } 8... O-O { Black continues to develop. } { [%clk 0:04:48] } 9. d4 { Black is not worried about this threat as it would allow Bxc5. Black would then have a bishop pointed at white's king. } { [%csl Rc5][%cal Rd4c5,Re7c5] [%clk 0:04:39] } 9... Bg4 { Pinning the knight. } { [%csl Rd1][%cal Rg4d1] [%clk 0:04:23] } 10. h3 { [%clk 0:04:36] } 10... Bxf3 { [%clk 0:04:08] } (10... Bh5 { This is possible to maintain the pin, but we preferred to go after the hanging pawn. after Qxf3. } { [%csl Rd1][%cal Rh5d1] }) 11. Qxf3 { Removing the queen from defending the pawn. } { [%csl Rd4][%cal Rc6d4] [%clk 0:04:33] } 11... Nxd4 { [%clk 0:04:07] } 12. Qd3 { [%clk 0:04:27] } 12... a6 { [%clk 0:04:03] } 13. Ba4 { [%clk 0:04:22] } 13... b5 { Trying to trap white's light squared bishop. } { [%csl Ba4] [%clk 0:04:02] } 14. Bb3 { Danya miscalculated c4 here so instead of trapping the bishop and hanging the knight black instead... } { [%clk 0:04:20] } 14... Re8 { ... plays a simple developing move to strengthen black's position. } { [%clk 0:03:11] } (14... c4 { While this does trap the bishop, it also hangs the knight and at the end of this line, white has won a pawn back. } 15. Qxd4 cxb3 16. cxb3 { White winning the pawn back. }) 15. Be3 { [%clk 0:04:15] } 15... Nxb3 { [%clk 0:03:10] } 16. axb3 { [%clk 0:04:03] } 16... d4 { 0-1 Black wins. } { Pawn forking the minor pieces. } { [%csl Re3,Rc3][%cal Rd4e3,Rd4c3] [%clk 0:03:07] } 0-1


