{ THEMES and Things to Look For
- Bending the rule around moving the queen in the opening. This is fine if the queen an be better positioned on a safe square.
- It might be obvious, but playing chess requires focus. Blunders can appear at any moment, in this case white's queen is trapped.

Stream footage here: https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=OwrIaWwPJvM
Game played here: https://www.chess.com/analysis/game/live/5802933815 }
1. e4 { [%clk 0:04:59] } 1... e5 { [%clk 0:04:57] } 2. Nf3 { [%clk 0:04:54] } 2... d6 { [%clk 0:04:57] } 3. d4 { Start of the Philidor. In the Philidor, white has ideas of developing rapidly into the center. } { [%clk 0:04:53] } 3... exd4 { [%clk 0:04:56] } 4. Nxd4 { [%clk 0:04:49] } 4... Be7 { [%clk 0:04:55] } 5. Nc3 { [%clk 0:04:48] } 5... Bf6 { Weird move, but doesn't offer any concrete threats so white continues to develop. } { [%csl Gc4][%cal Gf1c4] [%clk 0:04:54] } 6. Bc4 { [%clk 0:04:24] } 6... Ne7 { [%clk 0:04:51] } 7. O-O { [%clk 0:04:23] } 7... O-O { [%clk 0:04:49] } 8. Be3 { [%clk 0:04:20] } 8... Nd7 { Ideas of a4, but a4 should usually be played to prevent black from playing b5, but this isn't being threatened so... } { [%csl Ga4][%cal Ga2a4] [%clk 0:04:48] } 9. f4 { ... white expands on the kingside instead. } { [%clk 0:03:53] } 9... a6 { Now black has ideas of playing b5 so we can prevent that by... } { [%csl Gb5][%cal Gb7b5] [%clk 0:04:45] } 10. a4 { ... playing a4. } { [%csl Rb5][%cal Ra4b5] [%clk 0:03:49] } 10... Ng6 { White's pieces are a more active than black's allowing white to start coordinating an attack. The most natural piece to develop here is the queen, where should we place it? } { [%csl Ge3,Gd4,Gc3,Gc4,Rg6,Rf6,Rd7] [%clk 0:04:43] } 11. Qh5 { The most active space for the queen. The queen can also not be attacked on this space. Perhaps violating a general opening principle to not expose the queen, but here the queen is safe and involved in the attack. } { [%clk 0:03:03] } (11. Qg4 { This walks into future discovered attacking ideas. } { [%csl Rg4][%cal Rc8g4] }) 11... Nb6 { [%clk 0:04:28] } 12. Ba2 { [%clk 0:02:57] } (12. Bb3) 12... c5 { [%clk 0:04:19] } 13. Nf3 { Potentially going to g5. } { [%csl Gg5][%cal Gf3g5] [%clk 0:02:34] } 13... c4 { Blocking the influence of white's bishop. Note that black's knight is the sole piece supporting the c4 pawn, how can black remove the defender? } { [%csl Gb6][%cal Ra2c4,Gb6c4] [%clk 0:04:17] } 14. a5 { Preparing taking the pawn. } { [%csl Rb6,Rc4][%cal Ra5b6,Ra2c4] [%clk 0:02:03] } 14... Nd7 { [%clk 0:03:57] } 15. Bxc4 { [%clk 0:02:02] } 15... Nc5 { Note that the isolated d6 pawn is the only piece protecting the knight. This is a weakness that can be targeted. } { [%csl Bd6][%cal Gd6c5] [%clk 0:03:56] } 16. e5 { [%csl Rd6][%cal Re5d6] [%clk 0:01:43] } 16... dxe5 { Removing the defender. } { [%csl Rc5][%cal Re3c5] [%clk 0:03:53] } 17. Bxc5?? { However... the time to take was not right as this now traps the queen after black plays... } { [%csl Bh5] [%clk 0:01:41] } (17. Rad1 { This would have been the more accurate move, bringing the rook into the attack with tempo. } { [%csl Rd8][%cal Rd1d8] }) 17... Nxf4 { [%clk 0:03:42] } 18. Qxe5 { Sacrificing the queen. } { [%clk 0:01:35] } 18... Bxe5 { [%clk 0:03:40] } 19. Nxe5 { [%clk 0:01:35] } 19... Ne6 { Hanging the rook. } { [%csl Rf8][%cal Rc5f8] [%clk 0:03:20] } 20. Bxf8 { [%clk 0:01:30] } 20... Qxf8 { [%clk 0:03:11] } 21. Ne4 { White has chances due to development advantage. } { [%csl Be5,Be4,Bc4,Bf1] [%clk 0:01:22] } 21... h6 { [%clk 0:03:01] } 22. Rxf7 { [%clk 0:01:09] } 22... Qb4 { [%clk 0:02:49] } 23. Raf1 { [%clk 0:00:56] } 23... Qxb2 { [%clk 0:02:43] } 24. Nd6 { [%clk 0:00:30] } 24... Qd4+! { [%clk 0:02:38] } (24... Qxe5 { If white takes the wrong knight, there are some tactics resulting in white winning back the queen. } 25. Rf8+ Kh7 26. Rh8+ Kxh8 27. Nf7+ Kg8 28. Nxe5) 25. Kh1 { [%clk 0:00:28] } 25... Qxd6 { [%clk 0:02:36] } 26. Ng6 { [%clk 0:00:28] } 26... Kh7 { [%clk 0:02:24] } 27. Bd3 { Creating possibilities for a discovered check. } { [%csl Rh7][%cal Rd3h7] [%clk 0:00:25] } 27... Kg8 { [%clk 0:02:18] } 28. Ne7+ { White is forced to take the knight or be mated. } { [%csl Re7][%cal Rd6e7] [%clk 0:00:24] } 28... Kh8 { Forced mate now possible with the bishop controlling the h7 square. } { [%csl Rh7][%cal Rd3h7] [%clk 0:02:14] } 29. Rf8+ { [%clk 0:00:14] } 29... Nxf8 { [%clk 0:02:04] } 30. Rxf8# { 1-0 White wins by checkmate. } { [%csl Rh7][%cal Rd3h7] [%clk 0:00:14] } 1-0


