{ THEMES and Things to Look For
- Development is important, but it is also okay to move a piece in the opening more than once if you can identify and exploit a deficiency in your opponents position.
- Allowing the possibility of opening up the board favors the player with the development lead.

Stream footage here: https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=U8zArIhxato
Game played here: https://www.chess.com/live/game/5821733499 }
1. f4 { [%clk 0:05:00] } 1... d5 { [%clk 0:04:59] } 2. Nf3 { [%clk 0:05:00] } 2... Bg4 { Similar to playing a Trompowsky, except as black. Idea is to develop the bishop outside of the pawn chain. } { [%csl Gg4,Ge6][%cal Gc8g4,Ge7e6] [%clk 0:04:54] } 3. e3 { Could develop the knight to either space. } { [%csl Gd7,Gc6][%cal Gb8d7,Gb8c6] [%clk 0:05:00] } 3... Nd7 { [%clk 0:04:45] } 4. h3 { [%clk 0:04:59] } 4... Bxf3 { [%clk 0:04:39] } 5. Qxf3 { Black wants to play e5, can black play it now? White is threatening the hanging pawn. } { [%csl Ge5,Rd5][%cal Ge7e5,Rf3d5] [%clk 0:04:58] } 5... e5 { Yes, this is okay because white has created a number of weaknesses on the kingside pawn structure by moving the f and h pawns. } { [%csl Gf4,Gh3][%cal Gf2f4,Gh2h3] [%clk 0:03:56] } 6. Bb5 { [%clk 0:04:55] } (6. Qxd5 { If white takes the pawn, black gets compensation by developing while castling queenside. } 6... Qh4+ 7. Kd1 O-O-O { [%csl Ba1,Bb1,Bc1,Bf1,Bh1] }) 6... c6 { Defending the knight while supporting the center with tempo. } { [%csl Gd5,Rb5][%cal Rc6b5,Gc6d5] [%clk 0:03:48] } 7. Ba4 { [%clk 0:04:49] } 7... Ngf6 { There is no need for black to push the e pawn, leaving open the possibility of opening up the center. This is one way in which black can take advantage of their development lead. } { [%cal Ge5e4,Re5f4] [%clk 0:03:47] } 8. fxe5 { [%clk 0:04:44] } 8... Nxe5 { [%clk 0:03:46] } 9. Qe2 { Note that white has played h3 which has left a weak square on the kingside. Development is important, but it is also okay to move a piece in the opening more than once if you can identify and exploit a deficiency in your opponents position. } { [%csl Bg3][%cal Gh2h3] [%clk 0:04:43] } 9... Ne4 { This allows black to stick a knight on it while also creating threats on white's king with the queen. } { [%csl Gg3,Gh4][%cal Ge4g3,Gd8h4,Rh4e1] [%clk 0:03:18] } 10. Qh5 { [%csl Re5,Re8][%cal Rh5e5,Re5e8] [%clk 0:04:02] } 10... Bd6 { Defending the e5 knight so we can play Ng3. } { [%csl Ge5][%cal Gd6e5,Ge4g3] [%clk 0:03:06] } 11. Rh2 { Black would like to position the queen on h4 so the queen can join the attack on the king. } { [%csl Gh4][%cal Gd8h4] [%clk 0:03:47] } 11... Qf6 { Forcing white's queen back due to threatened mate in 2 thereby allowing black to play Qh4. } { [%csl Ge2,Rf2,Gh4][%cal Gh5e2,Rf6f2,Gf6h4] [%clk 0:02:30] } (11... g6 { This also gives black strong attacking chances. } 12. Qh6 { White could choose to play Qh6 defending the h4 square, however... } { [%csl Rh4][%cal Rh6h4] } 12... Qf6 { Black can just attack in the same way as the line that was played and white has no way to effectively defend the f2 square. } { [%csl Rf2][%cal Rf6f2] } 13. g4 { Trying to defend it with the rook, but... } { [%csl Gf2][%cal Gh2f2] } (13. Qf4 { This looks like it defends f2, but loses the queen. } 13... Nd3+ 14. cxd3 Bxf4) 13... Nf3+ 14. Ke2 Nxh2 { ... black now wins the rook and yet again threatens mate. } { [%csl Rf1,Bc1,Bb1,Ba1][%cal Rf6f1] }) 12. Nc3 { [%clk 0:03:34] } 12... Qf2+ { [%clk 0:02:28] } 13. Kd1 { [%clk 0:03:28] } 13... Qf1# { 0-1 Black wins by checkmate. } { This is why these openings are dubious for beginner-intermediate players. They violate many of the opening rules and give principled players a lot of attacking opportunities that must be precisely defended.
Again, this mate is made possible by the underdevelopment of white's pieces allowing black to build an overwhelming attack into the kingside where the structure was already weakened by the choice in opening. } { [%csl Bc1,Ba1] [%clk 0:02:28] } 0-1


