1. e4 { [%clk 0:05:00] } 1... e5 { [%clk 0:04:59] } 2. Nf3 { [%clk 0:04:59] } 2... Nc6 { [%clk 0:04:58] } 3. Bc4 { [%clk 0:04:59] } 3... h6? { [%clk 0:04:57] } 4. c3 { Preparing d4 } { [%cal Gd2d4] [%clk 0:04:49] } 4... Nf6 { [%clk 0:04:56] } 5. d4 { [%clk 0:04:48] } 5... exd4 { How do we solve the problem of the hanging d4 pawn? } { [%csl Be4] [%clk 0:04:49] } 6. e5 { [%clk 0:04:33] } 6... Qe7 { White does not need to worry about the now hanging e5 pawn because black has positioned the queen in front of their queen, inviting a pin. } { [%csl Re5][%cal Be1e8,Rc6e5,Re7e5] [%clk 0:04:48] } 7. O-O { Preparing for the opening of the e file while tucking our knight away. } { [%cal Gf1e1,Re1e8] [%clk 0:04:02] } 7... Ng4 { [%clk 0:04:42] } 8. cxd4 { [%clk 0:04:01] } 8... g6 { Note that black's knight is trapped, so white can win it with... } { [%csl Gh2,Gf2,Ge3,Ge5,Gf6,Gh6] [%clk 0:04:35] } 9. h3 { [%clk 0:04:00] } 9... Nxf2 { Opening up the f file. } { [%cal Rf1f8] [%clk 0:04:22] } 10. Rxf2 { [%clk 0:03:59] } 10... Bg7 { [%clk 0:04:22] } 11. Nc3 { Working towards completing our developing with material advantage. } { [%clk 0:03:47] } 11... O-O { [%clk 0:04:01] } 12. Nd5 { Centralizing the knight with tempo. } { [%cal Rd5e7] [%clk 0:03:45] } 12... Qd8 { [%clk 0:03:59] } 13. Be3 { [%clk 0:03:36] } 13... Ne7 { White has the option of playing Nf6 now that black's queen no longer guards the square. } { [%csl Gf6][%cal Gd5f6] [%clk 0:03:55] } 14. Qd2 { However, white can opt for sticking to the usual plan of creating a battery and attacking black's fianchetto'd bishop. } { [%csl Rh6,Rg7][%cal Rd2h6,Rh6g7] [%clk 0:03:22] } 14... Kh7 { [%clk 0:03:50] } 15. Raf1 { Bringing in the final piece into the attack. } { [%clk 0:03:05] } 15... Nxd5 { [%clk 0:03:39] } 16. Bxd5 { [%clk 0:03:04] } 16... c6 { Noting here that the check given by Ng5 opens up the f file. So how do we open up the f file to make a check with a discovered attack possible? } { [%csl Gg5,Rf8][%cal Gf3g5,Rf1f8] [%clk 