{ THEMES and Things to Look For
- When one piece is the sole defender of two other pieces, look for overloading tactics.
- Modern opening theory will require violating opening principles.

Stream footage here: https://youtu.be/U8zArIhxato?t=622
Game played here: https://www.chess.com/live/game/5821782803 }
1. e4 { [%clk 0:05:00] } 1... c5 { [%clk 0:04:59] } 2. Nf3 { [%clk 0:04:58] } 2... Nc6 { [%clk 0:04:58] } 3. d4 { Priming the "open" Sicilian, by allowing the c and d files to be half open. } { [%clk 0:04:56] } 3... cxd4 { [%clk 0:04:57] } 4. Nxd4 { [%clk 0:04:56] } 4... e5 { Black chooses to play the Kalashnikov variation, weakening the d6 and f6. } { [%csl Bf6,Bd6] [%clk 0:04:57] } 5. Nb5 { [%clk 0:04:33] } 5... d6 { [%clk 0:04:56] } 6. N1c3 { [%clk 0:04:31] } 6... a6 { [%clk 0:04:54] } 7. Na3 { [%clk 0:04:30] } 7... b5 { Targeting the other weak square where white can outpost the knight. } { [%csl Gd5][%cal Gc3d5] [%clk 0:04:54] } 8. Nd5 { [%clk 0:04:30] } 8... Nf6 { Transposing into the Sveshnikov, all opening theory up to this point. We would like to keep this knight outpost on d5... } { [%csl Bd5,Rf6][%cal Rf6d5] [%clk 0:04:51] } 9. Bg5 { So we can pin black's knight with the goal of eliminating it. } { [%csl Rf6][%cal Rg5f6,Rf6d5] [%clk 0:04:17] } 9... Be7 { [%clk 0:04:50] } 10. Bxf6 { [%clk 0:04:16] } 10... Bxf6 { [%clk 0:04:49] } 11. c3 { With ideas of redeploying the knight. } { [%csl Gc2,Ge3][%cal Ga3c2,Gc2e3] [%clk 0:03:58] } (11. c4 { This move is also theory. It weakens the b4 and d4 squares, but gives an attacking tempo. } { [%csl Bd4,Bb4,Rb5][%cal Rc4b5] }) 11... O-O { [%clk 0:04:45] } 12. Nc2 { Still the main line of the Sveshnikov. } { [%clk 0:03:57] } 12... Be6 { Note the b5 pawn is weak, how can we undermine it? } { [%csl Bb5] [%clk 0:04:25] } 13. a4 { Offering opening up the a file which would allow white to deploy the rook. } { [%cal Ra1a6] [%clk 0:03:38] } 13... bxa4 { [%clk 0:03:48] } 14. Rxa4 { Now allowing the bishop to be deployed to c4 to support the knight. } { [%csl Gc4][%cal Gf1c4,Gc4d5] [%clk 0:03:36] } 14... Bg5 { [%clk 0:03:23] } 15. Bxa6 { Snagging the free pawn instead. } { [%clk 0:03:28] } 15... Bxd5 { [%clk 0:03:02] } 16. Qxd5 { [%clk 0:03:26] } 16... Ne7 { [%clk 0:02:46] } 17. Qd3 { [%clk 0:03:25] } 17... Qb6 { [%clk 0:02:37] } 18. Nb4 { A nice "glue" move, further supporting the bishop and preventing Qxb2. } { [%csl Gb4][%cal Gb4a6,Rb6b2] [%clk 0:03:05] } 18... Ng6 { The knight would like to go to f4, how do we stop it? } { [%csl Gh4][%cal Gg6h4] [%clk 0:02:14] } 19. g3 { [%csl Rh4][%cal Rg3h4] [%clk 0:02:47] } 19... Ne7 { [%clk 0:02:00] } 20. O-O { [%clk 0:02:45] } 20... f5 { A scary looking move, threatening to open up the f file, however this also opens up black's king for attack. } { [%clk 0:01:59] } 21. exf5 { [%clk 0:02:23] } 21... Nxf5? { The key observation is that the g5 bishop and a8 rook are defended by a single piece, the f8 rook. This opens up the possibility for overloading tactics... } { [%csl Gf8][%cal Gf8f5,Gf8a8] [%clk 0:01:47] } 22. Bc4+ { White exploiting the overloaded rook on f8 by given a check and discovered attack. } { [%csl Ra8,Rf5,Gf8][%cal Ra4a8,Rd3f5,Gf8a8,Gf8f5] [%clk 0:02:15] } 22... Kh8 { [%clk 0:01:45] } 23. Rxa8 { [%clk 0:02:14] } 23... Rxa8 { [%clk 0:01:26] } 24. Qxf5 { White now up a minor piece. } { [%clk 0:02:10] } 24... Bf6 { [%clk 0:01:25] } 25. Bd3 { Threatening checkmate. } { [%csl Rh7][%cal Rd3h7] [%clk 0:02:07] } 25... Kg8 { [%clk 0:01:13] } 26. Qxh7+ { [%clk 0:02:05] } 26... Kf7 { [%clk 0:01:08] } 27. Bg6+ { [%clk 0:01:54] } 27... Ke6 { We would like black's king to be on e7, is there a way white can force black's king onto that square? } { [%csl Rb6,Re7][%cal Ge6e7,Gb4d5,Rd5e7,Rd5b6] [%clk 0:01:08] } 28. Qh3+ { 1-0 White wins. } { Yes, the only square left for black's king is e7 now allowing white to win the queen. } { [%csl Rd5,Rf5,Rf7,Rd7][%cal Rh3c8,Rg6e8,Rb4d5] [%clk 0:01:33] } 28... Ke7 29. Nd5+ Kd8 30. Nxb6 1-0


