{ THEMES and Things to Look For
- Some opening principles can be violated, in this game trading a weakened kingside pawn structure for building a strong center.
- Trading one advantage for another, in this case a strong pawn center for a material advantage.

Stream footage here: https://youtu.be/U8zArIhxato?t=1247
Game played here: https://www.chess.com/live/game/5821831418 }
1. e4 { [%clk 0:05:00] } 1... c5 { [%clk 0:04:59] } 2. Nf3 { [%clk 0:05:00] } 2... Nc6 { [%clk 0:04:58] } 3. d4 { [%clk 0:04:59] } 3... cxd4 { [%clk 0:04:58] } 4. Nxd4 { [%clk 0:04:58] } 4... g6 { Starting the accelerated dragon. } { [%clk 0:04:58] } 5. Nxc6 { [%clk 0:04:56] } 5... bxc6 { [%clk 0:04:55] } 6. Qd4 { [%clk 0:04:56] } 6... Nf6 { [%clk 0:04:45] } 7. e5 { [%clk 0:04:54] } 7... Nd5 { [%clk 0:04:44] } 8. e6 { Looks scary because of the discovered attack on h8. } { [%csl Rh8][%cal Rd4h8] [%clk 0:04:51] } 8... f6 { Stopping the discovered attack. This may violate rules on moving kingside pawns, but it serves a specific purpose in this line to eventually prepare e5. } { [%csl Ge5][%cal Ge7e5] [%clk 0:04:28] } 9. exd7+ { [%clk 0:04:47] } 9... Bxd7 { [%clk 0:04:27] } 10. Be2 { Now black can play e5 with tempo. } { [%csl Ge5][%cal Ge7e5,Re5d4] [%clk 0:04:42] } 10... e5 { [%clk 0:04:12] } 11. Qc4 { [%clk 0:04:33] } 11... Bd6 { [%clk 0:04:06] } 12. O-O { [%clk 0:04:30] } 12... Qb6 { [%clk 0:04:05] } 13. Nc3 { [%clk 0:04:23] } 13... Be6 { Supporting the knight. } { [%csl Gd5][%cal Ge6d5,Gc6d5,Rc4d5,Rc3d5] [%clk 0:04:04] } 14. Nxd5 { Which piece to take with? } { [%csl Rd5][%cal Re6d5,Rc6d5] [%clk 0:04:18] } 14... cxd5 { Now black has a nice pawn center. } { [%csl Bd5,Be5] [%clk 0:03:52] } 15. Qh4 { [%clk 0:04:16] } 15... O-O { [%clk 0:03:47] } 16. Bh6 { [%clk 0:04:11] } 16... Rf7 { This looks scary for black, but at the moment white has no credible attack against black's king and black retains strong control over the center. } { [%csl Bd5,Be5][%cal Rh6f8,Rh4e7] [%clk 0:03:41] } 17. b3 { [%clk 0:04:07] } 17... Rc8 { Black can continue to develop, attacking the hanging pawn. } { [%csl Rc2][%cal Rc8c2] [%clk 0:03:23] } 18. c4 { Could take and open up the c file, or push the pawn down the d file. } { [%cal Rd5c4,Gd5d4] [%clk 0:04:05] } 18... d4 { Seizing more space in the center with ideas of taking even more space. } { [%cal Gf6f5,Gg6g5] [%clk 0:03:14] } 19. Rfd1 { We would like to play e4, but that is currently not possible due to the queen attacking the space. How do we prepare e4? } { [%csl Re4][%cal Ge5e4,Rh4e4] [%clk 0:03:56] } 19... f5 { Preparing e4. } { [%csl Ge4][%cal Gf5e4,Ge5e4] [%clk 0:02:59] } 20. Rac1 { [%clk 0:03:40] } 20... e4 { Preparing d3. } { [%cal Gd4d3] [%clk 0:02:58] } 21. Bf4 { [%clk 0:03:23] } 21... d3 { [%clk 0:02:51] } 22. Bxd6 { Two possibilities to capture. Black should calculate to figure out which one is better. } { [%csl Rd6,Re2][%cal Rd3e2,Rb6d6] [%clk 0:03:09] } 22... dxe2 { [%clk 0:02:16] } (22... Qxd6 { Could have also taken the other bishop and preserved the pawn chain, but it is better to win material by transforming the strong center into a concrete material advantage. } { [%csl Bd3,Be4,Bf5] }) 23. Re1?? { [%clk 0:03:05] } (23. Rd2 { Another line that demonstrates Danya's idea. } 23... e3 24. Rd4 { [%csl Gd4][%cal Gh4d4] } 24... exf2+ 25. Kxf2 { Engine says the position is even, but this position looks difficult to play for white with the exposed king. }) 23... Qxd6 { Now black is just up a bishop. } { [%clk 0:02:15] } 24. Rxe2 { [%clk 0:03:02] } 24... Rd7 { Building the gun. } { [%cal Rd7d1,Gc8d8] [%clk 0:02:11] } 25. Qh6 { [%clk 0:02:13] } 25... Rcd8 { [%clk 0:02:06] } 26. Qe3 { [%clk 0:02:08] } 26... Qd3 { Forcing the trade of queens. This is good because black has control of the open file and up material. } { [%clk 0:01:34] } (26... Qd1+ { This is not mate. } 27. Rxd1 Rxd1+ 28. Re1) 27. Qxd3 { [%clk 0:01:59] } 27... Rxd3 { [%clk 0:01:32] } 28. f3 { [%clk 0:01:56] } 28... e3 { [%clk 0:01:30] } 29. f4 { [%clk 0:01:52] } 29... Rd1+ { [%clk 0:01:30] } 30. Re1 { Forcing the rook trade. } { [%clk 0:01:48] } 30... Rxc1 { [%clk 0:01:29] } 31. Rxc1 { [%clk 0:01:47] } 31... g5 { Ideas of creating an isolated pawn on f4. } { [%csl Bf4] [%clk 0:01:25] } 32. g3 { [%clk 0:01:43] } 32... gxf4 { [%clk 0:01:24] } 33. gxf4 { [%clk 0:01:42] } 33... Rd4 { Attacking the now isolated pawn. } { [%csl Rf4][%cal Rd4f4] [%clk 0:01:22] } 34. Re1 { [%clk 0:01:37] } 34... Re4 { [%clk 0:01:22] } 35. Kg2 { [%clk 0:01:35] } 35... Bf7 { [%clk 0:01:20] } 36. Kf3 { [%clk 0:01:25] } 36... Bh5+ { [%clk 0:01:19] } 37. Kg3 { [%clk 0:01:24] } 37... e2 { [%clk 0:01:18] } 38. Kh4 { [%clk 0:01:16] } 38... Bg6 { [%clk 0:01:14] } 39. Kg5 { [%clk 0:01:14] } 39... Kg7 { [%clk 0:01:09] } 40. h3 { [%clk 0:01:00] } (40. h4 { This is mate in 1 } 40... h6#) 40... h6+ { [%clk 0:01:05] } 41. Kh4 { [%clk 0:00:59] } 41... Rxf4+ { Now black has connected passed pawns. } { [%csl Bf5,Be2] [%clk 0:00:57] } 42. Kg3 { [%clk 0:00:58] } 42... Re4 { [%clk 0:00:56] } 43. Kf2 { [%clk 0:00:54] } 43... Bh5 { [%clk 0:00:55] } 44. a4 { The bishop protects the pawn so now the rook is free to go after the pawns. } { [%csl Ge2][%cal Gh5e2] [%clk 0:00:47] } 44... Rd4 { [%clk 0:00:52] } 45. Ke3 { [%clk 0:00:42] } 45... Rd1 { [%clk 0:00:51] } 46. Rxe2 { [%clk 0:00:38] } 46... Bxe2 { [%clk 0:00:49] } 47. Kxe2 { [%clk 0:00:37] } 47... Rb1 { [%clk 0:00:49] } 48. Kd3 { [%clk 0:00:36] } 48... Rxb3+ { [%clk 0:00:49] } 49. Kd4 { [%clk 0:00:35] } 49... f4 { [%clk 0:00:48] } 50. c5 { [%clk 0:00:34] } 50... f3 { [%clk 0:00:48] } 51. c6 { [%clk 0:00:32] } 51... Rb1 { [%clk 0:00:44] } 52. Ke3 { [%clk 0:00:29] } 52... Rc1 { [%clk 0:00:44] } 53. Kxf3 { [%clk 0:00:27] } 53... Rxc6 { [%clk 0:00:43] } 54. Ke4 { [%clk 0:00:27] } 54... Ra6 { 0-1 Black wins. } { [%clk 0:00:43] } 0-1


