{ THEMES and Things to Look For
- Don't cash in too soon. Development is crucial, there is no need to immediately win material when you can develop and go for checkmate. Try to bring all of your pieces into the game!
- A good rule of thumb is to attack pawns that cannot be defended by any other pawns. Your opponent will have to "waste" pieces to defend it while you take the initiative.
- If there is a two move combination, a useful question can be "what if I played the second move first?" This can be a useful technique to discover more powerful lines.

Stream footage here: https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ii1vl5wLPWU
Game played here: https://www.chess.com/live/game/5830658526 }
1. e4 c5 2. Ne2 { 2...Ne2 is sort of weird since it obstructs the bishop, but it's theoretically okay. } 2... Nc6 3. c3 Nf6 4. d3 { Black has a slight development lead after white plays the somewhat passive 3.c3, how does black take advantage of this? } 4... d5 { Opening up the center by attacking white's e4 pawn. Also opening up development possibilities for the bishop. } { [%csl Re4,Gc8][%cal Rd5e4,Rf6e4,Gc8h3] } 5. f3 e5 { Continuing to expand in the center. } 6. Bg5 { Pinning the knight. } { [%csl Rf6][%cal Rg5d8] } 6... Be7 { Removing the pin. } { [%csl Gf6][%cal Ge7f6] } 7. Bxf6 { White's move is bad here because it delays development of their pieces. There is no need to trade at this time. } 7... Bxf6 8. Qc2 { Black is better here with superior center control. White has also weakened the kingside pawn structure giving black a diagonal to strike on should castling occur. } { [%csl Be5,Bd5,Bc5,Bf2][%cal Rb6g1] } 8... O-O (8... d4 { A move like d4 is no good because it results in a closed center which is detrimental to black who has the development lead and would like to open the center up for attacking opportunities against white's king. } 9. c4) 9. exd5 { White doing what black wants which is to open up the center with a pawn exchange. } 9... Qxd5 { If you don't believe Danya, just look at the computer eval! Even on material, but black is much better here due to lead in development and attacking opportunities against the un-castled white king. } 10. Nd2 { Now black wants to continue opening up the center by removing the d-pawn. There are two ways of accomplishing this, should we use the rook or the bishop? } { [%csl Rd3,Rf5,Rd8][%cal Gc8f5,Rf5d3,Rd8d3,Gf8d8] } 10... Rd8 { We use the rook. } (10... Bf5 { Why not Bf4? } 11. Ne4 { Ne4 shuts down the bishop and attacks black's bishop, nullifying the initial idea of attacking the d3 pawn. } { [%cal Re4f6] }) 11. Ng3 { White is now supporting the d3 pawn twice, how can we continue to apply pressure to it? Bf5 is no longer possible because white's knight covers it. } { [%csl Rf5,Gd3][%cal Rg3f5,Gf1d3,Gc2d3] } 11... b6 { This allows us to prepare Ba6 with the added benefit of supporting the c pawn. } { [%csl Ga6,Rd3][%cal Gc8a6,Ra6d3,Gb6c5] } 12. c4 { Why is c4 bad? It severely weakens the d4 square where our knight will be well placed. Additionally, note that white's d pawn cannot be defended by another pawn. Black can have ideas of targeting it down the road. } { [%csl Bd4,Gc6,Bd3][%cal Gc6d4] } 12... Nd4 { Which we can play immediately, threatening trading queens with a fork. } { [%csl Rc2][%cal Rd4c2] } 13. Qc3 Qc6 { The bishop has a nice x-ray against the queen, how can we prep an attack? } { [%csl Rc3][%cal Rf6a1] } 14. O-O-O e4 { Ideas of a discovered attack with check to win the queen. } { [%csl Rc3,Ge2][%cal Rf6a1,Gd4e2] } 15. Qa3 (15. Ndxe4?? { This opens up a fork and discovered attack allowing black to... } 15... Ne2+ 16. Bxe2 Bxc3 { ... win the queen. }) 15... e3 16. Nde4 Be5 17. Ne2 { How do we support this pawn? } { [%csl Be3] } 17... f5 { Preparing supporting the e pawn with tempo by attacking the knight. } { [%csl Re4,Gf4][%cal Rf5e4,Gf5f4,Gf4e3] } 18. Nxd4? { White plays into black's hands and allows us to support the pawn with an equal trade. } 18... cxd4 { This also has the consequence of opening up the c file where black now has easier access to attack white's king. } { [%cal Rc6c1] } (18... Bxd4 { We could take with the bishop here, but it blunts black's attacking chances by closing up the position. Here the queen is still blocked by two pawns. Eventually b5 could have been played to open things up again. } { [%csl Rc6][%cal Rc6c5,Gb6b5,Rb5c4] }) 19. Ng5 h6 (19... Bf4 { Bf4 is another idea to kick white's knight while also threatening a discovered attack and pawn fork. } { [%csl Rg5,Rd1,Rf1,Ge2][%cal Rf4g5,Rf4c1,Ge3e2,Re2f1,Re2d1] } 20. Nh3 e2+ 21. Nxf4 exd1=Q+ 22. Kxd1 { Black does win the exchange here trading a bishop and pawn for a rook, but black's attack is blunted. }) (19... e2 { Another idea from the Bf4 line is to play the pawn fork first. This results in the even better... } 20. Bxe2 Bf4+ 21. Kb1 Bxg5 { ... winning of the knight for a pawn while retaining the dark squared bishop for attacking ideas against the queenside. }) 20. Nh3 b5 { Chipping away at white's pawns to launch an attack against the king. } { [%cal Rc6c1,Rb5c4] } 21. c5 { How do we win this pawn and threaten the king? } { [%csl Bc5] } 21... Rd5 22. Qb3 { Pinning the rook, but this is not too concerning. } { [%cal Rb3g8] } 22... Be6 { Calmly developing our own pieces while breaking the pin and threatening a discovered attack of our own. } { [%cal Re6a2] } 23. Kb1 Rxc5 { Black has an overwhelming advantage here with all of their pieces in the game and in some way or another pointed at white's king. White's pieces are scattered and uncoordinated leaving them with a poor defense and no counterplay. } { [%csl Bh3,Bh1,Bf1,Bd1][%cal Re5b2,Re6b3,Rc5c1,Rc6c1,Ra8a5] } 24. Qa3 Rc2 25. Qe7 { White attempts some counterplay, but, again, it's unsupported due to white's undeveloped pieces and doomed to fail. } 25... Rc8 { A nice Alekhine's gun with three pieces controlling the file. Named after Alexander Alekhine, former Russian World Chess Champion. Alekhine apparently died under suspect circumstances, officially "choking on a piece of food". } { [%cal Rc8c1] } 26. Be2 Rxb2+ { Sacing the rook to open up the king for a checkmate. } 27. Kxb2 { [%cal Re6a2,Re5b2] } 27... Qc2+ 28. Ka1 Qxa2# { 0-1 Black wins by checkmate. } 0-1


