{ THEMES and Things to Look For
- Development is key. Danya finds a checkmate in this game because he made disciplined development moves that allowed white to control key squares.
- You don't always have to castle, if the king is safe in the center then use that time to play other developing moves.
- Pawn structure is not everything in the opening. If a pawn structure is "ruined", it is okay if there is other compensation such as supporting the center.

Stream footage here: https://youtu.be/ii1vl5wLPWU?t=1021
Game played here: https://www.chess.com/live/game/5830717350 }
1. e4 { [%clk 0:05:00] } 1... e5 { [%clk 0:05:00] } 2. Nf3 { [%clk 0:04:59] } 2... Nc6 { [%clk 0:04:59] } 3. Bc4 { Beginning of the Italian. } { [%clk 0:04:58] } 3... d6? { Not a great move because it obstructs the bishop from developing. } { [%cal Gf8d6] [%clk 0:04:57] } 4. c3 { Preparing d4 to occupy the center with pawns. Also opens up the queen to attack two pawns. } { [%csl Rb3][%cal Gd2d4,Gd1b3,Rb3b7,Rb3f7] [%clk 0:04:34] } 4... Bg4 { [%clk 0:04:55] } 5. d4 { [%clk 0:04:23] } 5... exd4 { [%clk 0:04:52] } 6. cxd4 { Pawn center achieved. We are not concerned about the pin, white's bishop is in a nice position pressuring the g7 pawn. } { [%csl Be4,Bd4,Rf7][%cal Rg4d1,Rc4f7] [%clk 0:04:22] } 6... Qf6 { White attacking the knight and d pawn. } { [%csl Rf3,Rd4][%cal Rf6d4,Rf6f3] [%clk 0:04:43] } 7. Be3 { Supporting the pawn while developing a piece. } { [%cal Ge3d4] [%clk 0:04:03] } 7... Bxf3? { Black wants to ruin the pawn structure, but this is not too concerning. } { [%clk 0:04:39] } 8. gxf3 { This allows an additional pawn to support the center. Additionally white has the lead in development, maintains the bishop pair, and has an uncontested bishop looking down towards black's kingside. } { [%csl Be3,Bc4][%cal Gf3e4,Rc4g8] [%clk 0:03:56] } 8... a6 { Another passive move from black, giving white time to develop. } { [%clk 0:04:35] } 9. Nc3 { [%clk 0:03:30] } 9... h6 { Yet another passive move. } { [%clk 0:04:27] } 10. Nd5 { [%clk 0:03:23] } 10... Qd8 { [%clk 0:04:19] } 11. Rc1 { Continuing development as black continues to play passive moves. } { [%clk 0:03:20] } 11... Nf6 { [%clk 0:04:09] } 12. Qb3 { The king here is okay in the center because it is safe. Black has no threats, and white still has the option to castle kingside if needed. } { [%csl Be1][%cal Rb3g8] [%clk 0:03:05] } 12... Nxd5 { [%clk 0:03:46] } (12... Na5 { Na5 is an attempt to disrupt white's attack, but fails after some nice tactics. } 13. Nxf6+ Qxf6 14. Qa4+ Nc6 15. d5 { [%cal Ra4e8,Rd5c6] } 15... b5 { Black can attempt to counter-fork, but after... } 16. Bxb5 { ... black loses the exchange anyway with the queen staring at an undefended rook. } { [%cal Ra4a8,Rb5e8,Rd5c6] } 16... axb5 17. Qxa8+) 13. Bxd5 { [%csl Rb7,Rf7][%cal Rb3b7,Rb3f7] [%clk 0:03:03] } 13... Na5?? { Now forced mate in two. } { [%clk 0:03:34] } 14. Bxf7+ { [%clk 0:02:51] } 14... Kd7 { [%clk 0:03:31] } 15. Qe6# { 1-0 White wins by checkmate. } { Note that the rook that was developed holds the key square which allows for checkmate. This is a result of disciplined development of pieces to good squares. } { [%csl Rc6][%cal Rc1c6] [%clk 0:02:50] } 1-0


