{ THEMES and Things to Look For
- When attacking on one side of the board, we prefer to keep other parts of the board closed so that all resources can be dedicated to that attack.

Stream footage here: https://youtu.be/ii1vl5wLPWU?t=1488
Game played here: https://www.chess.com/live/game/5830745215 }
1. e4 d6 2. d4 Nf6 3. Nc3 g6 { Start of the Pirc defense where d6 and g6 are played. This grants white control of the center. Key idea for white is to castle queenside to attack on the kingside. } { [%csl Bd4,Be4] } 4. Be3 (4. f4 { White also has the option of playing f4, which is called the Austrian attack to further cement control over the center. Danya plays this line in a later game. } { [%csl Bd4,Be4,Bf4] }) 4... Bg7 5. Qd2 { Preparing queenside castling and creating a battery to trade bishops. } { [%csl Rh6,Rg7][%cal Rd2h6,Rh6g7] } 5... O-O 6. Bh6 Nbd7 (6... Nc6 { Black should have immediately struck towards the center. This prevents the immediate h4 because... } 7. h4 Bxh6 8. Qxh6 Nxd4 { ... black wins the pawn here and opens up the center. }) (6... Ng4 { If black attempts to remove the bishop by other means, we try to preserve it as it is a key piece in the attack against the kingside. } { [%csl Rh6][%cal Rg4h6] } 7. Bg5 h6 8. Bh4 { Preserving the dark squared bishop for later use. } { [%csl Bh4] }) (6... Bxh6 { Trading the bishops here gives up control of the long diagonal and opens the black king up for attack. } 7. Qxh6) 7. h4 { Long term idea here for white is to start an attack against the kingside by opening up the h file. There is no need to trade bishops yet, instead using this move to start a pawn storm. } { [%cal Rg2g4,Rh4h5] } 7... Nh5 8. g4 Nhf6 { We could immediately play h5, but the g pawn is hanging so... } { [%csl Rg4][%cal Rh4h5,Rf6g4] } 9. f3 { ... we defend it first. } { [%cal Gf3g4] } 9... e5 { White now has the option of opening up or closing the center. Given that white is attacking kingside, which does white want? } { [%cal Rd4e5,Gd4d5] } 10. d5 { White wants to close the center so all resources can be directed towards the kingside attack. } (10. h5 { Allowing the center to be opened introduces complications. White cannot simply retake because... } 10... exd4 11. Qxd4?? { ... this hangs the bishop. } { [%csl Rh6][%cal Rg7h6] } 11... Bxh6) 10... Nc5 11. h5 g5?? { Hanging the pawn and allowing white to reinforce the attack. } { [%csl Rg5][%cal Rd2g5] } 12. Qxg5 Nxh5 13. Qxh5 Qf6 14. Bxg7 Qxg7 { At this point, white's attack has been blunted so now white should look to develop the rest of their pieces. } 15. O-O-O a5 16. Nge2 { Ideas of outposting the knight and attacking black's queen. } { [%csl Gf5][%cal Ge2g3,Gg3f5] } 16... a4 17. a3 { Shutting down black's attempt at counterplay. Unfortunately black's counter attack will be too slow. } 17... Bd7 18. Ng3 Rab8 19. Nf5 { Forcing black to either move the defending queen or allowing the g file to be opened up where the rook will go. } { [%csl Rg7,Gg1][%cal Rf5g7,Bg1g7,Gd1g1] } 19... Bxf5 20. gxf5 b5 21. Bxb5 Kh8 22. Rhg1? { Oops mouseslip! } { [%csl Gg1][%cal Gd1g1] } 22... Qf6 23. Rh1 { Attempting to correct the previous error to allow Rdg1 } { [%cal Gd1g1] } 23... h6 24. Qxh6+ Qxh6+ 25. Rxh6+ Kg7 26. Rdh1 Rh8 27. f6+ Kg8 28. Rxh8# { 1-0 White wins by checkmate. } 1-0


