{ Or as I like to call it, the Gucci Piano.

THEMES and Things to Look For
- Developing while having a material advantage
- Attacking your opponents more active pieces

Stream footage here: www.youtube.com/watch?v=FqVMAv3wKes
Game played here: www.chess.com/live/game/5830829526 }
1. e4 { [%c_effect e4;square;e4;type;Book] [%clk 0:04:59] } 1... e5 { [%clk 0:04:59] } 2. Nf3 { [%clk 0:04:58] } 2... Nc6 { [%clk 0:04:58] } 3. Bc4 { White plays the Italian } { [%clk 0:04:52] } 3... Bc5 { [%clk 0:04:57] } 4. c3 { [%clk 0:04:50] } 4... Nf6 { [%clk 0:04:56] } 5. d4 { [%clk 0:04:48] } 5... exd4 { [%clk 0:04:55] } 6. cxd4 { [%clk 0:04:44] } 6... Bb4+ { [%clk 0:04:55] } 7. Nc3 { [%clk 0:04:43] } 7... Nxe4 { [%clk 0:04:53] } 8. O-O { [%clk 0:04:41] } 8... Bxc3 { [%clk 0:04:51] } 9. d5 { Key mistake was going after the wrong knight, should have gone after the e4 knight with Re1. } { [%csl Be4] [%clk 0:04:39] } 9... Bf6 { [%clk 0:04:41] } 10. dxc6 { White threatening Re1 to pin and potentially win the knight, however black has the simple resource d5 to simultaneously defend the knight and attack the bishop. } { [%csl Ge1,Gd5][%cal Gf1e1,Re1e8,Gd7d5,Gd5e4,Rd5c4] [%clk 0:04:30] } 10... bxc6 { [%clk 0:04:20] } 11. Re1 { [%clk 0:04:06] } 11... d5 { Executing the aformentioned d5 defense } { [%clk 0:04:18] } 12. Bd3 { bf5 is an option to reinforce the knight, however, it is more important to unpin the knight even if it results in losing the pawn in the exchange. } { [%cal Gc8f5] [%clk 0:04:01] } 12... O-O { [%clk 0:03:53] } 13. Bf4 { [%clk 0:03:59] } 13... Bf5 { White opts to not take the knight, so we can reinforce it now. } { [%clk 0:03:48] } 14. Qc2 { [%clk 0:03:43] } 14... c5 { [%clk 0:03:36] } 15. Ne5 { Black should be looking for ways to develop and dislodge the knight. } { [%csl Be5] [%clk 0:03:29] } 15... Re8 { re8 is great as it develops the rook and attacks the knight } { [%csl Re5][%cal Re8e5] [%clk 0:03:19] } 16. f3 { Black opens up this diagonal with f3, this will be very relevant in a few moves. } { [%cal Ba7g1] [%clk 0:03:25] } 16... Bxe5 { Note that trading here is good for black as black is up two pawns } { [%clk 0:03:15] } 17. Bxe5 { [%clk 0:03:23] } 17... Rxe5 { [%clk 0:03:14] } 18. fxe4 { [%clk 0:03:22] } 18... dxe4 { [%clk 0:03:13] } 19. Bxe4 { Simple forking tactic enabled by white weakening the castled kingside pawn structure. } { [%csl Rd4][%cal Gd8d4,Rd4g1,Rd4e4] [%clk 0:03:21] } 19... Qd4+ { [%clk 0:02:59] } 20. Kh1 { [%clk 0:03:15] } 20... Rxe4 { [%clk 0:02:58] } 21. Rxe4 { [%clk 0:03:10] } 21... Bxe4 { [%clk 0:02:56] } 22. Qe2 { [%clk 0:03:07] } 22... Rd8 { Simple developing move while up material. } { [%clk 0:02:55] } 23. h3 { [%clk 0:03:05] } 23... g6 { [%clk 0:02:52] } 24. Re1 { [%clk 0:03:03] } 24... f5 { [%clk 0:02:51] } 25. Kh2 { [%clk 0:03:00] } 25... Qd2 { [%clk 0:02:49] } 26. a3 { Forcing the trade or winning material or checkmate. } { [%csl Rb2,Rg2,Re2][%cal Rd2b2,Rd2g2] [%clk 0:02:52] } 26... Qxe2 { [%clk 0:02:48] } 27. Rxe2 { [%clk 0:02:51] } 27... c4 { Preparing c3 to create a passed pawn. } { [%cal Gc4c3,Bc7c5] [%clk 0:02:45] } 28. g4 { [%clk 0:02:49] } 28... Rd3 { [%clk 0:02:42] } 29. gxf5 { [%clk 0:02:41] } 29... gxf5 { [%clk 0:02:41] } 30. Rf2 { [%clk 0:02:35] } 30... c3 { [%clk 0:02:40] } 31. bxc3 { [%clk 0:02:33] } 31... Rxc3 { [%clk 0:02:40] } 32. h4 { [%clk 0:02:28] } 32... Rc2 { White resigns. } { [%clk 0:02:27] } 0-1


