{ THEMES and Things to Look For
- Allow for flexibility in play. If there is a piece that could be placed on two squares, but it is unclear which square is better, perhaps moving another piece first would be better to allow for those two options after seeing what your opponent does first.
- When a piece is trapped, look for "sacrifices" which may allow even exchanges to get out of the trap.
- Trading is great when you are trading a less active piece for a more active one.

Stream footage here: https://youtu.be/FqVMAv3wKes?t=823
Game played here: https://www.chess.com/live/game/5830878277 }
1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bb5 a6 4. Ba4 Nf6 { Continuing the main line of the Ruy Lopez } 5. O-O Be7 6. Re1 { White threatening to remove the knight defender on c6 and take the e pawn. } { [%cal Ra4c6,Rf3e5] } 6... b5 7. Bb3 d6 { Continuing down a common line in the Ruy Lopez } 8. c3 O-O 9. d3 (9. h3 { This is the main move as confirmed by Lichess opening explorer. }) 9... Bg4 { Bg4 is played, but Danya notes that this is not the most accurate move. The best place for a piece is not necessarily where it is pinning another piece. } (9... Be6 { This is perhaps the better move to contest the diagonal that white's bishop currently controls. }) 10. Nbd2 Na5 11. Bc2 c5 12. Nf1 Nc6 13. Ne3 { This is why Bb4 is not as good of a move as it seems, this allows white to reposition the knight from the queenside to the kingside with tempo. } 13... Be6 14. d4 { White threatening d5 fork. } { [%csl Re6,Rc6][%cal Gd4d5,Rd5e6,Rd5c6] } 14... cxd4 15. cxd4 { Fork threatened again! } { [%csl Re6,Rc6][%cal Gd4d5,Rd5e6,Rd5c6] } 15... exd4 16. Nxd4 Nxd4 17. Qxd4 Nd7 18. Nd5 Bxd5 { Removing one of white's most active (and annoying) pieces. } 19. exd5 Bf6 { Getting the dark squared bishop onto a nice diagonal with tempo attacking white's queen. } { [%cal Rf6a1] } 20. Qd3 g6 21. Rb1 { Here black would like to develop the knight to a square but which one? Unclear which is better so... } { [%csl Ge5,Gc5][%cal Gd7c5,Gd7e5] } 21... Rc8 { ... instead of choosing which square to choose to put the knight on just yet, we develop the rook first and wait to see what white does first. This allows some flexibility in black's play.
Black also has a nice fork available with the Qa5. } { [%csl Ra2,Re1,Gd8][%cal Gd8a5,Ra5a2,Ra5e1] } 22. Bd1 Qa5 23. b4 Qxa2 24. Bg4 (24. Bb3 { Black's queen is trapped here, but black has a way to get out of this.
When your hanging queen is trapped, "sacrifices" are potential ways to get out of them. There are two possible ways to attack white's queen. } { [%cal Gd7e5,Gc8c3] } 24... Ne5 { This fails because... } (24... Rc3 { This "sacrifice" succeeds because it allows for either trading queens or rooks. } { [%cal Gf6c3,Ra2b1] }) 25. Rxe5 { ... white can simply take and black's queen is still trapped. } 25... Qxb1 26. Qxb1 Bxe5 { White ends up being better here after trading two rooks for the queen and a knight. }) 24... Ne5 25. Rxe5 Bxe5 26. Bxc8 Rxc8 27. Be3 Rc3 28. Qd1 Qc4 29. Rc1 { There is no need to trade here, black can simply... } 29... Qxb4 { ... take the pawn first. } 30. Bd2 Rxc1 { Now we are okay with trading since black is ahead in material. } 31. Qxc1 Qc4 0-1


