[Annotator "https://lichess.org/@/rudisco"]

{ THEMES and Things to Look For
- Times where it is appropriate to sacrifice are to open up an attack on the king. This should be done carefully and with calculation!
- Whenever your opponent makes a move near their king, it can be useful to note what weaknesses may have been introduced and look for winning lines.
- Beginners should first learn key opening concepts before trying openings that break them such as the Scandinavian.
- Don't lose your cool when your opponent delivers a move that you did not see. Back peddling into an even position is fine, don't try to force it.

Stream footage here: https://youtu.be/FqVMAv3wKes?t=1526
Game played here: https://www.chess.com/live/game/5830938327 }
1. e4 { [%clk 0:05:00] } 1... d5 { Scandinavian was first played in 1475. The first ever recorded game of chess played with the current rules featured the Scandinavian. After that, the second recorded game was in 1837! } { [%clk 0:04:59] } 2. exd5 { [%clk 0:04:57] } 2... Qxd5 { Danya does not recommend the Scandinavian for beginner players because the main drawback of the Scandi is black only has developed the queen after moving it multiple times in the opening. It requires an understanding of when to break opening principles which beginners do not possess. Basics first, then add the twists. } { [%clk 0:04:58] } 3. Nc3 { [%clk 0:04:51] } 3... Qa5 { [%clk 0:04:55] } 4. d4 { [%clk 0:04:37] } 4... Bf5 { [%clk 0:04:54] } 5. Nf3 { [%clk 0:04:35] } 5... Bg4 { Black again loses development tempo by moving the bishop twice in the opening. } { [%clk 0:04:52] } 6. Be2 { [%clk 0:04:18] } 6... Nf6 { [%clk 0:04:40] } 7. O-O { [%clk 0:04:13] } 7... e6 { [%clk 0:04:39] } 8. Ne5 { Centralizing the knight. } { [%clk 0:03:56] } 8... Bxe2 { [%clk 0:04:35] } 9. Qxe2 { [%clk 0:03:56] } 9... Be7 { [%clk 0:04:32] } 10. Bf4 { A nice move to support the knight and skewer the c7 pawn. } { [%cal Rf4c7] [%clk 0:03:43] } 10... O-O { [%clk 0:04:23] } 11. a4 { Grabbing space on the queenside and solidifying control over the b5 square, possibly as a space for the queen. } { [%cal Ge2b5] [%clk 0:03:34] } 11... Qb6 { [%clk 0:04:18] } 12. Nb5 { [%clk 0:03:04] } 12... c6 { Critical move to find here with the aim of getting the knight to c7 to attack the trapped rook. } { [%csl Gc7,Ra8][%cal Gb5c7,Rc7a8] [%clk 0:04:10] } 13. Nc4! { While apparently hanging the b5 knight, simultaneously gives white control over the critical c7 square while also delivering an attack on black's queen. } { [%csl Rb8,Rc7,Rb6][%cal Rf4b8,Rc4b6,Gb5c7] [%clk 0:02:39] } 13... Qd8 { [%clk 0:04:00] } 14. Nc7 { Executing the plan. } { [%clk 0:02:38] } 14... Nd5! { Strong move by black, attacking the bishop while also threatening to trap the knight after Nxa8. } { [%csl Rf4,Bc7][%cal Rd5f4] [%clk 0:03:44] } 15. Nxd5 { This forces a retreat from white. } { [%clk 0:02:03] } (15. Nxa8 { If white goes for the rook instead, black can force a line that wins the exchange... } 15... Nxf4 16. Qe5 Qxd4 17. Qxd4 Ne2+ 18. Kh1 Nxd4 19. Nc7 Bd8 { Black will soon be up a knight after trapping it. }) 15... cxd5 { [%clk 0:03:40] } 16. Ne5 { Centralizing the knight. } { [%clk 0:02:02] } 16... Nc6 { [%clk 0:03:28] } 17. c3 { [%clk 0:02:00] } 17... Rc8 { [%clk 0:03:17] } 18. Rfe1 { [%clk 0:01:58] } 18... Nxe5 { [%clk 0:03:12] } 19. Bxe5 { [%clk 0:01:58] } 19... Bd6 { [%clk 0:03:11] } 20. h3 { [%clk 0:01:57] } 20... Qc7 { [%clk 0:03:05] } 21. Qg4 { Threatening mate in 1. } { [%csl Rg7][%cal Rg4g7] [%clk 0:01:53] } 21... Bxe5 { Could take with either rook or pawn, which one? } { [%cal Rd4e5,Re1e5] [%clk 0:03:02] } 22. Rxe5 { The rook to preserve the pawn structure and prepare an attack. } { [%clk 0:01:43] } 22... Qb6 { [%clk 0:02:58] } 23. Qe2 { [%clk 0:01:39] } 23... Rc6 { [%clk 0:02:52] } 24. h4 { [%clk 0:01:39] } 24... f6 { [%clk 0:02:50] } 25. Re3 { [%clk 0:01:37] } 25... a5 { [%clk 0:02:48] } 26. Re1 { [%clk 0:01:36] } 26... Re8 { [%clk 0:02:42] } 27. h5 { [%clk 0:01:36] } 27... Kf7 { Sacrificing this pawn is fine to open up the position on black's king. } { [%cal Gh5h6] [%clk 0:02:38] } 28. h6 { [%clk 0:01:35] } 28... g6 { [%clk 0:02:35] } 29. Rf3 { Skewering the King. } { [%cal Rf3f7] [%clk 0:01:34] } 29... f5 { Severely weakening the e5 square. } { [%csl Be5] [%clk 0:02:31] } 30. Qe5 { Threatening Qg7+ } { [%csl Rg7][%cal Re5g7] [%clk 0:01:32] } 30... Rg8 { [%clk 0:02:29] } 31. Re2 { [%clk 0:01:29] } 31... g5?? { Blunders into the forced mate which Danya now delivers. } { [%clk 0:02:05] } 32. Rxf5+! { [%clk 0:01:16] } 32... exf5 { [%clk 0:02:03] } 33. Qxf5+ { [%clk 0:01:15] } 33... Rf6 { [%clk 0:02:00] } 34. Qxh7+ { [%clk 0:01:08] } 34... Kf8 { [%clk 0:01:39] } 35. Qe7# { 1-0 White wins by checkmate. } { [