{ THEMES and Things to Look For
- Converting material advantages by developing pieces and not going out of one's way to make trades
- Developing plans to create and then promote passed pawns
- Importance of calculating to avoid blundering apparent material gains into stronger tactics

Stream footage here: www.youtube.com/watch?v=qW-mT-FbLnA
Game played here: www.chess.com/live/game/5831083915 }
1. e4 { [%clk 0:05:00] } 1... c5 { [%clk 0:04:58] } 2. c3 { Alapin Sicilian, named after Semyon Alapin, a Lithuanian chess master from the early 20th century. The first person to play the Alapin was William Poupart (sp?). Semyon was the 4th person to play it in recorded chess games.
The purpose of the Alapin is to control the center by preparing d4. Black would like to prevent this. } { [%cal Gd2d4,Gc2c3] [%clk 0:04:58] } 2... d5 { d5 doesn't directly prevent d4, but allows black to contest the center } { [%clk 0:04:22] } (2... e5 { e5 is another option, but weakens black's control of the d5 square } { [%csl Gd5] }) 3. e5 { Again, white attempts to prepare d4 while tightening their grip on the center } { [%cal Gd2d4] [%clk 0:04:54] } (3. exd5 Qxd5 { There is another line here which can be compared to the Scandinavian, however some main positional differences is that white's c3 pawn now blocks the knight from developing and attacking the queen. } { [%csl Bc3][%cal Gb1c3] } 4. d4 Nf6 5. Nf3 Nc6 { An interesting opening where it is unclear who is better. }) 3... d4 { Played to prevent white from executing their plan of placing a pawn on d4 } { [%clk 0:04:18] } 4. cxd4 { [%clk 0:04:51] } 4... cxd4 { [%clk 0:04:17] } 5. Nf3 { [%clk 0:04:51] } 5... Nc6 { [%clk 0:04:12] } 6. Bb5 { [%csl Rb5][%cal Rb5e8] [%clk 0:04:48] } 6... Bd7 { [%clk 0:03:56] } 7. d3 { Typical mistake by white as it weakens the d2 square guarding the queen and enables black to pick up a free bishop. } { [%csl Gd2,Ra5][%cal Ra5e1,Ra5b5,Gd2d3,Gd8a5] [%clk 0:04:47] } (7. O-O { Note that the natural move of castling appears to lose materials for white. The pin on the c6 diagonal works both ways and allows black to make a favorable exchange for a pawn. } { [%csl Re5][%cal Rd7b5,Rb5d7,Rc6e5] } 7... Nxe5 (7... e6 { The better move for black is to simply continue development. }) 8. Nxe5 Bxb5 { However... after the exchange white has... } 9. Qb3! { ... forking the bishop by threatening checkmate. This demonstrating the value of calculating a little bit further to identify if "free" material is truly free. } { [%csl Rf7,Rb5][%cal Rb3b5,Rb3f7] }) 7... Qa5+ { [%clk 0:03:25] } 8. Bd2 { [%clk 0:04:28] } 8... Qxb5 { Three pawns are also being attacked. } { [%csl Re5,Rd3,Rb2][%cal Rc6e5,Rb5e5,Rb5d3,Rb5b2] [%clk 0:03:24] } 9. O-O { [%clk 0:04:27] } 9... Qxd3 { Taking a pawn and more control of the center. } { [%clk 0:03:23] } (9... Qxb2 { This is actually a better move than what was played. Usually it's hard to win material here because of bc3, but the pawn on d4 guards the critical square which means the only way to save the rook for white is... } { [%csl Ra1,Bd4][%cal Rb2a1,Gd2c3] } 10. Na3 { ... to give up the knight. } 10... Qxa3) 10. Na3 { [%clk 0:04:16] } 10... e6 { When up material, do not forget to develop! e6 opens up the diagonal for the dark squared bishop. } { [%cal Gf8b4] [%clk 0:03:02] } 11. Qe1 { [%clk 0:04:13] } 11... Bxa3 { In general trading is good when up, but not the only way to win. Players get in trouble when going out of the way to trade and accidentally weakening their position. } { [%clk 0:02:56] } 12. bxa3 { [%clk 0:04:07] } 12... Nge7 { [%clk 0:02:55] } 13. Rb1 { [%clk 0:04:00] } 13... b6 { [%clk 0:02:53] } 14. Bb4 { Fine to trade if it falls into your lap. } { [%cal Rc6b4] [%clk 0:03:53] } 14... Nxb4 { [%clk 0:02:51] } 15. Qxb4 { [%clk 0:03:50] } 15... Qc3 { Theoretically forcing the queen trade because white has no way to defend the a3 pawn AND get the queen to safety. } { [%csl Ba3] [%clk 0:02:38] } 16. Qd6 { There is an opportunity to trap the queen. Qc5 is the obvious idea, but there is a better move... } { [%cal Gc3c5] [%clk 0:03:42] } 16... Nf5 { ... and this forces white to exchange the queen while also allowing black to develop a piece into the center. } { [%cal Rf5d6] [%clk 0:02:24] } 17. Qb4 { [%clk 0:03:25] } 17... Qxb4 { [%clk 0:02:23] } 18. Rxb4 { [%clk 0:03:22] } 18... Bc6 { [%clk 0:02:18] } 19. Nxd4 { [%clk 0:03:20] } 19... Nxd4 { Again, trading when convenient without weakening black's position. } { [%clk 0:02:17] } 20. Rxd4 { [%clk 0:03:18] } 20... Ke7 { Connecting the rooks on row 8. Castling would accomplish the same objective, but it is better to have a more active, centralized king in the endgame especially after queens are exchanged. } { [%cal Gh8a8,Ga8h8] [%clk 0:02:00] } 21. Rc1 { [%clk 0:03:15] } 21... Rac8 { [%clk 0:01:59] } 22. Rdc4 { [%clk 0:03:11] } 22... Bd7 { Defending the rook and forcing the favorable rook trade for black. } { [%csl Gc8][%cal Gd7c8,Gh8c8,Rc8c1] [%clk 0:01:51] } (22... Bb7 { This is inaccurate because of ... } 23. Rc7+ { ... a seemingly nasty check if white takes the bishop ... } 23... Kd8 { Black must play accurately here and defend the rook, otherwise black will give up a rook and will be in danger of being mated. However, this position is still winning for black as white has no way to avoid the rook trade. }) 23. Rxc8 { [%clk 0:03:05] } 23... Rxc8 { [%clk 0:01:46] } 24. Rd1 { [%clk 0:03:04] } 24... Rc2 { Infiltrating the 2nd rank and attacking the hanging pawn with the long term plan of creating passed b and a pawns } { [%csl Bb6,Ba7,Ra2,Ra3][%cal Bb6b4,Ba7a4,Rc2a2,Ra2a3] [%clk 0:01:44] } 25. h4 { [%clk 0:02:50] } 25... Rxa2 { [%clk 0:01:43] } 26. Rd3 { [%clk 0:02:48] } 26... b5 { [%clk 0:01:43] } 27. g4 { [%clk 0:02:45] } 27... a5 { [%clk 0:01:41] } 28. g5 { [%clk 0:02:42] } 28... b4 { [%clk 0:01:41] } 29. axb4 { [%clk 0:02:38] } 29... axb4 { [%clk 0:01:41] } 30. Rb3 { [%clk 0:02:37] } 30... Ra4 { Defending the passed pawn with the goal of dislodging the rook using the light squared bishop. } { [%csl Gb4,Bd7][%cal Ga4b4,Bd7c6,Bc6d5,Rd5b3] [%clk 0:01:40] } 31. f4 { [%clk 0:02:34] } 31... Bc6 { [%clk 0:01:30] } 32. Kf2 { [%clk 0:02:30] } 32... Bd5 { Executing the plan and dislodging the blockading rook. } { [%csl Rb3][%cal Rd5b3] [%clk 0:01:30] } 33. Rb2 { [%clk 0:02:28] } 33... b3 { [%clk 0:01:29] } 34. Kg3 { [%clk 0:02:21] } 34... Ra2 { Same idea here as we push the passed pawn to convert a material advantage into a win. } { [%cal Bd5e4,Re4b1] [%clk 0:01:28] } 35. Rb1 { [%clk 0:02:08] } 35... b2 { [%clk 0:01:27] } 36. Kf2 { [%clk 0:01:52] } 36... Be4 { [%clk 0:01:25] } 37. Rxb2 { [%clk 0:01:43] } 37... Rxb2+ { [%clk 0:01:25] } 38. Ke3 { [%clk 0:01:42] } 38... Bf5 { [%clk 0:01:24] } 39. h5 { [%clk 0:01:37] } 39... Rh2 { [%clk 0:01:23] } 40. g6 { [%clk 0:01:35] } 40... fxg6 { [%clk 0:01:21] } 41. hxg6 { [%clk 0:01:34] } 41... Bxg6 { Same idea here, creating passed pawns to promote. There are also ideas of moving the king around to play for checkmate immediately, but it's easier to just push pawns. } { [%csl Bh7,Bg7][%cal Bg7g5,Bh7h5] [%clk 0:01:21] } 42. Kf3 { [%clk 0:01:32] } 42... Bf5 { [%clk 0:01:20] } 43. Kg3 { [%clk 0:01:30] } 43... Rh3+ { [%clk 0:01:19] } 44. Kg2 { [%clk 0:01:29] } 44... h5 { 0-1 Black wins. } { Danya emphasizes that there is, clearly, not only one way to convert a winning position. He is playing sound, logical moves that anyone with some study can find. } { [%clk 0:01:19] } 0-1


