{ THEMES and Things to Look For
- Pawn structure is not as critical during the middle game as long as the structure doesn't result in the King being attacked. Pawn structure becomes important in the endgame where the structures can determine who is winning.
- Clarifying tensions should be done carefully as this could lead to weakening one's position even if the resultant trades are equal.
- Be careful not to allow your pieces to be trapped on one side of the board. This is open equivalent to giving your opponent advantage in material. See black's dark squared bishop.

Stream footage here: www.youtube.com/watch?v=qW-mT-FbLnA
Game played here: www.chess.com/live/game/5845034015 }
1. e4 { [%clk 0:05:00] } 1... Nc6 { The start of the Nimzowitsch Defense, d4 and Nf3 are good replies. } { [%cal Gd2d4,Gg1f3] [%clk 0:04:58] } 2. Nf3 { [%clk 0:04:54] } 2... d5 { Transitioning into a Scandinavian like position } { [%clk 0:04:57] } 3. exd5 { [%clk 0:04:50] } 3... Qxd5 { [%clk 0:04:56] } 4. Nc3 { Developing with tempo attacking the queen } { [%cal Rc3d5] [%clk 0:04:37] } 4... Qh5 { [%clk 0:04:54] } 5. d4 { Standard Scandinavian move, taking control of the center. } { [%clk 0:04:32] } 5... Bg4 { Idea for black here is to pile on pressure on the d4 pawn both by directly attacking it and indirectly by attacking the pieces defending it. Ideas of castling long will add another piece attacking along the half open d file. This line requires precise play from white. } { [%csl Rh5,Rg4,Rd8,Rc6][%cal Rh5d1,Rc6d4,Ga8d8,Ge8c8] [%clk 0:04:51] } 6. Bb5 { For white, the theory here is to immediately attack the c6 knight even though Bxf3... g3 ruining the pawn structure is threatened. The other moves will result in losing the d pawn. } { [%cal Rg4f3,Rg2f3] [%clk 0:04:00] } (6. d5 { Tempting move since it looks active attacks the knight, but allows... } 6... O-O-O { ... which now pins the pawn to the queen. } { [%cal Rd8d1] } 7. Be2 e6 { The pawn is simply lost for no compensation. }) (6. Be2 { Runs into similar problems as d5, black castles and continues to pile pressure on d4 } 6... O-O-O 7. Be3 e5 { The pawn is lost no matter what white does. } { [%cal Re5d4,Rh5d1,Rd8d4,Rc6d4] }) 6... e6 { [%clk 0:04:34] } 7. Qe2 { Idea here is to preemptively unpin the pawn. } { [%clk 0:03:52] } 7... Bb4 { Black continues to apply pressure by pinning the knight } { [%cal Rb4e1] [%clk 0:04:24] } (7... Bxf3 8. gxf3 { [%csl Bf3,Bf2] } 8... O-O-O 9. Bxc6 bxc6 { Here the pawn structure for black is disastrous because the king has already castled queenside. However, for white they have the option to... } { [%csl Bf3,Bf2,Bc6,Bc7] } 10. Bd2 { ... opt for castling queenside where the pawn structure is intact. Black is attacking the pawn, but their king is exposed and open to mate. } { [%csl Ra6][%cal Rh5f3,Ge1c1,Ge2a6,Ra6c8] }) 8. Bd2 { Which white wants to immediately unpin with Bd2 } { [%clk 0:03:37] } (8. Bxc6+ { White should have probably taken the knight sooner to prevent black from defending with Ne7 }) 8... Ne7 { [%clk 0:04:14] } 9. a3 { Chase the bishop away } { [%cal Ra3b4] [%clk 0:03:22] } 9... Ba5 { [%clk 0:04:06] } 10. b4 { Continuing the chase } { [%clk 0:03:20] } 10... Bb6 { [%clk 0:04:05] } 11. Be3 { Supporting the d4 pawn, pawn is not hanging because black's knight is pinned. } { [%cal Rb6d4,Re3d4,Rb5e8] [%clk 0:03:16] } 11... O-O { [%clk 0:03:50] } 12. Rd1 { [%clk 0:03:14] } 12... Nf5? { This is inaccurate by black because it allows... } { [%cal Ge7f5] [%clk 0:03:45] } 13. Bxc6 { [%clk 0:02:58] } 13... bxc6 { ... this exchange which traps black's bishop and ruins the pawn structure. Allowing the bishop to be trapped here is a CRITICAL error.
d4 holds for now, but we would like to defend it with the c pawn which requires... } { [%csl Bc6,Bd4][%cal Gc2c3] [%clk 0:03:44] } 14. Ne4 { ... moving the knight out of the way, preferably to an active center square. } { [%clk 0:02:31] } 14... Nxe3? { Common mistake at the 1600 level because the knight on f5 was controlling the center. Common error at this level due to wanting to clarify tension at the cost of weakening their position. This allows... } { [%clk 0:03:26] } (14... Nh4 { This is a winning line for black and demonstrates the cost of simplifying positions for the sake of clarity. } { [%cal Rh5f3,Rh4f3] } 15. Ng3 Nxg2+ 16. Kf1 Qh3) 15. fxe3 { fxe3 which allows white to support d4 with tempo } { [%cal Re3d4] [%clk 0:02:17] } 15... f5 { Black attempts to attack, but weakens the e5 square which would make a nice outpost for white. White is also able to get away while landing an attack on the queen. } { [%csl Be5][%cal Gf7f5] [%clk 0:03:10] } 16. Ng3 { [%clk 0:02:15] } 16... Qh6 { [%clk 0:02:59] } 17. O-O { [%clk 0:02:11] } 17... f4 { [%clk 0:02:53] } 18. exf4 { [%clk 0:02:10] } 18... Rxf4 { [%clk 0:02:50] } 19. c3 { [%clk 0:02:10] } 19... Re8 { Danya likes white's position here because black's bishop is suck in the corner and hard to get into the game. Black would like to activate the bishop by opening up the diagonal with the pawn. } { [%csl Bb6][%cal Ge6e5,Rb6g1] [%clk 0:02:40] } 20. Qe5 { White stops black from activating the bishop by sticking the queen in front of the pawn. } { [%clk 0:01:34] } (20. Kh1 { A nice line demonstrating how important it is to activate all pieces, in this case the black bishop.
Kh1 played to unpin the d4 pawn. } { [%csl Bd4][%cal Rb6g1] } 20... e5 21. dxe5 { This then allows white to take with the unpinned pawn. } 21... Bxf3 { White can take with either the pawn or the rook. Taking with the pawn avoids mate, but taking with the rook is more natural. } { [%cal Rf1f3,Rg2f3] } 22. Rxf3 { And now there is forced mate with a queen sac. } 22... Qxh2+ 23. Kxh2 Rh4#) 20... Bxf3 { [%clk 0:02:28] } 21. Rxf3 { [%clk 0:01:31] } 21... Rxf3 { [%clk 0:02:27] } 22. gxf3 { White is now better after black trades off their most active pieces on the kingside. } { [%clk 0:01:30] } 22... Rf8 { Black attempts to capitalize on the weakened pawn structure but... } { [%csl Bf3,Bh2] [%clk 0:02:00] } 23. Kg2 { ... white easily defends with the king while also unpinning the pawn } { [%csl Bd4][%cal Gg2f3,Gg2h2,Rb6g1] [%clk 0:01:26] } 23... Qg6 { Threatening infiltration... } { [%cal Gg6c2] [%clk 0:01:51] } 24. Rd2 { ... which is simply stopped with Rd2 } { [%clk 0:01:19] } 24... Rf5 { Nice move, can't take with the knight due to the pin. } { [%cal Rg6g2] [%clk 0:01:47] } 25. Qe4 { [%clk 0:01:15] } 25... Qg5? { This blunders the pawn. } { [%csl Re6][%cal Re4e6] [%clk 0:01:43] } 26. Qxe6+ { [%clk 0:01:12] } 26... Rf7 { [%clk 0:01:35] } 27. Re2 { [%clk 0:01:11] } 27... Qd8 { [%clk 0:01:27] } 28. Qxc6 { Winning the pawn. } { [%clk 0:01:06] } (28. Qe8+ { Trading queens and rooks also works, black's bishop is more or less not in play and this would setup a pleasant endgame for white. } 28... Qxe8 29. Rxe8+ Rf8 30. Rxf8+ Kxf8) 28... Kf8 { [%clk 0:01:24] } 29. Re5 { [%clk 0:01:02] } 29... Rf6 { [%clk 0:01:18] } 30. Qe4 { [%clk 0:01:00] } 30... c6 { [%clk 0:01:14] } 31. Qxh7 { Another free pawn } { [%clk 0:00:58] } 31... Rh6 { [%clk 0:01:07] } 32. Rf5+ { [%clk 0:00:56] } 32... Ke7 { [%clk 0:01:02] } 33. Qxg7+ { [%clk 0:00:55] } 33... Kd6 { [%clk 0:01:00] } 34. Qxh6+ { [%clk 0:00:54] } 34... Kc7 { [%clk 0:00:53] } 35. Rf7+ { [%clk 0:00:53] } 35... Kb8 { [%clk 0:00:52] } 36. Qxc6 { [%clk 0:00:52] } 36... Qc8 { [%clk 0:00:41] } 37. Qxc8+ { [%clk 0:00:47] } 37... Kxc8 { [%clk 0:00:40] } 38. h4 { [%clk 0:00:47] } 38... Bd8 { The bishop is finally free, but too late. } { [%csl Bd8] [%clk 0:00:37] } 39. h5 { [%clk 0:00:46] } 39... Bg5 { [%clk 0:00:36] } 40. Nf5 { [%clk 0:00:44] } 40... Bd2 { [%clk 0:00:35] } 41. h6 { [%clk 0:00:43] } 41... Bxh6 { [%clk 0:00:34] } 42. Nxh6 { [%clk 0:00:43] } 42... a5 { [%clk 0:00:33] } 43. f4 { [%clk 0:00:43] } 43... axb4 { [%clk 0:00:30] } 44. axb4 { [%clk 0:00:43] } 44... Kd8 { [%clk 0:00:29] } 45. f5 { [%clk 0:00:43] } 45... Ke8 { [%clk 0:00:28] } 46. f6 { [%clk 0:00:43] } 46... Kd8 { [%clk 0:00:27] } 47. Kf3 { [%clk 0:00:43] } 47... Ke8 { [%clk 0:00:26] } 48. Kf4 { [%clk 0:00:43] } 48... Kd8 { [%clk 0:00:26] } 49. Ke5 { [%clk 0:00:42] } 49... Kc8 { [%clk 0:00:25] } 50. Kd6 { [%clk 0:00:42] } 50... Kb8 { [%clk 0:00:23] } 51. Rd7 { [%clk 0:00:41] } 51... Kc8 { [%clk 0:00:22] } 52. f7 { [%clk 0:00:41] } 52... Kb8 { [%clk 0:00:19] } 53. f8=Q# { 1-0 White wins by checkmate. } { [%clk 0:00:41] } 1-0


