{ THEMES and Things to Look For
- Violating the general rules of taking toward the center and opening up squares to develop.
- Defending with tempo, when you have pieces hanging, look for moves that defend the piece while also attacking.

Stream footage here: https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=OkjaIVnXg9o
Game played here: https://www.chess.com/live/game/5845138966 }
1. e4 { [%clk 0:05:00] } 1... d6 { [%clk 0:05:00] } 2. d4 { [%clk 0:04:58] } 2... Nf6 { [%clk 0:04:59] } 3. Nc3 { [%clk 0:04:57] } 3... g6 { [%clk 0:04:59] } 4. f4 { Start of the Austrian attack, idea is to build a big center by developing pieces to support the center.
The Austrian was first played in 1850 by John Cochrane. } { [%csl Gd4,Ge4,Gf4] [%clk 0:04:48] } 4... Bg7 { [%clk 0:04:58] } 5. Nf3 { [%clk 0:04:42] } 5... O-O { White has some choices in how to develop the bishop.
While Bc4 looks like a good move to develop the bishop, it runs into... } { [%csl Gd3,Gc4][%cal Gf1c4] [%clk 0:04:57] } 6. Bd3 { Yes, the bishop is blocked, but this follows the idea of the Austrian which is to support the center. } { [%clk 0:04:10] } (6. Bc4 Nxe4 7. Nxe4 d5 { ... this pawn fork tactic. which results in black winning a pawn. }) 6... Nbd7 { [%clk 0:04:53] } 7. e5 { [%clk 0:03:54] } 7... dxe5 { Which pawn to take with? f pawn would open up the bishop and get ideas of a kingside attack ready. However, Danya does not like this line and would normally play dxe5, though he does note that either are fine. } { [%cal Gc1h6,Gh2h4] [%clk 0:04:51] } 8. fxe5 { [%clk 0:03:22] } (8. dxe5 { Danya would have normally taken with the d pawn. This appears to violate several rules: taking away from the center and blocking the dark squared bishop from developing. } 8... Ne8 9. Be3 c6 10. Qe2 Nc7 11. O-O-O { This is the long term idea, opening up the d file with dxe5 allows for white to castle queenside and grab control of the open d file. } { [%cal Rd1d8] }) 8... Ng4 { [%clk 0:04:50] } (8... Ne8 { Black has an easy idea to break up the structure. } { [%cal Gc7c5,Rc5d4] } 9. Bf4 c5 10. d5 { Danya does note that this position is fine, but it feels "flimsy". }) 9. h3 { [%clk 0:03:10] } 9... Nh6 { [%clk 0:04:47] } 10. Bf4 { Bishop positioned to overprotect the center pawn. } { [%csl Gf4][%cal Gf4e5] [%clk 0:03:08] } 10... c5 { [%clk 0:04:41] } 11. d5 { [%clk 0:02:45] } (11. Qd2 { This is not as effective even though we attack the knight because... } 11... cxd4 12. Bxh6 dxc3 { And black is up a pawn with tempo. }) 11... Qb6 { [%clk 0:04:28] } 12. Qd2 { Ignoring the hanging pawn for now to continue to build up pressure on the kingside. } { [%csl Rb2,Rh6][%cal Rb6b2,Rd2h6] [%clk 0:02:32] } (12. Qc1 { Qc1 is an option here to defend, however, it makes it harder to castle queenside. } { [%csl Rh6,Gb2][%cal Gc1b2,Rc1h6] } 12... Nf5 13. Bxf5 gxf5 { White would love to castle long here and get the kingside attack rolling, but is unable to. }) 12... Qxb2 { Attacking the rook so we must move it first before continuing the kingside attack. } { [%csl Ra1][%cal Rb2a1] [%clk 0:04:13] } 13. Rb1 { [%clk 0:02:25] } 13... Qa3 { Key move here, immediately taking Bxh6 results in... } { [%clk 0:04:10] } 14. Rb3 { A move that serves two purposes 1) defending the knight so the queen can attack, and attacking black's queen with tempo. } { [%csl Gc3,Ra3][%cal Gb3c3,Rb3a3] [%clk 0:02:02] } (14. Bxh6 Bxh6 15. Qxh6 Qxc3+ { An equal exchange, but black gets an attacking tempo. }) 14... Qa5 { [%clk 0:04:08] } 15. Bxh6 { [%clk 0:02:01] } 15... Nxe5 { [%cal Rf3e5,Rh6f8] [%clk 0:03:59] } 16. Nxe5 { Forcing black into losing the exchange. } { [%csl Rf8][%cal Rh6f8] [%clk 0:01:51] } (16. Bxg7 { Why not Bxg7 to to win two pieces? } 16... Nxf3+ { Because black has an intermediate move which puts white up only a pawn vs a minor piece for a rook. } 17. gxf3 Kxg7) 16... Bxe5 { [%clk 0:03:46] } 17. Bxf8 { [%clk 0:01:50] } 17... Bg3+? { This check turns out to be a mistake both because it doesn't really accomplish anything and leaves the bishop open to being tactically exploited as we will see. } { [%clk 0:03:36] } (17... Kxf8 { Here black should have just taken and not exposed the bishop further. } 18. Ne4 Qc7) 18. Kf1 { [%clk 0:01:48] } 18... Kxf8 { White's king here is in an uncomfortable spot, one idea is to trade queens to lessen the disadvantage of white's king being in the open vs black's king which castled and is, for the time being, safe. } { [%csl Bf1,Bf8] [%clk 0:03:29] } 19. Ne4 { Allows white to force the queen trade by "fork" attacking and defending with the knight. } { [%csl Ra5,Rg3][%cal Rd2a5,Re4g3,Ge4d2] [%clk 0:01:25] } 19... Qxd2 { [%clk 0:03:00] } (19... Qc7 { Black could attempt to defend here with Qb7, but it still results in a minor piece trade. } 20. Nxg3 Qxg3) 20. Nxd2 { [%clk 0:01:24] } 20... b6 { [%clk 0:02:58] } 21. Be4 { [%clk 0:01:17] } 21... Rb8?? { Hanging the bishop.
Danya, of course, here pulls out Chessbase and brings us back to a game he played in 2006 where he made a similar board awareness blunder. Danya goes on somehow to draw the game a piece down. } { [%csl Rg3][%cal Rb3g3] [%clk 0:02:54] } (21... Bc7 { Trying to bring the bishop back results in... } 22. d6 { ... a nasty discovered attack. } { [%csl Rc7,Ra8][%cal Re4a8,Rd6c7] }) 22. Rxg3 { 1-0 White wins. } { [%clk 0:01:16] } 1-0


