{ THEMES and Things to Look For
- Removing the defenders, sometimes this tactical idea will require multiple steps to remove more than one defender before winning material.
- Attacking the opponent's king when it is stuck in the center and ours is castled by opening up the position and developing pieces.
- Keeping the tension (in this case two bishops staring down each other) to avoid giving the opponent an equal trade and a better position.
- Calculate tactics!

Stream footage here: https://youtu.be/OkjaIVnXg9o?t=1138
Game played here: https://www.chess.com/live/game/5845210321 }
1. Nf3 { [%clk 0:05:00] } 1... d5 { [%clk 0:04:56] } 2. d4 { [%clk 0:04:58] } 2... Nf6 { [%clk 0:04:54] } 3. Bf4 { Start of the London system (though, sort of a different move order than the usual 1.d4 2.Bf4). } { [%clk 0:04:56] } 3... Bf5 { [%clk 0:04:45] } 4. e3 { [%clk 0:04:54] } 4... e6 { [%clk 0:04:44] } 5. Bd3 { Two options here, trading bishops or dropping back to g7. } { [%cal Rf5d3,Gf5g6] [%clk 0:04:52] } 5... Bg6 { [%clk 0:04:27] } (5... Bxd3 6. cxd3 { This is okay, but allows white to easily develop and start queenside attacking ideas. } { [%cal Gd1b3,Gb1c3,Ga1c1] }) 6. Qe2 { [%clk 0:04:47] } (6. Bxg6 hxg6 { Idea here is to open up the h file if white retakes. } { [%cal Rh8h1] }) 6... Bd6 { [%clk 0:04:25] } 7. Ne5 { [%clk 0:04:42] } 7... O-O { [%clk 0:04:21] } 8. Nd2 { How to undermine white's center knight? } { [%csl Be5] [%clk 0:04:37] } 8... c5 { By attacking the pawn defending it. } { [%cal Rc5d4,Gd4e5] [%clk 0:04:09] } 9. c3 { [%clk 0:04:35] } 9... Nc6 { More pressure onto the knight. } { [%csl Re5,Rd4][%cal Rc6e5,Rc5d4] [%clk 0:04:08] } 10. g4? { g4 results in the loss of a pawn by white. Currently white has two defenders and black has one attacker. How do we exchange to win the pawn? } { [%cal Ge2g4,Ge5g4,Rf6g4] [%clk 0:04:25] } 10... Bxe5 { First removing one defender. } { [%clk 0:03:19] } 11. dxe5 { [%clk 0:04:22] } 11... Bxd3 { Then distracting the other one. } { [%clk 0:03:18] } 12. Qxd3 { [%clk 0:04:17] } 12... Nxg4 { Also resulting in an attack on the e5 pawn.
Is opening up the g file dangerous here? No, because white has no pieces attacking the kingside. } { [%cal Rg4e5,Rc6e5,Bg1g8] [%clk 0:03:16] } 13. h3 { [%clk 0:04:05] } 13... Ngxe5 { [%clk 0:03:09] } 14. Qe2 { [%clk 0:03:56] } 14... Ng6 { Prophylactic move to defend against Rg1 attacking ideas. } { [%csl Be1][%cal Bg1g6] [%clk 0:03:06] } 15. Bg3 { [%clk 0:03:46] } 15... d4 { White's king is still in the center and it's time to open up the position to setup black's attack. } { [%csl Be1] [%clk 0:03:05] } 16. O-O-O { Which pawn to take? } { [%clk 0:03:34] } 16... dxc3 { Definitely the c pawn to weaken the king's pawn structure with ideas of Qa5 to shift the attack to the queenside. } { [%cal Rd8a5] [%clk 0:02:55] } 17. bxc3 { [%clk 0:03:32] } 17... Qa5 { Attacking two pawns. } { [%csl Rc3,Ra2][%cal Ra5c3,Ra5a2] [%clk 0:02:54] } 18. Nb3 { [%clk 0:03:24] } 18... Qxc3+ { [%clk 0:02:51] } 19. Kb1 { [%clk 0:03:12] } 19... c4 { [%clk 0:02:47] } 20. Nd4 { [%clk 0:03:05] } (20. Rc1 { If black attempts to attack the queen, then white forces the queen trade while up three pawns. } 20... Qd3+ 21. Qxd3 cxd3) 20... e5 { Black would like white to trade knights to open up the b file so we can get the rook into the attack. e5 also nicely restricts white's bishop from controlling the b1 square. } { [%csl Rc6,Rb8,Ge5][%cal Rd4c6,Ga8b8,Rb8b1,Rg3b8] [%clk 0:02:10] } 21. Nc2 { [%clk 0:02:44] } (21. Nxc6 bxc6 { White is lost here as mate can only be prevented with heavy loss of material. }) 21... Rfd8 { [%clk 0:02:04] } 22. h4 { [%clk 0:02:38] } 22... Rd3 { [%clk 0:02:00] } 23. h5 { [%clk 0:02:34] } 23... Nge7 { We would like to get over to the juicy c3 square to attack (and hopefully fork something) in white's cramped position. } { [%cal Ge7d5,Gd5c3] [%clk 0:01:59] } 24. Rhe1 { [%clk 0:02:05] } 24... Nd5 { [%clk 0:01:57] } 25. h6 { [%clk 0:01:58] } 25... Qxe1! { This appears to be a mistake because Qxe1 is possible. } { [%clk 0:01:22] } 26. Qxe1 { [%clk 0:01:44] } (26. Nxe1 { Black also wins material if white takes with the knight. } 26... Nc3+ 27. Kc2 { [%csl Gc6] } 27... Nb4+ 28. Kb2 Nxe2 29. Rxd3 cxd3) 26... Rxd1+ { However, black can force the queen back onto a forkable square. } { [%cal Gd5c3,Rc3d1,Rc3b1] [%clk 0:01:14] } 27. Qxd1 { [%clk 0:01:42] } 27... Nc3+ { [%clk 0:01:13] } 28. Kc1 { [%clk 0:01:41] } 28... Nxd1 { [%clk 0:01:13] } 29. Kxd1 { Now black has an abundance of material to work with to win. } { [%clk 0:01:39] } 29... gxh6 { [%clk 0:01:13] } 30. Kd2 { [%clk 0:01:33] } 30... b5 { [%clk 0:01:12] } 31. Kc3 { [%clk 0:01:31] } 31... Rd8 { [%clk 0:01:11] } 32. a3 { [%clk 0:01:12] } 32... Rd3+ { [%clk 0:01:10] } 33. Kb2 { [%clk 0:01:10] } 33... a5 { [%clk 0:01:10] } 34. Ne1 { [%clk 0:01:00] } 34... Rb3+ { [%clk 0:01:09] } 35. Ka2 { [%clk 0:00:57] } 35... b4 { [%clk 0:01:08] } 36. axb4 { [%clk 0:00:55] } 36... Nxb4+ { [%clk 0:01:07] } 37. Ka1 { [%clk 0:00:51] } 37... c3 { [%clk 0:01:07] } 38. Bxe5 { [%clk 0:00:48] } 38... c2 { [%clk 0:01:06] } 39. Nxc2 { Gaining more material with the threat of the passed pawn. } { [%clk 0:00:45] } 39... Nxc2+ { [%clk 0:01:05] } 40. Ka2 { [%clk 0:00:43] } 40... Rb5 { [%clk 0:01:05] } 41. Bf4 { [%clk 0:00:38] } 41... h5 { Now we turn our attention to pushing the other passed pawns. } { [%cal Bh7h5,Bf7f5,Bh5h3] [%clk 0:01:03] } 42. e4 { [%clk 0:00:36] } 42... a4 { [%clk 0:01:03] } 43. e5 { [%clk 0:00:34] } 43... a3 { [%clk 0:01:02] } 44. Bc1 { The bishop is doing double duty and is soon to crumble under the pressure. } { [%csl Gc1][%cal Gc1f4,Gf4c1,Rc1a3,Gf4e5] [%clk 0:00:32] } 44... h4 { [%clk 0:01:01] } 45. Bf4 { [%clk 0:00:30] } 45... Rb2# { 0-1 Black wins by checkmate. } { [%clk 0:01:00] } 0-1


