[Annotator "https://lichess.org/@/rudisco"]

{ THEMES and Things to Look For
- Basic principles can be violated if your opponent is also violating them. For example, giving up development tempos to move pawns.
- When up material, develop!
- When your position is cramped, development may require moving a piece more than once.
- Do not be afraid to give up a pawn or two for a better position.

Stream footage here: https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=WwRujiYpgq0
Game played here: https://www.chess.com/live/game/5852707212 }
1. e4 { [%clk 0:05:00] } 1... c5 { Start of the Sicilian } { [%clk 0:04:58] } 2. f4 { [%clk 0:04:58] } 2... d5 { [%clk 0:04:49] } 3. e5 { Weakening white's control over the f5 square } { [%csl Bf5] [%clk 0:04:57] } (3. exd5 { White could also take here and black is gambiting a pawn for compensation that wins development, weakens white's kingside pawn structure, and gives black control over the d4 square. } 3... Nf6 4. c4 e6 5. dxe6 Bxe6 { At the end of this line, black is down a pawn, but has compensation in the form of better development, white's kingside is permanently weakened, and d4 is ripe for an outpost for the knight. } { [%csl Bd4,Gb8][%cal Gb8c6,Gc6d4] }) 3... Nc6 { [%clk 0:04:23] } 4. Nf3 { [%clk 0:04:55] } 4... Bf5 { [%clk 0:04:18] } 5. c3 { White has ideas of going d4 to reinforce the center. } { [%csl Gd4][%cal Gd2d4,Gd4e5] [%clk 0:04:41] } 5... d4 { Black occupies it instead. Black is allowed to "violate" the principle of not developing pieces in the opening because white has also given up development tempo by moving pawns. } { [%clk 0:04:03] } 6. d3 { [%clk 0:04:29] } 6... e6 { [%clk 0:03:56] } 7. Be2 { Black has some development issues due to white's pawn controlling the natural squares for the bishop and the knight, restricting them to developing e7 which will block the other from developing. To fix this, black must move one of them twice. } { [%csl Rf6,Rd6,Ge7][%cal Re5f6,Re5d6,Gf8e7,Gg8e7] [%clk 0:04:28] } 7... Nge7 { Moving the knight first with ideas of planting it on d5. } { [%csl Gd5][%cal Ge7d5] [%clk 0:03:21] } 8. O-O { [%clk 0:04:26] } 8... Nd5 { [%clk 0:03:19] } 9. c4 { [%clk 0:04:06] } 9... Ne3 { [%clk 0:03:18] } 10. Bxe3 { [%clk 0:04:04] } 10... dxe3 { [%clk 0:03:18] } 11. Qc1 { [%clk 0:04:04] } 11... Nd4 { This appears to sac a pawn, however black has Nc3 to win the trapped rook. } { [%csl Re3,Gc2,Ra1][%cal Gd4c2,Rc1e3,Rc2a1] [%clk 0:03:02] } 12. Nc3 { Black has a couple of options here. Simple development with bf7 is a move, however there is a material winning trade. } { [%clk 0:03:55] } 12... Nxe2+ { [%clk 0:02:24] } 13. Nxe2 { The pawn is now hanging, which piece to take with? } { [%csl Rd3][%cal Rf5d3,Rd8d3] [%clk 0:03:52] } 13... Qxd3 { The queen is better as it defends black's e3 pawn. } { [%csl Ge3][%cal Gd3e3] [%clk 0:02:23] } (13... Bxd3 14. Qxe3 { Taking with the bishop hangs the pawn and gives white easy development ideas. } { [%cal Ga1d1] }) 14. Rd1?? { Hanging the knight } { [%csl Re2][%cal Rd3e2] [%clk 0:03:48] } 14... Qxe2 { [%clk 0:02:20] } 15. Ng5 { [%clk 0:03:44] } 15... Be7 { No need to rush things, just develop the bishop. White does not have any concrete threats and black sets themselves up for castling kingside. } { [%cal Ge8g8] [%clk 0:01:54] } 16. Nf3 { [%clk 0:03:39] } 16... O-O { [%clk 0:01:52] } 17. Re1 { [%clk 0:03:34] } 17... Qf2+ { [%clk 0:01:45] } 18. Kh1 { [%clk 0:03:33] } 18... Rfd8 { Again, more development in exchange for the pawn. } { [%csl Re3][%cal Rc1e3,Re1e3] [%clk 0:01:24] } 19. Rxe3 { [%clk 0:03:26] } 19... Rd7 { Ideas of doubling on the open d file. } { [%cal Ga8d8,Rd8d1] [%clk 0:01:24] } 20. Qe1 { [%clk 0:03:20] } 20... Qxe1+ { Trading is now fine with material advantage and we are not going out of the way to do so. } { [%clk 0:01:19] } 21. Rexe1 { [%clk 0:03:16] } 21... Rad8 { [%clk 0:01:19] } 22. h3 { [%clk 0:03:15] } 22... h5 { Stopping g4. } { [%csl Gg4][%cal Gg2g4,Rh5g4] [%clk 0:01:18] } 23. Kh2 { [%clk 0:03:12] } 23... Rd3 { [%clk 0:01:16] } 24. b3 { [%clk 0:03:05] } 24... h4 { 0-1 Black wins. } { Now really preventing white from playing g4 threatening en-passant } { [%clk 0:01:15] } 0-1


