{ THEMES and Things to Look For
- Sometimes there won't be a principle that can be applied to find the best move, sometimes it will just require calculation.
- Gaining an edge at higher levels will require you to calculate better than your opponent.

Stream footage here: https://youtu.be/WwRujiYpgq0?t=937
Game played here: https://www.chess.com/analysis/game/live/5852773726 }
1. e4 { [%clk 0:05:00] } 1... c5 { [%clk 0:04:59] } 2. Bc4 { When 2.bc4 is played, black will want to play e6 at some point to blunt the bishop's influence. } { [%cal Ge7e6] [%clk 0:04:58] } 2... Nc6 { However, for now we develop the knight first. } { [%clk 0:04:41] } 3. Nf3 { [%clk 0:04:57] } 3... e6 { [%clk 0:04:40] } 4. O-O { [%clk 0:04:56] } 4... Nf6 { [%clk 0:04:38] } 5. Re1 { [%clk 0:04:52] } (5. e5 { e5 is not a threat as black has an easy way to meet it while developing. } 5... Ng4 6. Re1 { Defending the pawn. } { [%csl Ge5][%cal Ge1e5,Rg4e5] } 6... d6 7. exd6 Bxd6 8. h3 Nf6 { Idea for black in this position is to fianchetto the bishop with long term ideas of using them to support a kingside attack. } { [%cal Gc8b7,Gb7b6,Rb7h1,Rd6h2] }) 5... Nxe4 { Temporarily sacrificing the knight for center control and a pawn fork. } { [%csl Gd5][%cal Gd7d5,Rd5e4,Rd5c4] [%clk 0:04:21] } (5... d5 { d5 not as effective due to the discovered pin, preventing black from recapturing. } { [%cal Re1e8] } 6. exd5 { [%csl Re6][%cal Re1e8] }) 6. d4 { [%clk 0:04:43] } (6. Rxe4 d5 { What should white do here? } { [%csl Rc4,Re4][%cal Rd5e4,Rd5c4] } 7. Bxd5 { Getting maximum material out of the exchange. } 7... Qxd5 8. Nc3 { [%cal Gc3e4,Rc3d5] } 8... Qd8 { Now white does not have a light squared bishop and black has the idea of fianchettoing the bishop for uncontested control of the long diagonal. } { [%csl Gb7][%cal Gb7b6,Gc8b7,Rb7h1] }) 6... d5 { White chooses not to take so black is just up a pawn and can still play d5 for center control also defending the knight. } { [%cal Gd5e4,Rd5c4] [%clk 0:04:19] } 7. Bb5 { [%clk 0:04:41] } 7... Bd7 { [%clk 0:04:04] } 8. Nc3 { Should we take the knight? } { [%csl Rc3][%cal Re4c3] [%clk 0:04:35] } 8... Nxc3 { Yes, an even material trade plus ruining white's pawn structure. } { [%clk 0:03:51] } 9. bxc3 { [%clk 0:04:33] } 9... Be7 { [%clk 0:03:49] } 10. c4 { Should we take a pawn? If so which one and how? } { [%csl Rd4,Rc4][%cal Rc5d4,Rd5c4,Rc6d4] [%clk 0:04:27] } 10... Nxd4 { 0-1 Black wins. } { [%clk 0:03:12] } (10... dxc4 { This is unpleasant because it gives white a lot of piece activity even if they sac two pawns. } { [%cal Re1e8] } 11. d5 exd5 12. Qxd5 O-O 13. Bb2 { A lot of activity for white's pieces while black's remain on the back ranks. } { [%cal Rb2g7] }) (10... O-O { Castling is okay, but allows white the initiative and a chance to restore material equiality. } 11. cxd5 exd5 12. dxc5 Bxc5 13. Qxd5) 11. Nxd4 { The game may have continued here and this is why we take with the knight. } (11. Bxd7+ { White may try to remedy this by taking the bishop first, but this also doesn't work. } 11... Qxd7 12. Nxd4 cxd4 13. cxd5 Qxd5 { Leaving white down another pawn with the same skewer idea. } { [%cal Ge7f6,Rf6a1] }) 11... cxd4 12. Qxd4?? Bf6 { Skewering the queen and the rook. } { [%csl Rd4,Ra1][%cal Rf6a1] } 0-1


