[Annotator "https://lichess.org/@/rudisco"]

{ THEMES and Things to Look For
- Developing while attacking is critical, not every move in an attack has to directly threaten checkmate. Sometimes a preparational move such as opening up a file can be just as powerful if not required for an attack to succeed.
- To find overloading tactics, one must identify two possible avenues for a checkmate and find a way to threaten both so that the defending piece is overloaded.

Stream footage here: https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ZlIq20_wnho
Game played here: https://www.chess.com/live/game/5852815220 }
1. e4 { [%clk 0:04:59] } 1... d6 { [%clk 0:05:00] } 2. d4 { Another Pirc! } { [%clk 0:04:57] } 2... c6 { Different from the other lines, but we can adhere to our development principles here. Could occupy the center or develop the knight. } { [%csl Gf4,Gc3][%cal Gb1c3,Gf2f4] [%clk 0:05:00] } 3. Nc3 { Developing the knight and tightening control of the center. } { [%csl Ge4][%cal Gc3e4] [%clk 0:04:49] } (3. c4 { Danya makes a point here that the amount of the center that one occupies is not the most important measure of a good position, the amount of control one has matters as wel. }) 3... Qc7 { [%clk 0:04:59] } 4. Nf3 { [%clk 0:04:27] } 4... e5 { [%clk 0:04:59] } 5. Bc4 { [%clk 0:04:22] } 5... Bg4 { Pinning the knight and indirectly exerting pressure on d4. } { [%csl Rf3,Rd4][%cal Rg4d1,Re5d4] [%clk 0:04:57] } 6. Be3 { Developing and defending the pawn. } { [%csl Gd4][%cal Ge3d4] [%clk 0:04:00] } (6. Bxf7+ { This is one tactical idea to draw the king out and end up a pawn in the exchange. However... } 6... Qxf7 { ... the queen is sitting on the 7th rank instead of the 8th and is available to defend. Note that even if the queen were on d8, this still would not work as the queen can take the knight and defend the bishop. } { [%csl Gd8,Rg5][%cal Rd8g5,Gg5g4] }) 6... Bh5 { This pin is annoying, how can we break the pin without bringing the bishop back to e2? } { [%csl Rd1][%cal Rh5d1] [%clk 0:04:54] } 7. h3 { With ideas of getting rid of the pin. Note that at first this move looks passive, however it is okay when the opponent has chosen a very passive line, white is still ahead in development. White is also not concerned about castling as the king is safe in this position and will have the option of castling queenside later. } { [%cal Gg2g4] [%clk 0:03:18] } 7... Be7 { [%clk 0:04:49] } 8. g4 { Getting rid of the pin with ideas of castling queenside. } { [%cal Gd1e2,Ge1c1] [%clk 0:03:16] } 8... Bg6 { [%clk 0:04:36] } 9. Qe2 { [%clk 0:03:12] } 9... Nf6 { [%clk 0:04:33] } 10. O-O-O { Getting the king to safety and developing the rook. White does offer the pawn sac here for development. } { [%csl Re4][%cal Rd1d8,Rf6e4] [%clk 0:02:58] } 10... Nxe4 { [%clk 0:04:27] } 11. Nxe4 { [%clk 0:02:56] } 11... Bxe4 { [%clk 0:04:26] } 12. dxe5 { Here white chooses to open up the center to take advantage of the lead in development when black's king is still stuck in the center. } { [%clk 0:02:46] } 12... Bxf3 { There are three things that we could capture here. Turns out all three moves lead to a clear advantage according to the engine, but the played move is mot accurate. } { [%csl Rf3,Rf7,Rd6][%cal Re2f3,Rc4f7,Re5d6] [%clk 0:04:19] } 13. Bxf7+ { Danya style move, sacrificing the bishop really open up the center. } { [%clk 0:02:13] } (13. Qxf3 { Trading material and threatening mate. } { [%csl Rf7][%cal Rf3f7] } 13... d5 { This allows black to control the center and take a free tempo that can be used to get their king to safety. } { [%csl Rc4][%cal Rd5c4,Ge8g8] }) (13. exd6 { Opening up the center while proposing a queen trade. } { [%cal Rd6c7,Rd6e7] } 13... Bxe2 14. dxc7 { Black may be threatening the rook, but white is threatening to promote. } { [%csl Rd1][%cal Gc7c8,Re2d1] } 14... Nd7 15. Bxe2 { White winning the exchange up a pawn } 15... Rc8 16. Bf4 { [%csl Gc7][%cal Gf4c7] }) 13... Kxf7 { [%clk 0:04:12] } 14. Qxf3+ { [%clk 0:02:12] } 14... Kg8 { [%clk 0:04:09] } 15. e6 { Threatening mate. } { [%csl Rf7][%cal Re6f7,Rf3f7] [%clk 0:01:55] } (15. exd6 { This might be a better line, more directly opening up the center and bringing all of white's pieces into play. } 15... Bxd6 16. Qf5) 15... Bf6 { [%clk 0:04:05] } 16. Bf4! { Critical move! This move appears innocuous, but is further developing the bishop into a position to open up the d file so the rooks can be moved into play.
The long term idea is to create two mating threats and overload black to checkmate. } { [%csl Rd8,Rd6,Rf7][%cal Rd1d8,Rf4c7,Rf3f7] [%clk 0:01:31] } 16... Be5 { [%clk 0:03:50] } 17. Bxe5 { Black plays into white's hands and opens up the d file for white. } { [%clk 0:01:29] } 17... dxe5 { [%clk 0:03:49] } 18. Rd8+ { Overloading and distracting the queen so white can mate. } { [%clk 0:01:19] } (18. Rd7 Qxd7 (18... Nxd7 19. Qf7# { This doesn't work since black's queen no longer defends the mating square. }) 19. exd7 Nxd7 20. Qb3+ Kf8 21. Qxb7 { Another winning line, but why not just go for mate? }) 18... Qxd8 { [%clk 0:03:45] } 19. Qf7# { 1-0 White wins by checkmate. } { Note that this mate was wholly prepared by 16...Bf4, this allowed white to break through the d file and complete the mating attack started by the initial bishop sacrifice. } { [%clk 0:01:19] } 1-0


