{ THEMES and Things to Look For
- Open positions are better for the player with better developed pieces. If you have a development advantage, look to open up the position for attacking opportunities.
- 15 second rule - once you've finished calculating a line, spend 15 more seconds thinking to see if you have missed anything.

Stream footage here: https://youtu.be/ZlIq20_wnho?t=662
Game played here: https://www.chess.com/live/game/5852862143 }
1. e4 { [%clk 0:05:00] } 1... c5 { [%clk 0:04:57] } 2. f4 { [%clk 0:04:59] } 2... d5 { [%clk 0:04:56] } 3. e5 { [%clk 0:04:57] } 3... Nc6 { Following the same line as a previous game. } { [%clk 0:04:55] } 4. Nf3 { [%clk 0:04:56] } 4... Bf5 { [%clk 0:04:52] } 5. Be2 { [%clk 0:04:56] } 5... e6 { [%clk 0:04:45] } 6. O-O { [%clk 0:04:54] } 6... Nge7 { Preparing Ng6. } { [%csl Gg6][%cal Ge7g6] [%clk 0:04:43] } 7. Nh4 { Throwing a wrench into black's plans with the threat of an exchange that ruins black's structure. } { [%csl Rf5][%cal Rh4f5] [%clk 0:04:53] } 7... Bg6 { Vacating the square so the knight has space. } { [%csl Gf5][%cal Ge7f5] [%clk 0:04:21] } (7... Ng6 8. Nxf5 exf5 { This would be a disastrous structure for black. }) 8. c3 { Black has the option to meet c3 with d4 much like the last game but... } { [%csl Gd4,Gf5][%cal Gd5d4,Ge7f5] [%clk 0:04:51] } 8... Nf5 { ... black chooses the knight this time. } { [%clk 0:04:17] } 9. Nxf5 { [%clk 0:04:49] } (9. Nxg6 { If white takes the bishop, black opens up the h file for the rook to prepare a kingside attack. } { [%cal Rh7g6,Rh8h2] } 9... hxg6 { [%cal Rh8h1,Rd8h4] }) 9... Bxf5 { [%clk 0:04:10] } 10. Bg4 { [%clk 0:04:47] } 10... Bd3 { This move doesn't look like it accomplishes much but the idea is... } { [%clk 0:03:49] } (10... Bg6 11. f5 { Playing Bg6 immediately allows white to break up the pawn structure and attack the bishop since white's g4 bishop is there to support the advance. } { [%csl Gg4][%cal Rf5g6,Rf5e6,Gg4f5] }) 11. Be2 { [%clk 0:04:45] } 11... Bg6 { ... to allow black to move back to g6 safely and neutralize the threat of white playing f5. Danya emphasizes a "15 second rule" for calculating. Once you've calculated a line, take 15 more seconds to see if you are missing anything. } { [%csl Rf5][%cal Rf4f5,Re6f5,Rg6f5] [%clk 0:03:48] } 12. d3 { [%clk 0:04:43] } (12. d4 { If white played d4 instead, black has an idea to win material. } 12... Qb6 { [%csl Rd4,Rb2][%cal Rb6b2,Rb6d4,Rc6d4,Rc5d4] } 13. Be3 Qxb2 { Attempting to defend one, loses the other. }) 12... Be7 { [%clk 0:03:40] } 13. Bg4 { [%clk 0:04:41] } 13... O-O { White plays Bg4 again, but now it is too late, black has had a chance to castle and get the king to safety before the center is opened up. } { [%clk 0:03:35] } 14. f5 { [%clk 0:04:38] } 14... exf5 { [%clk 0:03:34] } 15. Bxf5 { White hangs the pawn. } { [%csl Re5][%cal Rc6e5] [%clk 0:04:36] } 15... Nxe5 { Note here that white's problems down the line are directly related to undeveloped pieces. White spent too many tempi moving the bishop around and it is at the cost of their development. } { [%csl Bc1,Bb1,Ba1,Be5,Bg6,Be7] [%clk 0:03:25] } 16. Bxg6 { What to take with? } { [%cal Rh7g6,Rf7g6,Re5g6] [%clk 0:04:31] } 16... fxg6 { However, opening up the f file is very beneficial for black due to white's undeveloped pieces. This allows black to leverage their development to gain the initiative and attack white's king before white's pieces can be brought into the game. } { [%csl Bc1,Bb1,Ba1][%cal Rf8f1] [%clk 0:02:53] } (16... Nxg6 { This adheres to the principle of maintaining the pawn structure and protecting the king. } { [%csl Bf7,Bg7,Bh7] }) (16... hxg6 { Another move that doesn't expose black's king. }) 17. Rxf8+ { [%clk 0:04:29] } (17. Bf4 { White would have been better advised to play this so they can develop with tempo by attacking the knight. } { [%csl Re5][%cal Rf4e5] }) 17... Qxf8 { [%clk 0:02:51] } 18. Nd2?? { Blundering another pawn. } { [%csl Rd3][%cal Re5d3] [%clk 0:04:27] } 18... Nxd3 { [%clk 0:02:50] } 19. Nf3 { [%clk 0:04:21] } 19... c4 { Outposting the knight. } { [%cal Gc4d3] [%clk 0:02:35] } 20. Qe2 { [%clk 0:04:18] } 20... Bc5+ { Developing the bishop with tempo so black can develop the rook. } { [%csl Ge8][%cal Ga8e8] [%clk 0:02:30] } 21. Nd4 { [%clk 0:04:13] } 21... Re8 { [%clk 0:02:28] } 22. Qf3 { [%clk 0:04:08] } 22... Re1+ { 0-1 Black wins. } { Notice where the forced mate is going to be delivered, the opened f file! Black wins by overloading the queen which is tasked with too many defensive jobs. This is directly a result of white's bishop and rook being undeveloped.
All of black's pieces in the final position are developed and aimed towards the white's king. } { [%csl Bc1,Ba1,Re1,Rd3,Rc5,Rf8][%cal Rf8f1] [%clk 0:02:26] } 0-1


