1. e4 { [%clk 0:05:00] } 1... c5 { [%clk 0:04:59] } 2. Qf3 { [%clk 0:04:56] } 2... Nc6 { [%clk 0:04:58] } 3. c3 { [%clk 0:04:55] } 3... Nf6 { Immediate ideas of opening up the center... } { [%csl Gd5][%cal Gd7d5] [%clk 0:04:50] } 4. h3 { [%clk 0:04:52] } 4... e6 { ... which we prepare with e6 } { [%csl Gd5][%cal Gd7d5] [%clk 0:04:27] } 5. d3 { [%clk 0:04:51] } 5... d5 { [%clk 0:04:26] } 6. Nd2 { [%clk 0:04:49] } 6... Bd6 { Following the usual plan of developing. } { [%clk 0:04:17] } 7. Be2 { [%csl Ge3] [%clk 0:04:45] } 7... O-O { [%clk 0:04:01] } 8. exd5?? { Positional blunder allowing black to open up a direct line of attack against the king. White also cannot immediately castle and all their pieces are sitting in a very cramped position. } { [%csl Bd2,Be2,Bg1,Bc1][%cal Re8e1] [%clk 0:04:41] } 8... exd5 { [%clk 0:03:49] } 9. g4 { [%clk 0:04:19] } 9... Re8 { Executing black's plan to attack the king. } { [%cal Re8e1] [%clk 0:03:45] } 10. Qg2 { [%clk 0:04:08] } 10... Ne5 { Stopping white from playing Nf3 and preparing to check and pin the bishop. } { [%csl Rf3,Rd3,Re2][%cal Re5f3,Re5d3,Re8e1] [%clk 0:03:29] } 11. Kd1 { Black has most of their pieces developed, how can they continue the attack? } { [%csl Gd4,Gc4][%cal Gc5c4,Gd5d4] [%clk 0:03:56] } 11... c4 { Allowing Nd3 if white attempts to close the position with d4. } { [%csl Gd3][%cal Ge5d3] [%clk 0:02:59] } (11... d4? { This is a mistake because it allows white to close the position and make attacking the king more difficult. } 12. c4 { Closing the position. }) 12. d4 { It looks like white is also able to close the position here, but the crucial difference is that this allows the knight to be outposted on d3. } { [%csl Gd3][%cal Ge5d3] [%clk 0:03:54] } 12... Nd3 { [%clk 0:02:50] } 13. Bxd3 { [%clk 0:03:52] } 13... cxd3 { Now black dominates the center. } { [%csl Gc4,Ge4,Gc2,Ge2][%cal Gd5c4,Gd5e4,Gd3c2,Gd3e2] [%clk 0:02:49] } 14. g5 { [%clk 0:03:45] } 14... Ne4 { [%clk 0:02:48] } (14... Qe7 { Would have also been an excellent move threatening mate. } { [%csl Re1][%cal Re7e1] }) 15. Nxe4 { [%clk 0:03:41] } 15... dxe4 { [%clk 0:02:47] } 16. h4 { [%clk 0:03:23] } 16... Qa5 { Ideas of setting up mate. } { [%csl Rc2][%cal Ga5a4,Ra4c2] [%clk 0:02:37] } 17. h5 { [%clk 0:02:59] } 17... Be6 { Preparing the way for Qa4 } { [%csl Rc2,Rb3][%cal Re6b3,Ga5a4,Ra4c2] [%clk 0:02:36] } (17... Qa4+ { However, Qa4 cannot be played immediately due to... } 18. b3) 18. a3 { [%clk 0:02:53] } 18... Qa4+? { White prevents themselves from playing b3 so Qa4 can be played immediately. } { [%clk 0:02:33] } 19. Ke1 { [%clk 0:02:49] } 19... Qc2 { Plans to set up a mating battery. } { [%csl Rd1][%cal Ge6b3,Rb3d1] [%clk 0:02:29] } 20. Rh2 { [%clk 0:02:24] } 20... Bb3 { [%clk 0:02:27] } 21. Bd2 { [%clk 0:02:05] } 21... Bxh2 { [%clk 0:02:17] } 22. Qxh2 { [%clk 0:02:03] } 22... e3 { Thematic move for this game, further opening up the position! } { [%cal Re8e1] [%clk 0:02:09] } 23. fxe3 { [%clk 0:02:00] } 23... Rxe3+ { [%clk 0:02:08] } 24. Kf1 { [%clk 0:01:51] } 24... Rae8 { Bringing all of black's pieces into the game. } { [%cal Re8e1,Rb3d1] [%clk 0:02:01] } 25. Qf4 { [%clk 0:01:40] } 25... R3e4 { [%clk 0:01:55] } 26. Qf5 { [%clk 0:01:33] } 26... Qxd2 { 0-1 Black wins. } { [%clk 0:01:52] } 0-1


