{ THEMES and Things to Look For
- Retreating rather than trading can be the right thing to do especially if it crowds your opponents position and prevents them from developing.
- Developmental advantages can be spent by using those tempi to disrupt or attack your opponent.

Stream footage here: https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=iQQDU3H7vaU
Game played here: https://www.chess.com/live/game/5860442631 }
1. e4 { [%clk 0:04:56] } 1... d5 { [%clk 0:04:59] } 2. exd5 { [%clk 0:04:54] } 2... Qxd5 { Start of the Scandinavian } { [%clk 0:04:58] } 3. Nc3 { [%clk 0:04:51] } 3... Qa5 { [%clk 0:04:57] } 4. d4 { [%clk 0:04:49] } 4... Bf5 { [%clk 0:04:55] } 5. Nf3 { [%clk 0:04:47] } 5... Nc6 { Applying pressure to the pawn. } { [%csl Rd4][%cal Rc6d4] [%clk 0:04:54] } 6. Bb5 { Attacking the knight and preventing black from castling. An example of dynamic development, choosing developmental ideas tailored to what the opponent is playing vs developing to nice looking squares without a strategic reason. } { [%csl Gc8,Rc6][%cal Ge8c8,Rb5c6] [%clk 0:04:23] } 6... Bd7 { How can white pressure black's position? } { [%clk 0:04:24] } (6... O-O-O 7. Bxc6 bxc6 { If black castles, the exchange ruins the pawn structure defending the queen. }) 7. d5 { White is allowed to move this pawn again because of their developmental lead. Black's pieces are somewhat stuck so white can take advantage of these weaknesses on the queenside. } { [%csl Bf3,Bc3,Bb5,Rc6][%cal Rd5c6] [%clk 0:04:07] } 7... Nb8 { Defending the bishop. } { [%csl Gd7][%cal Gb8d7] [%clk 0:04:10] } 8. Bc4 { White does not trade bishops because this helps improve black's position. By playing a retreating move to support the pawn, white prevents the knight's development. Also note how black's knight is blocked by the bishop. } { [%csl Rc6,Gd5][%cal Rd5c6,Gc4d5,Gb8c6,Gb8d7] [%clk 0:03:32] } 8... Qc5 { [%clk 0:04:04] } 9. Qe2 { A multi purpose move. Defending the c4 bishop, x-raying black's king, preparing Bc3 and long castling. } { [%csl Gc4,Re8,Ge3,Gc1][%cal Ge2c4,Re2e8,Gc1e3,Ge1c1] [%clk 0:03:17] } (9. b3 { Why is this move inaccurate? } { [%csl Bc3] } 9... b5 10. Be2 { It leaves the knight undefended and allows black opportunities to win it. } { [%csl Rc3][%cal Rc5c3] } 10... Qxc3+) 9... Nf6 { [%clk 0:03:57] } 10. Be3 { Development with tempo. } { [%csl Rc5][%cal Re3c5] [%clk 0:03:16] } 10... Qb4 { [%clk 0:03:52] } 11. O-O-O { Completing white's development. } { [%clk 0:03:15] } 11... Na6 { How can white prevent black from castling? } { [%clk 0:03:39] } 12. Ne5 { This is a subtle move, threatening Nf7 which would be a fork on the rooks. White also has Bxa6 to break open the position should black castle queenside. } { [%csl Rf7,Ra6][%cal Re5f7,Re2a6] [%clk 0:02:38] } (12. Bxa6 { Taking here is tempting to disrupt black's plans of castling queenside, however... } 12... bxa6 { ... after opening up the b file black is now allowed attacking ideas. } { [%cal Ga8b8,Rb8b1] }) 12... O-O-O { [%clk 0:03:23] } 13. Bxa6 { [%clk 0:02:35] } 13... bxa6 { [%clk 0:03:21] } 14. Nxf7 { [%clk 0:02:24] } (14. Qxa6+ { Tempting move, but it gets met with } { [%csl Rc8][%cal Ra6c8] } 14... Qb7 { which throws a wrench into white's attack by offering the queen trade. }) 14... Rg8 { A nice series of intermezzo moves to follow, allowing white to win more material than just trading a knight for a rook. } { [%clk 0:03:17] } 15. Qxa6+ { Now white offers the trade... } { [%clk 0:02:16] } 15... Qb7 { [%clk 0:03:15] } 16. Qxb7+ { ... and accept it ... } { [%clk 0:02:15] } 16... Kxb7 { [%clk 0:03:14] } 17. Nxd8+ { ... with the goal of removing the defender so white can win the whole rook. } { [%clk 0:02:14] } 17... Kc8 { [%clk 0:03:13] } 18. Ne6 { [%clk 0:02:14] } 18... Bxe6 { [%clk 0:03:11] } 19. dxe6 { [%clk 0:02:12] } 19... g6 { [%clk 0:03:03] } 20. Rd4 { [%clk 0:02:08] } 20... Ne8 { Black would like to cut off the d file. How can white preempt this? } { [%csl Gd6][%cal Ge8d6] [%clk 0:02:57] } 21. Rd7 { By sliding the rook in before black can close the file. } { [%clk 0:01:38] } 21... Nd6 { [%clk 0:02:54] } 22. Nd5 { [%clk 0:01:35] } 22... c6 { [%clk 0:02:37] } 23. Nxe7+ { [%clk 0:01:29] } 23... Bxe7 { [%clk 0:02:33] } 24. Rxe7 { [%clk 0:01:29] } 24... Re8 { [%clk 0:02:27] } 25. Rxe8+ { [%clk 0:01:28] } 25... Nxe8 { The series of trades here are great for white. White does not have to go out of the way to find them and the simplification benefits white who is up material. } { [%clk 0:02:25] } 26. Rd1 { [%clk 0:01:28] } 26... Kc7 { [%clk 0:02:23] } 27. e7 { [%clk 0:01:28] } 27... Nd6 { [%clk 0:02:19] } 28. Rxd6 { [%clk 0:01:27] } 28... Kxd6 { [%clk 0:02:17] } 29. e8=Q { [%clk 0:01:27] } 29... c5 { [%clk 0:02:15] } 30. Bf4+ { [%clk 0:01:26] } 30... Kd5 { [%clk 0:02:13] } 31. b3 { [%clk 0:01:25] } 31... h6 { [%clk 0:02:11] } 32. c4+ { [%clk 0:01:24] } 32... Kd4 { [%clk 0:02:10] } 33. Qe3# { 1-0 White wins by checkmate. } { [%clk 0:01:24] } 1-0


