{ THEMES and Things to Look For
- When defending pieces, it is in general a good idea to defend a piece with the piece of lesser value.
- Demonstrating the Greek Gift sacrifice setup.
- Open positions benefit the player with better development. If you have a more developed position, don't voluntarily close the position!

Stream footage here: https://youtu.be/iQQDU3H7vaU?t=538
Game played here: https://www.chess.com/live/game/5860481431 }
1. e4 e6 2. d4 c6?! { Weird move from black. } 3. Nc3 { White continues plans of developing and controlling the center. } 3... d5 { Black plays some cross between the French and the Caro Kann. Not good because it is passive, giving white the development lead. } { [%csl Be6,Bd5,Bc6] } 4. Nf3 { Continuing with development. Not playing e5 to allow the position to open up and allow white to take advantage of their better development. } (4. e5? { Why is e5 inaccurate? White has a development advantage and closing the position prevents white from leveraging that advantage. It also allows black... } 4... c5 { ... the ability to attack the base of the pawn chain, but white cannot play c3 to support. } { [%csl Rd4][%cal Rc5d4,Gc2c3] } 5. dxc5 Bxc5 { Now black has equal if not better development. }) 4... dxe4 5. Nxe4 Be7 6. Bd3 { Nice space for the bishop, defending the knight. } (6. Bc4 { Not such a nice space for the bishop which is just staring down a supported pawn. } { [%cal Rc4f7] }) 6... Nf6 7. Nxf6+ Bxf6 { How can white support this pawn? } { [%csl Rd4][%cal Rf6d4,Gc1e3,Ge3d4,Gc2c3,Gc3d4] } 8. c3 { Defending with the piece of lesser value. } 8... O-O { Elements of the Greek Gift sacrifice are available here. However, black's bishop currently controls the square. The Greek Gift sac has also been called the Trojan Horse sacrifice.
The first person to have played the Greek Gift sacrifice is Gioachino Greco in 1620. } { [%csl Rh7,Gg5][%cal Rd3h7,Gf3g5,Rg5h7,Rf6g5] } 9. h4 { Preparing Ng5. This is a key move because even if black does not accept the gift, it allows white to continue attacking and weakening black's kingside defenses. } { [%csl Gg5][%cal Gf3g5] } 9... b6 (9... h6 { What if black figures out the Greek Gift is coming? How can white prepare the attack? } 10. g4 { Preparing to target the "hook" in h6. } { [%csl Bh6][%cal Gg4g5] } (10. Bc2 { Ideas of building a battery. } { [%cal Gd1d3] } 10... Nd7 11. Qd3 { Threatening mate in 1. } { [%csl Rh7][%cal Rd3h7] } 11... g6 { Black defends, but hangs the h pawn. } { [%csl Bh6] } 12. Bxh6)) (9... Qd5 { Black can try this. } 10. Bxh7+ Kxh7 11. Ng5+ Kg8 12. Qh5 Qf5 { Defending the critical square and stopping white's attack. } { [%csl Gh7][%cal Gf5h7] }) 10. Bxh7+ Kxh7 11. Ng5+ { The purpose of h4 is now revealed. } 11... Kg6 (11... Bxg5 { If black takes with the bishop, white has a forced mate. } 12. hxg5+ Kg8 13. Qh5) 12. Qd3+ Kh5 13. g4+ { No matter where black's king goes, checkmate is next. } { [%csl Gh6,Gg4,Rh7,Rf3][%cal Gh5h6,Gh5g4,Rd3h7,Rd3f3] } 1-0


