{ THEMES and Things to Look For
- Masterclass in building and maintaining a critical outpost.

Stream footage here: https://youtu.be/iQQDU3H7vaU?t=1195
Game played here: https://www.chess.com/live/game/5860530934 }
1. e4 { [%clk 0:05:00] } 1... c5 { [%clk 0:05:00] } 2. c4 { Somewhat rare move, but is a known line. } { [%csl Be4,Bc4] [%clk 0:04:57] } 2... Nc6 { White has severely weakened d4 since now no white pawn can attack it. This is a good spot for the knight to outpost on. } { [%csl Gd4][%cal Gc6d4] [%clk 0:04:44] } 3. Nc3 { How can black cement their control on d4? } { [%csl Bd4] [%clk 0:04:55] } 3... g6 { Preparing to fianchetto the bishop to control d5. } { [%csl Gg7,Rd4][%cal Gf8g7,Rg7d4] [%clk 0:04:17] } (3... d6 { d6 d5 is not good because it weakens black's d5 square as well allowing white the outpost. } { [%csl Bd5][%cal Ge7e5,Gc3d5] }) (3... e5 { Also creating the symmetrical weakness on black's side. } { [%csl Bd5] }) 4. Nf3 { [%clk 0:04:47] } 4... Bg7 { [%clk 0:04:16] } 5. d3 { [%clk 0:04:38] } 5... d6 { Preparing to develop the bishop. } { [%cal Gc8g4] [%clk 0:04:15] } 6. Be3 { How does black continue to enhance their control over d4? } { [%clk 0:04:36] } 6... Bg4 { Ideas of removing one of the defenders. } { [%csl Rf3,Gd4][%cal Rg4f3,Gf3d4] [%clk 0:03:54] } 7. Be2 { [%clk 0:04:34] } 7... Bxf3 { [%clk 0:03:53] } 8. Bxf3 { Black has no need to take the outpost yet. } { [%csl Gd4][%cal Gc6d4] [%clk 0:04:31] } 8... e6 { A seemingly passive move to prepare developing Ne7, however, note how passive white's position is, this gives black some time to develop in this way while maintaining control on d4. } { [%csl Ge7,Bf3,Be3,Bc3][%cal Gg8e7] [%clk 0:03:08] } (8... Nf6 { Nf6 not great because it blocks the bishop's influence on d4. } { [%cal Gg7f6] }) 9. Rc1 { [%clk 0:04:27] } 9... Nge7 { [%clk 0:03:06] } 10. O-O { [%clk 0:04:25] } 10... O-O { [%clk 0:03:04] } 11. b3 { [%clk 0:04:17] } 11... Nd4 { Now is the right time to put the knight on d4 now that black's development is completed. } { [%clk 0:02:56] } 12. Bg4 { [%clk 0:04:05] } (12. Bxd4 { If Bxd4, then we retake with... } 12... Bxd4 { ... the bishop to still allow for the outpost to be formed. } { [%csl Gd4][%cal Ge7c6,Gc6d4] }) 12... Nec6 { Lending more support to d4. "Look at these stallions ladies and gentlemen." } { [%csl Gd4][%cal Gc6d4] [%clk 0:02:54] } 13. f4 { With ideas of f5. } { [%csl Gf5][%cal Gf4f5] [%clk 0:03:50] } 13... f5 { Stopping f5. } { [%clk 0:02:46] } 14. Bh3 { [%clk 0:03:44] } 14... Qa5 { Activating the queen to pressure white's position. Okay to move the queen away from the king since white is not attacking. } { [%csl Rc3,Ra2][%cal Ra5c3,Ra5a2] [%clk 0:02:20] } (14... Qh4 { Also okay, but attacking with only the queen is ill advised. }) 15. Qd2 { [%clk 0:03:12] } 15... a6 { Starting a queenside attack by preparing b5. } { [%csl Gb5][%cal Gb7b5] [%clk 0:01:51] } 16. a4?? { Preventing the pawn break, but leaving the b pawn hanging and giving black a fork. } { [%csl Rb3,Rb5][%cal Rb3d2,Rb3c1,Rd4b3,Ra4b5] [%clk 0:03:05] } 16... Nxb3 { [%clk 0:01:39] } 17. Qc2 { [%clk 0:03:00] } 17... Nxc1 { [%clk 0:01:39] } 18. Qxc1 { [%clk 0:02:59] } 18... Qxc3 { [%clk 0:01:35] } 19. Qxc3 { [%clk 0:02:58] } 19... Bxc3 { [%clk 0:01:35] } 20. exf5 { [%clk 0:02:55] } 20... gxf5 { Taking towards the center. } { [%clk 0:01:34] } 21. Rb1 { [%clk 0:02:52] } 21... Rab8 { [%clk 0:01:33] } 22. g3 { [%clk 0:02:49] } 22... Bd4 { Forcing the trade of bishops so the knight can be forever outposted on d4. } { [%clk 0:01:20] } 23. Kf2 { [%clk 0:02:39] } 23... Bxe3+ { [%clk 0:01:19] } 24. Kxe3 { [%clk 0:02:37] } 24... Nd4 { [%clk 0:01:19] } 25. Bg2 { [%clk 0:02:31] } 25... b5 { Finally playing b5 to continue the queenside attack that was started with a6. } { [%clk 0:01:18] } 26. axb5 { [%clk 0:02:25] } 26... axb5 { [%clk 0:01:18] } 27. cxb5 { [%clk 0:02:24] } 27... Rxb5 { [%clk 0:01:18] } 28. Ra1 { [%clk 0:02:20] } 28... Rb2 { Infiltrating the 7th rank. } { [%csl Re2,Rg2][%cal Rb2g2] [%clk 0:01:17] } 29. Bf1 { [%clk 0:02:13] } 29... Rfb8 { 0-1 Black wins. } { [%clk 0:01:16] } 30. Ra7 Rxh2 31. Ra6 Rbb2 32. Rxd6 Rhe2+ 33. Bxe2 Rxe2# { Demonstrating a mating net idea. } 0-1


