{ THEMES and Things to Look For
- Flexibility in plans. Sometimes it is okay to retreat when an attack is blunted. Regroup, develop, and probe for other weaknesses!
- If your opponent appears to stop an idea, ask and calculate "what if I did it anyway?"
- When pawns have been moved near a king, look for weaknesses that can be exploited. In this case, pawns on g6 can make for nice "hooks" for white's pieces to attack.
- A successful attack requires two factors: 1) the presence of prices, 2) weaknesses near the opponent's king (Danya MS Paint session: https://youtu.be/woqgGKERnps?t=727)

Stream footage here: https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=woqgGKERnps
Game played here: https://www.chess.com/live/game/5860591675 }
1. e4 c6 { Start of the Caro-Kann } 2. d4 d5 3. exd5 cxd5 4. Bd3 { Guarding f5 and preventing black's bishop from occupying it. } { [%csl Gf5][%cal Gd3f5] } 4... Nc6 5. c3 Nf6 6. Ne2 (6. Nf3 { Slightly inaccurate due to allowing black to play a pin } 6... Bg4 { [%csl Rd1][%cal Rg4d1] } 7. Qb3 Bxf3 8. gxf3 { If white tries to escape the pin by moving the queen. Black gets an even material exchange plus ruining white's pawn structure. }) 6... Bg4 { Now the knight is guarded by the bishop so white can move the queen away and castle without fear of the pawn structure being ruined. } { [%csl Ge2][%cal Gd3e2] } 7. Qb3 { [%csl Ge2][%cal Gd3e2] } 7... Qd7 8. O-O e6 { Black wants Bd6, how can white try to contest this idea? } { [%csl Gd6][%cal Gf8d6] } 9. Bf4 { [%csl Rd6][%cal Rf4d6] } 9... Be7 (9... Bd6 { This appears to blunder a pawn but... } 10. Bxd6 Qxd6 11. Qxb7 Rb8 { ... black gets the skewer and will win the pawn back. } { [%csl Rb2][%cal Rb8b2] } 12. Qa6 Rxb2) 10. Nd2 O-O 11. h3 Bh5 { White's knights are a little passive so... } { [%csl Bd2,Be2] } 12. Ng3 { ... let's develop one with tempo. } { [%csl Rh5][%cal Rg3h5] } 12... Bg6 13. Bxg6 { Ruining black's structure with an even material exchange. } 13... hxg6 14. Nf3 { Development! } 14... b6 15. Rfe1 { Bringing a rook into the game. } (15. Rae1 { Why not Rae1? It cramps the other rook's movement possibilities. } { [%csl Bf1] }) 15... Na5 16. Qc2 (16. Ne5 Nxb3 17. Nxd7 Nxd7 18. axb3 { This is okay, but white is wasting an opportunity to build a kingside attack. }) 16... Nc4 { A nice hook for an attack by white. } { [%csl Bg6] } 17. h4 { [%csl Gh5][%cal Gh4h5] } 17... Rac8 18. Ng5 { Further preparing h5 by building up pressure on the kingside. } { [%csl Rh7,Gh5][%cal Rg5h7,Rc2h7,Gh4h5] } 18... Bd6 { [%csl Rf4][%cal Rd6f4] } 19. Bxd6 Qxd6 20. h5 { Ready to execute the attack. } 20... Qf4 { White's attack at this point is stalled. What should white do? } { [%csl Rg5][%cal Rf4g5] } 21. Nh3 { No need to sac the knight, calmly retreating the knight with tempo. } (21. Nxe6 { Also fails. } 21... fxe6 22. Qxg6 Qh6) (21. hxg6) (21. Nxf7 { This is doomed to failure because... } 21... Rxf7 22. Qxg6 { ... only the queen is involved in the attack and black has 3 pieces ready to defend. } { [%csl Bg6,Gf4,Gf6,Gf7] } (22. hxg6 { Also fails because... } 22... Re7 { ... black defends the pawn and prevents white from fully involving the rook in the attack. } { [%csl Ge6][%cal Ge7e6] })) 21... Qh6 { Note that black's f pawn is overloaded needing to defend both e6 and g6. } { [%csl Bf7][%cal Gf7e6,Gf7g6] } 22. hxg6 fxg6 { The overloaded pawn now hanging another pawn. } { [%csl Re6][%cal Re1e6] } (22... Qxg6 { This was the more accurate move for black. } 23. Qxg6 fxg6 24. Re2 (24. Rxe6 { This does not win a pawn for free because b2 is hanging. } { [%csl Rb2][%cal Rc4b2] }) (24. b3 { Unhanging the pawn, but... } 24... Na3 { ... severely weakening the c3 square allowing black to counterattack. } { [%csl Rc3][%cal Rc8c3] }) 24... Rce8 25. Rae1 { This is the best line for white. White crashes through and wins the pawn on e6 with Ng5. } { [%csl Re6,Gg5][%cal Gh3g5,Rg5e6] }) 23. Rxe6 Rfe8 24. Rae1 { Keeping control of the e file. } { [%csl Re8][%cal Re1e8] } 24... Kf7 25. Qe2 { Continuing to control the e file. } { [%csl Re8][%cal Re1e8] } (25. Rxe8 { Being up a pawn, trading for white is also okay as it does not introduce any weaknesses into white's position. } 25... Rxe8 26. Rxe8 Kxe8) 25... Nd6 { The knight would like to be on g5. } { [%csl Gg5][%cal Gh3g5] } 26. f4 { Preparing the Ng5 outpost. } { [%csl Gg5][%cal Gh3g5] } 26... Nde4 27. Nxe4 Nxe4 28. Ng5+ Kg8 (28... Nxg5 { Allowing white to fully cash in on control of the e file. } { [%csl Re8][%cal Re1e8] } 29. Rxe8 Rxe8 30. Qxe8+) 29. Qg4 { Unpinning the queen, allowing Nxe4 and black will be down a pawn in the exchange. } { [%csl Re4,Ge6][%cal Gg4e6,Rg5e4] } 1-0


