{ THEMES and Things to Look For
- Developing pieces to squares that are immediately not helpful, but may become helpful (e.g. xraying) can be thought of as stored "potential energy". As a position unwinds or opens up, that potential energy can be unleashed as kinetic energy onto the opponent.
- The attacker on one side of the board prefers closed positions in the middle so all resources can be directed into the attack.

Stream footage here: https://youtu.be/woqgGKERnps?t=1111
Game played here: https://www.chess.com/live/game/5860682705 }
1. e4 { [%clk 0:05:00] } 1... g6 { Black plays the modern. } { [%clk 0:04:58] } 2. d4 { [%clk 0:04:59] } 2... Bg7 { [%clk 0:04:57] } 3. Nc3 { White's plan is to occupy the center. } { [%clk 0:04:58] } 3... d6 { [%clk 0:04:56] } 4. Be3 { Oversupporting the pawn and building a strong, supported center. } { [%csl Gd4][%cal Ge3d4] [%clk 0:04:39] } 4... Nf6 { [%clk 0:04:56] } 5. f3 { More support. Preventing Ng4. } { [%csl Ge4,Gg4][%cal Gf3e4,Gf6g4] [%clk 0:04:13] } (5. Qd2 Ng4 { Playing Qd2 immediately allows Ng4 which is poised to slow white's development and potentially damage the pawn structure. }) (5. h3 { h3 also stops Ng4, but does not support the center pawn. } { [%csl Gg4,Be4][%cal Gf6g4] }) 5... O-O { [%clk 0:04:46] } 6. Qd2 { [%clk 0:04:11] } 6... Nbd7 { [%clk 0:04:43] } 7. Bh6 { Looking to trade for black's strongest piece. } { [%csl Rg7][%cal Rh6g7] [%clk 0:03:59] } 7... c5 { Black correctly striking in the center. } { [%csl Rd4][%cal Rc5d4] [%clk 0:04:41] } 8. d5 { Closing the center. Black's bishop has more potential influence now, but soon will not as white plans on trading. } { [%csl Rc3][%cal Rg7c3] [%clk 0:03:27] } 8... a6 { [%clk 0:04:38] } 9. h4 { Before trading the bishops, white can start building the attack on the kingside. } { [%csl Bg6] [%clk 0:02:59] } 9... b5 { [%clk 0:04:29] } 10. h5 { [%clk 0:02:58] } 10... b4 { [%clk 0:04:26] } (10... Nxh5?? { This blunders into forced mate. } 11. Rxh5 gxh5 12. Qg5) 11. Bxg7 { [%clk 0:02:51] } 11... Kxg7 { [%clk 0:04:23] } (11... bxc3 { Not retaking on g7 gives white a massive attack. } 12. Qh6) 12. hxg6 { Sacrificing the knight to open up the h file. } { [%csl Rh6][%cal Rh1h6,Rd2h6] [%clk 0:02:24] } 12... fxg6 { [%clk 0:04:21] } (12... bxc3 { [%csl Rd2][%cal Rc3d2] } 13. Qh6+ Kh8 (13... Kg8 14. gxh7+? (14. g7 { No mate because the knight defends. How can white apply pressure to the knight? } { [%csl Rh7][%cal Rh1h7,Gf6h7] } 14... Re8 { Below are a bunch of ideas that Danya presents. As it turns out, his move is a blunder since white's queenside counterplay is too fast for white to mount an attack on the kingside. The only move for white in this position is b3 to stop the counterplay. } 15. f4? { e5 doesn't work because e5 is defended twice by black. } { [%csl Ge5][%cal Ge4e5,Rd6e5,Rd7e5] } (15. Nh3 { This is an idea that Danya presents as correct, but the engine doesn't like this because it is too slow, black has an attack on the queenside that is faster. } { [%cal Gh3g5,Rg5h7] } 15... cxb2 16. Rb1 Qa5+ 17. c3 { Black's queenside attack is now overwhelming. }) (15. b3 $10 { White is forced to stop black's counterplay on the queenside. Stockfish says this position is equal. }) (15. g4 { [%csl Gg5][%cal Gg4g5] } 15... Rb8 16. g5 Ra8 17. gxf6 Nxf6 { This idea is both too slow and black just replaces the knight. })) 14... Kh8) 14. g7+ { [%csl Rh8,Rf8][%cal Rg7f8,Rg7h8] }) 13. Qh6+ { [%clk 0:02:22] } 13... Kg8 { [%clk 0:04:20] } 14. Nd1 { [%csl Ge3][%cal Gd1e3] [%clk 0:02:08] } (14. Nce2 { Worse because it blocks the bishop. }) 14... Rf7 { [%clk 0:04:18] } 15. Ne3 { [%clk 0:02:03] } 15... Rg7 { How can we bring in more pieces to the attack? The knight has a nice square it can occupy to aid in the attack. } { [%csl Rh7][%cal Gg1h3,Gh3g5,Rg5h7] [%clk 0:04:16] } 16. Nh3 { [%clk 0:01:38] } 16... Qf8 { [%clk 0:04:10] } 17. Ng5 { [%clk 0:01:37] } 17... Ne5 { [%clk 0:04:09] } 18. O-O-O { [%clk 0:01:30] } 18... Nf7 { [%clk 0:04:01] } 19. Nxf7 { White forced to trade here. } { [%clk 0:01:26] } 19... Qxf7 { [%clk 0:04:00] } 20. Bc4 { Bringing in the bishop to xray the queen and king. Ideas of opening up the diagonal. } { [%csl Rf7,Rg8,Ge5][%cal Rc4g8,Ge4e5] [%clk 0:01:11] } 20... Nh5 { [%clk 0:03:51] } 21. Rh4 { Ideas of tripling up on the h file. } { [%csl Gh1,Rh8][%cal Gd1h1,Rh1h8] [%clk 0:00:44] } 21... Qf6 { [%clk 0:03:44] } 22. Rdh1 { [%clk 0:00:43] } 22... Bd7 { [%clk 0:03:24] } 23. f4 { Supporting e5. } { [%csl Ge5][%cal Ge4e5] [%clk 0:00:41] } 23... Rf8 { [%clk 0:03:19] } 24. e5 { [%csl Rf6,Rd6][%cal Re5f6,Re5d6] [%clk 0:00:38] } 24... dxe5 { [%clk 0:03:03] } 25. d6+ { [%clk 0:00:37] } 25... e6 { [%clk 0:03:01] } 26. Ng4 { [%clk 0:00:30] } (26. f5 { This is a more accurate move that Danya points out. Black is unable to take exf5 due to the pin and taking the other way loses the knight. } { [%csl Re6][%cal Rc4g8] } 26... gxf5 27. Rxh5) 26... Qxf4+ { [%clk 0:02:58] } 27. Qxf4 { [%clk 0:00:29] } 27... exf4 { [%clk 0:02:56] } 28. Ne5 { [%clk 0:00:29] } 28... Ng3 { [%clk 0:02:46] } 29. Nxd7 { [%clk 0:00:27] } 29... Nxh1 { [%clk 0:02:42] } 30. Bxe6+ { [%clk 0:00:25] } 30... Kh8 { [%clk 0:02:40] } 31. Nxf8 { [%clk 0:00:24] } 31... Ng3 { [%clk 0:02:32] } 32. d7 { [%clk 0:00:23] } 32... Rxd7 { [%clk 0:02:24] } 33. Bxd7 { [%clk 0:00:22] } 33... Kg7 { [%clk 0:02:22] } 34. Nxh7 { [%clk 0:00:21] } 34... g5 { [%clk 0:02:18] } 35. Nxg5 { [%clk 0:00:20] } 35... Kg6 { [%clk 0:02:17] } 36. Nh3 { [%clk 0:00:20] } 36... Kg7 { [%clk 0:02:11] } 37. Nxf4 { [%clk 0:00:10] } 37... Kf6 { [%clk 0:02:09] } 38. Kd2 { [%clk 0:00:10] } 38... Ke5 { [%clk 0:02:06] } 39. Rg4 { [%clk 0:00:10] } 39... Ne4+ { [%clk 0:02:05] } 40. Ke3 { [%clk 0:00:10] } 40... Nd6 { [%clk 0:02:02] } 41. Nd3+ { [%clk 0:00:10] } 41... Kd5 { [%clk 0:01:57] } 42. Rg5+ { [%clk 0:00:09] } 42... Nf5+ { [%clk 0:01:53] } 43. Rxf5+ { [%clk 0:00:09] } 43... Kd6 { [%clk 0:01:52] } 44. Rxc5 { [%clk 0:00:09] } 44... Kxd7 { [%clk 0:01:50] } 45. Nxb4 { [%clk 0:00:08] } 45... a5 { [%clk 0:01:48] } 46. Rxa5 { [%clk 0:00:08] } 46... Kc7 { [%clk 0:01:47] } 47. g4 { [%clk 0:00:08] } 47... Kd7 { [%clk 0:01:45] } 48. Rd5+ { [%clk 0:00:08] } 48... Ke7 { [%clk 0:01:44] } 49. g5 { [%clk 0:00:08] } 49... Kf7 { [%clk 0:01:43] } 50. Kf4 { [%clk 0:00:08] } 50... Kg6 { [%clk 0:01:42] } 51. Rd6+ { [%clk 0:00:07] } 51... Kf7 { [%clk 0:01:41] } 52. Kf5 { [%clk 0:00:07] } 52... Ke7 { [%clk 0:01:40] } 53. Re6+ { [%clk 0:00:06] } 53... Kf7 { [%clk 0:01:38] } 54. g6+ { [%clk 0:00:06] } 54... Kg7 { [%clk 0:01:37] } 55. Re7+ { [