1. e4 { [%clk 0:05:00] } 1... c5 { [%clk 0:05:00] } 2. b4 { Start of the Wing gambit. } { [%clk 0:04:58] } 2... cxb4 { [%clk 0:04:53] } 3. Bc4? { [%clk 0:04:57] } (3. a3 { This is the correct line. }) 3... Nf6 { Attacking the hanging pawn. } { [%csl Re4][%cal Rf6e4] [%clk 0:04:30] } 4. e5 { [%clk 0:04:52] } 4... d5 { [%csl Rc4][%cal Rd5c4] [%clk 0:04:19] } 5. exf6 { [%clk 0:04:43] } (5. Bb5+ Bd7 6. Bxd7+ Nfxd7 { Black up a pawn with no compensation for white. }) 5... dxc4 { Black wins a pawn and has control of the queenside. } { [%csl Bb4,Bc4] [%clk 0:04:05] } 6. Qe2 { [%csl Rc4][%cal Re2c4] [%clk 0:04:29] } 6... Be6 { Defending and developing. An exception to the rule of not playing Be6 because it blocks the development of the dark squared bishop. however, in this case, white's pawn on f6 provides black with a solution to that problem. } { [%csl Gc4][%cal Ge6c4] [%clk 0:03:46] } 7. Bb2 { [%clk 0:04:08] } 7... exf6 { White allowing the diagonal to be opened quite nicely. } { [%cal Gf8c5] [%clk 0:03:44] } 8. c3 { [%clk 0:03:56] } 8... a5 { [%csl Gb4][%cal Ga5b4] [%clk 0:03:39] } 9. Nf3 { White's dark squared bishop is stuck. } { [%cal Gb2f6] [%clk 0:03:40] } 9... Nc6 { [%clk 0:03:37] } 10. O-O { [%clk 0:03:36] } 10... Bc5 { [%clk 0:03:36] } 11. Qe4 { [%clk 0:03:16] } 11... O-O { [%clk 0:03:29] } 12. cxb4 { [%clk 0:03:10] } 12... axb4 { [%clk 0:03:27] } 13. a3 { [%clk 0:03:00] } 13... bxa3 { [%clk 0:03:22] } 14. Nxa3 { Black has ideas of trading queens since black is up pawns and can play a winning endgame. } { [%clk 0:02:58] } 14... Qd3 { Forcing the trade because the knight is under attack. } { [%csl Ra3][%cal Rc5a3,Rd3a3] [%clk 0:02:47] } (14... Qd5 { Also a fine move to offer the trade, but is not forcing. }) 15. Qxd3 { [%clk 0:02:25] } 15... cxd3 { [%clk 0:02:44] } 16. Nb5 { Notice the open a file, how can black win control over it? Black would like a the knight on c2. However... } { [%cal Ra8a1,Gc6b4,Gb4c2,Rc2a1] [%clk 0:02:19] } 16... Bb6 { Prophylactic move preventing white from playing Nc7. } { [%csl Gc7][%cal Gb6c7,Gb5c7] [%clk 0:01:30] } (16... Nb4 17. Nc7 { ... playing it immediately allows white to execute on the same idea faster. } { [%csl Ra8][%cal Rc7a8] }) 17. Nd6 { [%clk 0:02:06] } 17... Nb4 { Sacrificing the pawn because the a file is more important. } { [%csl Rb7][%cal Rd6b7] [%clk 0:01:17] } 18. Ne4 { [%clk 0:01:52] } 18... Nc2 { [%clk 0:01:16] } 19. Rxa8 { [%clk 0:01:43] } 19... Rxa8 { [%clk 0:01:16] } 20. Ne1 { [%clk 0:01:37] } 20... Ra2 { The pawn is not blundered because... } { [%clk 0:01:11] } 21. Nxd3? { [%clk 0:01:35] } 21... Bc4 { Pinning the hanging knight and attacking the piece it is defending. Note how important the rook on the a-file is. } { [%csl Rf1,Rb2,Ba2][%cal Rc4f1,Ra2b2] [%clk 0:00:57] } 22. Nc3 { [%clk 0:01:20] } 22... Ra8 { [%clk 0:00:54] } 23. Ne1 { [%clk 0:01:03] } 23... Bxf1 { [%clk 0:00:52] } 24. Nxc2 { [%clk 0:01:02] } 24... Bc4 { [%clk 0:00:50] } 25. d4 { [%clk 0:00:58] } 25... Rd8 { [%clk 0:00:47] } 26. g3 { [%clk 0:00:55] } 26... Bxd4 { [%clk 0:00:46] } 27. Nxd4 { [%clk 0:00:53] } 27... Rxd4 { [%clk 0:00:46] } 28. Nb5 { How to win the endgame? Find the passed pawn and attempt to promote it. } { [%csl Bb7] [%clk 0:00:49] } 28... Rd1+ { [%clk 0:00:42] } 29. Kg2 { [%clk 0:00:48] } 29... Bxb5 { 0-1 Black wins. } { [%clk 0:00:42] } 0-1


