{ Old Benoni had a farm d4 c4 d5

THEMES and Things to Look For
- The player with better development benefits the most from open positions. If your pieces are better developed, look for opportunities to open the board up!
- Undermining and removing pieces defending a square so that we may develop a piece to that square.

Stream footage here: https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=FPI9J8_LmJQ
Game played here: https://www.chess.com/live/game/5913408079 }
1. d4 { [%c_effect d4;square;d4;type;Book] [%clk 0:05:00] } 1... c5 { Start of the Benoni } { [%clk 0:04:59] } 2. d5 { Controlling the center and preventing Nc6. } { [%csl Gc6][%cal Gb8c6] [%clk 0:04:52] } (2. dxc5 { Not a free pawn. } 2... Qa5+ { Could win it back immediately here, but this exposes the queen. } (2... e6 { This is the better line for black, to win the pawns while developing. } { [%csl Rc5][%cal Rf8c5] } 3. b4 a5 4. c3 axb4 5. cxb4 b6 6. cxb6 Bxb4+ $15 { Black is slightly better here with more center control and will win the pawn back. } { [%csl Rb6][%cal Rd8b6] })) 2... b5 { How does white respond to black playing on the flanks? } { [%clk 0:04:55] } 3. e4 { In the center. } { [%clk 0:04:29] } 3... a6 { White would like this square for the bishop. } { [%csl Bc4] [%clk 0:04:53] } 4. a4 { Ideas of freeing up the c4 square by undermining black's control over the square.
See this part of the video where Danya goes over the concept of freeing up a square https://youtu.be/FPI9J8_LmJQ?t=760. } { [%csl Bc4][%cal Gb5c4] [%clk 0:04:06] } 4... b4 { [%clk 0:04:49] } 5. Bc4 { [%clk 0:04:04] } 5... d6 { [%clk 0:04:44] } 6. Nf3 { [%clk 0:04:01] } 6... g6 { [%clk 0:04:42] } 7. O-O { [%clk 0:03:59] } 7... Bg7 { [%clk 0:04:36] } 8. a5 { Stopping black from playing a5 themselves. Note that black will have a difficult time removing this pawn. } { [%csl Ga5][%cal Ga1a5] [%clk 0:03:48] } 8... Nd7 { [%clk 0:04:31] } 9. c3 { Preparing the development of the bishop so white doesn't leave the b2 pawn hanging. } { [%cal Gc1g5,Rg7c3] [%clk 0:03:29] } 9... Ngf6 { [%csl Re4][%cal Rf6e4] [%clk 0:04:09] } (9... bxc3 10. Nxc3 { If black takes, white gets to develop and re-take. }) 10. Re1 { Defending the pawn. } { [%csl Ge4][%cal Ge1e4,Rf6e4] [%clk 0:03:15] } (10. Nbd2 { This would block the bishop. } { [%cal Gc1d2] }) 10... O-O { [%clk 0:04:00] } 11. Bg5 { [%clk 0:03:14] } 11... h6 { [%clk 0:03:50] } 12. Bh4 { [%clk 0:03:12] } 12... g5 { [%clk 0:03:46] } 13. Bg3 { [%clk 0:03:11] } (13. Nxg5?? { No need to sac here. White doesn't have enough pieces involved in the attack and black can quickly develop and defend. } 13... hxg5 14. Bxg5 Ne5 { [%csl Gf5,Rc4][%cal Gc8f5,Re5c4] }) 13... Ne8 { Stopping white's knight from developing to d2. } { [%csl Rc3][%cal Rg7c3,Rb4c3,Gb1c3,Gb2c3] [%clk 0:03:39] } 14. Qc2 { Defending the pawn and preparing Nd2. } { [%csl Gc3,Gd2][%cal Gc2c3,Gb2c3,Rg7c3,Rb4c3,Gb1d2] [%clk 0:02:46] } (14. Nbd2? bxc3 15. bxc3 Bxc3 { Blundering a pawn. }) 14... f6 { [%clk 0:03:34] } 15. Nbd2 { [%clk 0:02:38] } 15... Nc7 { What does white do when black is playing passively? } { [%clk 0:03:30] } 16. cxb4 { By opening up the position! } { [%clk 0:02:14] } 16... cxb4 { [%clk 0:03:15] } 17. Nd4 { Now the knight has access to some beautiful outposts. } { [%csl Gc6,Gf5][%cal Gd4c6,Gd4f5] [%clk 0:02:09] } 17... Nc5 { [%clk 0:02:51] } 18. Nc6 { [%csl Bc5] [%clk 0:02:06] } 18... Qe8 { Black's only active piece. How can white remove it? } { [%clk 0:02:48] } 19. Nb3 { [%csl Rc5][%cal Rb3c5] [%clk 0:01:57] } (19. Nxb4 { Why did white not play Nxb4? The knight occupies too good an outpost for it to be distracted by the pawn. }) 19... Nxb3 { [%clk 0:02:21] } 20. Bxb3 { Leaving the queen to control the open c-file. } { [%cal Rc2c8] [%clk 0:01:52] } 20... h5 { Black looking to trap the bishop. } { [%csl Rg3][%cal Gh5h4,Rh4g3] [%clk 0:02:13] } 21. h3 { Opening a space for the bishop to retreat to. } { [%csl Gh2][%cal Gg3h2] [%clk 0:01:47] } 21... h4 { [%clk 0:02:05] } 22. Bh2 { [%clk 0:01:46] } 22... Nb5 { [%clk 0:02:00] } (22... e6 { A seemingly reasonable move to undermine the pawn supporting the knight, but... } { [%csl Rd5][%cal Re6d5] } 23. Bxd6 { ... it allows white to win a pawn and activate the bishop. } { [%csl Rf8,Rc7][%cal Rd6f8,Rd6c7] }) 23. Rac1 { Activating the rook. } { [%csl Rc8][%cal Rc1c8] [%clk 0:01:33] } 23... Bd7 { [%clk 0:01:42] } 24. Qd3 { [%clk 0:01:30] } 24... Bxc6 { [%clk 0:01:31] } 25. dxc6+ { Opening a nice square for the bishop to aid in an attack and to protect the pawn's advance into a possible promotion. } { [%csl Ge6][%cal Gb3e6,Re6c8,Re6g8,Gc6c7,Gc7c8] [%clk 0:01:20] } (25. Rxc6 { This is also okay with ideas of doubling rooks on the open file, but dxc6+ opens up the position in a way that is even more beneficial and allows white to fully activate the light squared bishop. } { [%cal Ge1c1,Rc1c8] }) 25... Kh8 { [%clk 0:01:23] } 26. Be6 { [%clk 0:01:19] } 26... Nc7 { [%clk 0:01:09] } 27. Bd7 { [%clk 0:01:18] } 27... Qg6 { [%clk 0:00:58] } 28. f4 { Attempting to open up more lines of attack onto the opponent's king. Yes, this does also open up white's king to attacks, but white is better positioned to take advantage of a more open position. } { [%cal Rg6g2] [%clk 0:00:58] } 28... gxf4 { [%clk 0:00:36] } 29. Bxf4 { [%clk 0:00:57] } 29... Bh6 { [%clk 0:00:26] } 30. Rf1 { Not trading bishops just yet. White would like to trade on their own terms with a chance to attack a hanging pawn. } { [%csl Gf4,Rh4][%cal Gf1f4,Rf4h4] [%clk 0:00:49] } 30... Bxf4 { [%clk 0:00:17] } 31. Rxf4 { [%clk 0:00:48] } 31... d5 { [%csl Rd3][%cal Rg6d3] [%clk 0:00:14] } 32. Rxh4+ { Black resigns here } { [%clk 0:00:31] } 32... Kg7 33. Rg4 { Pin is incoming. } { [%csl Rg7][%cal Rg4g7] } 1-0


