[ChapterName "Game 119: Dutch Defense (White, ELO 1737)"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/dSNQO9Uq/peOLI38S"]
[Annotator "https://lichess.org/@/rudisco"]

{ THEMES and Things to Look For
- Some moves will not directly prepare another move, but will enable it on future moves.
- For "system" openings such as the London, one must be careful to not dogmatically play those moves in all situations. In some situations, the opponent will have reacted in such a way that warrants deviation.
- Some ideas will demand multiple moves to realize which may involve shuffling pieces around to make room for a key move that will enable the entire idea.
- When a pawn is pushed, look for what has been opened up or left undefended.
- A good question to ask for whether or not you can move kingside pawns after castling kingside is "does my opponent have enough pieces to mount a counterattack?"

Stream footage here: https://youtu.be/FPI9J8_LmJQ?t=1149
Game played here: https://www.chess.com/live/game/5913529555 }
1. d4 { [%c_effect d4;square;d4;type;Book] [%clk 0:04:59] } 1... d6 { [%clk 0:05:00] } 2. Bf4 { [%clk 0:04:55] } 2... f5 { Transposing into the Dutch Defense. } { [%clk 0:04:59] } 3. e3 { White continues with the London plan. } { [%clk 0:04:43] } 3... Nf6 { [%clk 0:04:58] } 4. Nf3 { [%clk 0:04:39] } 4... Nbd7 { The Dutch weakens this diagonal for black. How can white take advantage of this and potentially disrupt kingside castling? } { [%cal Ba2g8] [%clk 0:04:57] } 5. Bc4 { Normally in the London white plays Bd3 or Be2, however, this is a situation where Black has played a position with weaknesses that can be exploited by a better move. } { [%csl Rg8,Be2,Bd3][%cal Rc4g8] [%clk 0:04:08] } 5... Nb6 { [%clk 0:04:52] } (5... d5 { d5 succeeds in (temporarily) denying the bishop access to the diagonal, but severely weakens the e5 square which white can plant a knight outpost on. } { [%csl Ge5][%cal Gf3e5] } 6. Bb3 { White can also open the diagonal back up with ideas of c4. } { [%csl Gc4,Rd5][%cal Gc2c4,Rc4d5] }) 6. Bb3 { [%clk 0:04:06] } 6... e6 { White has Ng5 to attack the pawn, but... } { [%csl Gg5,Re6][%cal Gf3g5,Rg5e6] [%clk 0:04:51] } 7. O-O { ... no need to do it yet, white can continue developing. } { [%clk 0:03:59] } 7... Be7 { [%clk 0:04:49] } 8. h3 { Stopping black's knight from occupying g4 and to prevent black from trading for the bishop. } { [%csl Gg4,Gh5,Rf4][%cal Gf6g4,Gf6h5,Rh5f4] [%clk 0:03:58] } 8... h6 { [%clk 0:04:48] } (8... Nh5 { This move is less effective, because white can simply... } { [%csl Rf4][%cal Rh5f4] } 9. Bh2 { ... retreat the bishop into a safe space. }) 9. c4 { Blocking the diagonal for now, but with ideas of making further progress into the queenside. } { [%csl Gc5][%cal Gc4c5,Rb3g8] [%clk 0:03:51] } 9... O-O { [%clk 0:03:50] } 10. Nc3 { [%clk 0:03:48] } 10... a6 { How can c5 be prepared? } { [%csl Gc5][%cal Gc4c5] [%clk 0:03:36] } 11. Rc1 { This doesn't immediately prepare c5, but does strengthen the move when it is played! } { [%cal Gc1c6] [%clk 0:03:29] } 11... Bd7 { It would be nice to have a pawn on b4, but the bishop is blocking the way. } { [%csl Gb4,Gc5][%cal Gb2b4,Gc4c5] [%clk 0:03:35] } 12. a3 { Preparing Ba2. Key concept here of vacating a square so we can make room for another piece. } { [%csl Ga2][%cal Gb3a2] [%clk 0:02:51] } 12... Qe8 { [%clk 0:03:12] } 13. Ba2 { [%clk 0:02:50] } 13... Nc8 { [%clk 0:03:07] } 14. b4 { Further preparing c5. } { [%csl Gc5][%cal Gc4c5] [%clk 0:02:48] } 14... b5 { Black tries to employ the idea from the last game, attacking the pawn guarding the d5 square to carve it out for an outpost. However, this doesn't work as well because white is able to retain control over the d5 square even without the pawn's support. } { [%csl Rc4,Gd5][%cal Rb5c4,Gc4d5,Gf6d5,Ga2d5,Gc3d5] [%clk 0:03:02] } 15. c5 { Finally, c5 after multiple moves of preparation. } { [%clk 0:02:46] } 15... Bc6 { Hanging the pawn. } { [%csl Re6][%cal Ra2e6] [%clk 0:02:49] } (15... d5 16. Bxc7 { When a pawn is pushed, look for what has been opened up or left undefended. } { [%csl Ge5][%cal Gf3e5] }) 16. Bxe6+ { [%csl Rf5][%cal Re6f5] [%clk 0:02:28] } 16... Kh8 { [%clk 0:02:48] } 17. Bxf5 { [%clk 0:02:25] } 17... Bxf3? { When a pawn is pushed, look for what has been opened up or left undefended. } { [%clk 0:02:46] } 18. Qxf3 { Black allowing white to retake and develop with tempo. } { [%csl Ra8][%cal Rf3a8] [%clk 0:02:23] } 18... Na7 { Note that black lacks a light squared bishop which means white can more easily dominate those squares. } { [%csl Bf5] [%clk 0:02:38] } 19. Bb1 { Ideas of preparing Qe4 eventually and building a strong battery. } { [%csl Ge4,Rh7][%cal Gf3e4,Rb1h7,Gf6e4] [%clk 0:01:42] } 19... dxc5 { [%clk 0:02:35] } 20. bxc5 { [%clk 0:01:42] } 20... c6 { Black blocking the development of their knight. } { [%csl Gc6][%cal Ga7c6] [%clk 0:02:29] } 21. Qe2 { [%clk 0:01:37] } 21... g5 { [%clk 0:02:08] } 22. Be5 { [%clk 0:01:35] } 22... Qf7 { [%clk 0:02:06] } 23. Qc2 { Slightly better than Qd3 to prevent any ideas from black trying to xray the queen with the rook. } { [%clk 0:01:30] } (23. Qd3 Rad8 { Qd3 allows this x-ray. } { [%csl Rd3][%cal Rd8d3] }) 23... Rae8 { [%clk 0:02:00] } 24. Ne4 { Attacking twice. } { [%csl Rf6][%cal Re4f6,Re5f6] [%clk 0:01:15] } 24... Qg7 { Black avoids the loss of material for now. } { [%csl Gf6][%cal Gf8f6,Gg7f6,Ge7f6] [%clk 0:01:51] } (24... Nc8 { If black plays some random move... } 25. Nxf6 Bxf6 26. Bxf6+ { The queen is overloaded, black loses the exchange or immediately loses the game. } { [%csl Rh7][%cal Gf7h7,Rf7f6,Rb1h7] } 26... Qxf6 27. Qh7# { Black will either lose the exchange or be checkmated since the queen is overloaded. }) 25. f4 { Ideas of opening more files on the kingside so white can further pressure the knight and better attack black's king. White is not afraid of exposing their king as black's pieces are not active enough to mount a counterattack. } { [%csl Rf6][%cal Rf1f6] [%clk 0:01:00] } 25... Rf7 { [%clk 0:01:37] } (25... g4 26. hxg4 { Black cannot take with the knight as it is pinned. } { [%csl Rg7][%cal Re5g7] } 26... Qxg4 { Leaving h7 undefended. } { [%csl Bh7] } 27. Nxf6 { Removing the other defender of h7. Forcing the loss of material or mate. } { [%csl Rh7][%cal Rb1h7] }) 26. fxg5 { [%cal Rf1f6] [%clk 0:00:59] } 26... hxg5 { [%clk 0:01:35] } 27. Nxf6 { [%clk 0:00:58] } 27... Bxf6 { [%clk 0:01:34] } 28. Rxf6 { Black is now forced to lose material. } { [%clk 0:00:57] } 28... Rxe5 { [%clk 0:01:26] } 29. dxe5 { [%clk 0:00:55] } 29... Re7 { [%clk 0:01:20] } 30. Rcf1 { [%clk 0:00:52] } (30. Rf8+?? Qxf8 31. Qh7+ { Sacing does not work here as the rook continues to guard the square. } { [%csl Rh7][%cal Re7h7] }) 1-0


