{ THEMES and Things to Look For
- When trades occur, they can introduce weaknesses in the opponent's position that may allow tactics.
- Sometimes calculated risks will have to be taken such as weakening your own kingside defenses to reinforce an attack.

Stream footage here: https://youtu.be/n781_V5I0ac?t=1136
Game played here: https://www.chess.com/live/game/5914731665 }
1. d4 { [%clk 0:04:57] } 1... g6 { [%clk 0:04:59] } 2. Bf4 { White plays the London, black meets it with the King's Indian. } { [%clk 0:04:53] } 2... Bg7 { [%clk 0:04:58] } 3. e3 { [%clk 0:04:52] } 3... d6 { [%clk 0:04:56] } 4. Nf3 { Black would like to prepare e5. } { [%csl Ge5][%cal Ge7e5] [%clk 0:04:44] } 4... Nd7 { Stopping a queen trade while adding control onto the e5 square. } { [%csl Rd8,Ge5][%cal Rd1d8,Gd7e5] [%clk 0:04:21] } 5. Nbd2 { [%clk 0:04:32] } 5... e5 { [%clk 0:04:20] } 6. dxe5 { [%clk 0:04:26] } 6... dxe5 { [%clk 0:04:19] } 7. Bg3 { [%clk 0:04:25] } 7... Qe7 { Preparing Ng6 so that the e pawn doesn't hang when black blocks the bishop. } { [%csl Gf6][%cal Gg8f6] [%clk 0:03:46] } 8. c3 { [%clk 0:04:13] } 8... Ngf6 { [%clk 0:03:44] } 9. Bc4 { [%clk 0:03:43] } 9... O-O { [%clk 0:03:41] } 10. O-O { How should black develop the light squared bishop? Moving the d7 knight would hang the e pawn. } { [%csl Bc8] [%clk 0:03:40] } 10... b6 { Fianchetto! } { [%clk 0:03:26] } 11. Re1 { [%clk 0:03:07] } 11... Bb7 { [%clk 0:03:23] } 12. h3 { [%clk 0:02:47] } 12... Rad8 { Simple developing moves. } { [%clk 0:03:13] } 13. Qc2 { Black would like to be able to move the knight to give black control of the d-file. How can this be prepared? } { [%csl Gc5,Rd2][%cal Gd7c5,Rd8d2] [%clk 0:02:40] } 13... Nh5 { Preparing Nc5 by allowing the bishop to defend the pawn. } { [%csl Ge5][%cal Gg7e5] [%clk 0:02:40] } 14. Bh2 { [%clk 0:02:32] } (14. Bh4 Bf6 15. Bxf6 Qxf6 { In this line, black trades the dark squared bishops and is okay with that trade because it was passive anyway. }) 14... Nc5 { Allowing black's rook to control the open file without losing the e pawn. } { [%csl Rd2,Ge5][%cal Rd8d2,Gg7e5,Ge7e5] [%clk 0:02:39] } 15. Nf1?? { Critical error that allows black to start an overwhelming attack. } { [%clk 0:01:55] } (15. b4 { If white played b4, black has some tactical ideas. } 15... e4 { [%csl Rf3][%cal Re4f3] } 16. Nd4 (16. bxc5 exf3 17. gxf3 Qg5+ { Similar to the actual game, critically weakening the king's defense. }) 16... Nd3 17. Bxd3 exd3 18. Qxd3 c5 { Black wins the knight due to the pawn attack and the pin. } { [%csl Rd4,Rd3][%cal Rc5d4,Rd8d3] }) (15. g4 Nf6 16. Bxe5 { Black appears to give up the pawn, but has a tactical idea... } 16... Rxd2 17. Qxd2 (17. Nxd2 Qxe5 { Winning a knight and bishop for a rook plus an attack on white's king. }) 17... Bxf3 { Winning a knight and bishop for a rook, plus an attack on white's king. }) 15... Bxf3 { [%clk 0:02:20] } 16. gxf3 { Ruining the pawn structure so that black can more effectively attack white's king. } { [%clk 0:01:55] } 16... Qh4 { Forking the pawn and bishop. } { [%csl Rh3,Rc4][%cal Rh4h3,Rh4c4] [%clk 0:01:54] } 17. Be2 { [%clk 0:01:29] } 17... Qxh3 { [%clk 0:01:53] } 18. Ng3 { [%clk 0:01:21] } 18... Nxg3 { White offering a trade, black is up material, why not? } { [%clk 0:01:46] } 19. Bxg3 { [%clk 0:01:15] } 19... f5 { Ideas of opening up the f file so the rook can join the attack. } { [%csl Gf5,Gh5][%cal Gf8f5,Gf5h5] [%clk 0:01:26] } 20. Bf1 { [%clk 0:01:12] } 20... Qh5 { [%clk 0:01:25] } 21. f4 { White stops the idea of the rook lift, but weakens their control over e4. } { [%csl Be4] [%clk 0:00:58] } 21... g5 { This move does weaken black's king, but is a calculated risk. Note that white's pieces are not coordinated into a kingside attack. } { [%csl Bg5,Bf5,Be5] [%clk 0:00:46] } 22. Qe2 { White offering the trade. } { [%clk 0:00:46] } 22... Qg6 { Black rejects the exchange to continue the attack, x-raying the king. } { [%csl Rg1][%cal Rg6g1] [%clk 0:00:42] } 23. fxg5 { [%clk 0:00:37] } 23... Qxg5 { [%clk 0:00:38] } 24. Bg2 { [%clk 0:00:33] } 24... f4 { [%clk 0:00:37] } 25. Qc4+ { [%clk 0:00:28] } 25... Kh8 { [%clk 0:00:35] } 26. exf4 { [%clk 0:00:21] } 26... exf4 { [%clk 0:00:33] } 27. Bh2 { [%clk 0:00:07] } 27... f3 { [%clk 0:00:31] } 28. Qf1 { [%clk 0:00:07] } 28... Rg8 { No need to take the bishop immediately, black can bring in more material to strengthen the position. } { [%clk 0:00:25] } 29. Kh1 { [%clk 0:00:06] } 29... fxg2+ { [%clk 0:00:24] } 30. Qxg2 { [%clk 0:00:05] } 30... Qxg2+ { [%clk 0:00:23] } 31. Kxg2 { [%clk 0:00:05] } 31... Bxc3+ { [%clk 0:00:19] } 32. Kh1 { [%clk 0:00:05] } 32... Bxe1 { [%clk 0:00:18] } 33. Rxe1 { [%clk 0:00:03] } 33... Nd3 { [%clk 0:00:16] } 34. Rg1 { [%clk 0:00:03] } 34... Nxf2# { 0-1 Black wins by checkmate. } { [%clk 0:00:15] } 0-1


