{ THEMES and Things to Look For
- When you've blundered a piece, work step by step to improve your position.
- A useful question to ask in a position is "what is my opponent's strongest piece and how do I get rid of it?". This can involve an even material trade which is positionally advantageous if you're trading a more weakly positioned piece for another more strongly positioned piece.

Stream footage here: https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9Iu56-1zzfI
Game played here: https://www.chess.com/live/game/5914794898 }
1. e4 c5 2. c3 { Start of the Alapin } 2... d6 { Not the best line, allowing white to take control of the center. } { [%csl Gd4][%cal Gd2d4] } 3. d4 Nf6 { Attacking the pawn. } { [%csl Re4][%cal Rf6e4] } 4. Bd3 { Developing and defending. } { [%csl Ge4][%cal Gd3e4] } 4... Nc6 5. Nf3 e6 (5... Bg4 6. d5 Ne5 { Opening up this diagonal. } { [%cal Ba4e8] } 7. Nxe5 Bxd1 8. Bb5+ Qd7 9. Nxd7 Nxd7 10. Bxd7+ Kxd7 11. Kxd1 { White is up a piece. This is similar to a queens gambit setup which Danya shows here: https://youtu.be/9Iu56-1zzfI?t=521 }) 6. O-O Be7 { Options of opening up the center with dxc5 or developing the bishop. } { [%csl Gg5,Rc5][%cal Gc1g5,Rd4c5] } 7. Bg5?! { This is what allows black to win a pawn. } { [%csl Rg5][%cal Re7g5] } (7. dxc5 dxc5 8. Qe2 { Danya in retrospect prefers this line to cement central control and to stop the blundering of the pawn. } { [%csl Gd1,Rd8][%cal Gf1d1,Rd1d8] }) 7... O-O 8. Nbd2 cxd4 9. cxd4 e5 10. d5 { White chooses to keep the center closed to take advantage of the space advantage. } 10... Nb4 11. Be2 { Black has a tactical idea here to win a pawn. } 11... Nfxd5 { [%csl Rg5][%cal Re7g5] } 12. Bxe7 Nxe7 { d file is opened, how can white attack the pawn? } { [%csl Rd8,Bd6][%cal Rd1d8] } 13. Nc4 { [%csl Rd6][%cal Rc4d6,Rd1d6] } 13... Be6 (13... d5 14. exd5 Nexd5 { [%csl Re5][%cal Rc4e5] } 15. Ncxe5 { The other pawn hangs in this line allowing white to win a pawn back. }) 14. Nxd6 { Black allows white to win back the pawn. } 14... Qc7 { White has options to make a move with tempo, attacking black's queen. Is Rc1 or Nb5 better? } { [%csl Gc1,Gb5,Rc7][%cal Ga1c1,Gd6b5,Rb5c7] } 15. Nb5 { The knight is the vulnerable piece as it was only defended by the queen so getting it out of danger is a priority. } { [%csl Rc7][%cal Rb5c7] } 15... Qb6 { Hanging the pawn. } { [%csl Re5][%cal Rf3e5] } 16. Nxe5 a6 (16... Nxa2 { This loses material for black. Note that the bishop is the only piece defending the knight which means... } { [%csl Ga2][%cal Ge6a2] } 17. Nd7 { ... white can overload the bishop with this fork ... } { [%csl Rb6,Rf8][%cal Rd7b6,Rd7f8] } 17... Bxd7 18. Qxd7 { ... and both knights are hanging and both cannot be defended. } { [%csl Ra2,Re7][%cal Ra1a2,Rd7e7] }) 17. Nc3 Rad8 { The b4 knight is black's most active and strongest piece. How can white get rid of it? } { [%csl Bb4] } 18. Qa4 { Applying pressure to the knight. } { [%csl Rb4][%cal Ra4b4] } (18. Qe1 Nc2 { This walks into a fork. } { [%csl Re1,Ra1][%cal Rc2a1,Rc2e1] }) 18... Qc5 { Black now offering the possibility of trading knights. } { [%csl Re5,Rb4][%cal Rc5e5,Ra4b4] } 19. a3 { Forcing black to trade knights. } { [%csl Re5,Rb4][%cal Rc5e5,Ra4b4] } 19... Qxe5 20. Qxb4 b5?? { Blundering the knight. } { [%csl Re7][%cal Rb4e7] } (20... Qc7 { Should have gone Qc7 to defend the knight. } { [%csl Ge7][%cal Gc7e7] } 21. Rfc1 { White would look to x-ray the queen. } { [%csl Rc7][%cal Rc1c7] }) 21. Qxe7 Rd2 22. Rad1 Rxb2?? 23. Rd8 { Allowing white to threaten mate or loss of material for black. } { [%cal Rd8f8,Re7f8] } 23... h6 (23... Rxd8 24. Qxd8# { Mate! }) 24. Rxf8+ Kh7 25. Rxf7 Qxc3 26. Qxe6 Rxe2 27. Qf5+ 1-0


