{ THEMES and Things to Look For
- Just because an idea isn't possible in one position does not mean an opponent can't enable it by making some minor adjustments. Always be on the look out to see if a seemingly non-threat is now real.
- At some point chess is just about concrete calculation.

Stream footage here: https://youtu.be/9Iu56-1zzfI?t=735
Game played here: https://www.chess.com/live/game/5922322547 }
1. e4 { [%clk 0:05:00] } 1... c6 { Caro-Kann? } { [%clk 0:04:59] } 2. d4 { [%clk 0:04:55] } 2... d6 { Transposes into a weird Pirc, Caro-Kann } { [%clk 0:04:58] } 3. Nc3 { [%clk 0:04:44] } 3... Nd7 { [%clk 0:04:57] } 4. f4 { Taking control of the center. Yes, the kingside is weakened, but black has no concrete threats due to their passive development. } { [%clk 0:04:32] } 4... Qc7 { [%clk 0:04:51] } 5. Nf3 { Reinforcing the pawns in the center. } { [%clk 0:04:31] } 5... e5 { Black strikes in the center. } { [%csl Rf4,Rd4][%cal Re5d4,Re5f4] [%clk 0:04:45] } 6. Bc4 { White continues to develop while black's position remains passive. } { [%csl Rf7][%cal Rc4f7] [%clk 0:04:09] } 6... h6 { [%clk 0:04:36] } 7. O-O { White has a nice setup on the f file. } { [%cal Rf1f8] [%clk 0:04:07] } 7... Ngf6 { [%clk 0:04:35] } 8. fxe5 { Opening up the f file. } { [%clk 0:03:23] } 8... dxe5 { [%clk 0:04:28] } 9. d5 { Forcing black's knight and queen to be blocked by black's pawn. } { [%csl Ge5][%cal Gd7e5,Gc7e5] [%clk 0:03:14] } 9... Be7 { [%clk 0:04:16] } (9... cxd5 10. Bxd5 (10. Nxd5 { This hangs the bishop } { [%csl Rc4][%cal Rc7c4] }) 10... Nxd5 11. Nxd5 { A strong outpost for the knight. } { [%csl Bd5] }) (9... c5?? { Allowing white to win a rook by pushing the d pawn and knight. } 10. Nb5 Qb8 11. d6 { [%csl Gc7][%cal Gb5c7] }) 10. dxc6 { [%clk 0:03:00] } 10... bxc6 { [%clk 0:04:13] } 11. Be3 { [%clk 0:02:51] } 11... O-O { Now enabling Ng4 because white no longer has Bxf7 ideas to force the king out, how does white stop this? } { [%csl Gg4,Re3][%cal Gf6g4,Rg4e3] [%clk 0:04:11] } (11... Ng4 { This seems to force the bishop back, but allows a nice tactical idea for white. } { [%csl Re3][%cal Rg4e3] } 12. Bxf7+ { Temporarily sacrificing the bishop and forcing black's king into the open. } 12... Kxf7 13. Nxe5+ { A double check, forcing black's king to move. } { [%csl Rf7][%cal Re5f7,Rf1f7] } 13... Ke8 14. Nxg4 { Winning back the minor piece, putting white up two pawns in the exchange. }) 12. h3 { Simple idea to guarding g4. } { [%csl Gg4][%cal Gh3g4,Gf6g4] [%clk 0:02:33] } 12... Rd8 { [%clk 0:04:03] } 13. Qe1 { [%clk 0:02:26] } 13... Nf8 { [%clk 0:04:00] } 14. Qg3 { White now with a strong attack as the f and g pawns in front of black's king are pinned. Even the e pawn is threatened. } { [%csl Rh6,Rg8,Re5][%cal Rg3g8,Re3h6,Rc4g8,Rg3e5,Rf3e5] [%clk 0:02:15] } 14... Be6 { [%clk 0:03:34] } (14... Ng6 { Winning white the knight as the pawn is pinned. } { [%csl Rg6,Rg8][%cal Rg3g6,Rc4g8] } 15. Qxg6 { [%csl Rg8,Rf7][%cal Rc4g8] }) 15. Bxe6 { [%clk 0:01:53] } (15. Bxh6?? { This doesn't work because... } 15... Nh5 { ... black has Nh5 which defends the g pawn and forces the queen away which removes the pin on the pawn, hanging the h6 bishop. Note the c4 bishop is also hanging. } { [%csl Rg3,Bh6,Rc4,Gg7][%cal Rh5g3,Re6c4,Gh5g7] }) 15... Nxe6 { [%clk 0:03:31] } 16. Qxe5 { A lot of concrete calculation to justify taking with the queen. See the alternatives lines. } { [%clk 0:01:38] } (16. Bxh6 Nh5 17. Qg4 { This results in a draw or white sacrificing the bishop for no compensation. Because both of black's knights are defending the pawn, one can do something else. } { [%csl Gg7][%cal Ge6g7,Gh5g7] } 17... Nf6 18. Qg3 Nh5 19. Qg4 Nf6 20. Qg3 Nh5 { This draws. }) (16. Nxe5 { This does not work for white because... } 16... Bd6 { ... black can pin the knight to the queen and if white tries Rxf6 ... } { [%csl Rg3][%cal Rd6g3] } 17. Rxf6 Bxe5 { ... black takes the knight and forks the queen and rook. } { [%csl Rg3,Rf6][%cal Re5f6,Re5g3] }) 16... Bd6 { [%clk 0:03:11] } 17. Qf5 { [%clk 0:01:37] } 17... Nh7 { [%clk 0:02:53] } 18. e5 { Ideas of trying to control the d file. } { [%csl Rd6,Gd1][%cal Re5d6,Ga1d1] [%clk 0:01:35] } 18... Bc5 { [%clk 0:02:43] } 19. Bxc5 { [%clk 0:01:32] } 19... Nxc5 { [%clk 0:02:41] } 20. Rad1 { [%clk 0:01:31] } 20... Ne6 { Ideas of getting the knight to his juicy square. } { [%csl Gd6][%cal Gc3e4,Ge4d6] [%clk 0:02:30] } 21. Ne4 { [%clk 0:01:17] } 21... Rxd1 { [%clk 0:02:18] } 22. Rxd1 { [%clk 0:01:16] } 22... Qb6+ { Seems like white blunders a pawn here. } { [%clk 0:02:15] } 23. Kh2 { Playing this to stop potential back rank checks. } { [%csl Rb1][%cal Rb6b1] [%clk 0:01:11] } 23... Qxb2 { [%clk 0:02:11] } 24. Rd7 { Getting the rook through before outposting the knight. } { [%csl Gd6][%cal Ge4d6] [%clk 0:01:05] } 24... Rf8 { [%clk 0:02:09] } 25. Nd6 { [%clk 0:01:03] } 25... Neg5 { [%clk 0:01:48] } 26. Nxg5 { [%clk 0:01:00] } 26... Nxg5 { White needs to remove one of the defenders, which one and why? } { [%csl Gf7][%cal Gg5f7,Gf8f7,Gg8f7,Rf5f7,Rd6f7,Rd7f7] [%clk 0:01:39] } 27. h4 { [%clk 0:00:55] } 27... g6 { Black now plays a series of one move threats, however, none of them solve the underlying problem of defending the f pawn. } { [%csl Rf5,Bf7][%cal Rg6f5] [%clk 0:01:20] } 28. Qf6 { [%clk 0:00:51] } 28... Nh7 { [%clk 0:01:13] } 29. Qf4 { [%clk 0:00:51] } 29... g5 { [%clk 0:01:06] } 30. Qf5 { [%clk 0:00:46] } 30... Qxa2 { Attempting to add the queen to the defense but white would like to check on g6, how can the f pawn be removed? } { [%csl Gf7,Rg8,Gg6][%cal Ga2f7,Gf5g6,Rg6g8] [%clk 0:01:00] } 31. Nxf7 { Removing the defender of the g6 square by sacrificing the knight. } { [%csl Gg6][%cal Gf5g6] [%clk 0:00:37] } 31... Rxf7 { [%clk 0:00:58] } 32. Qg6+ { [%clk 0:00:24] } 32... Kf8 { [%clk 0:00:52] } 33. Rd8+ { [%clk 0:00:16] } 33... Ke7 { [%clk 0:00:51] } 34. Qd6# { 1-0 White wins by checkmate. } { [%clk 0:00:12] } 1-0


