{ THEMES and Things to Look For
- The Englund is a highly dubious opening. Editor's note: if you are interested in a learning a fairly comprehensive refutation, this Chessable course is excellent (and cost effective!) https://www.chessable.com/ending-the-englund-gambit/course/21854/
- Queens can be trapped in the middle game, especially when one is on the rim.
- Gambit players hate endgames because they will likely be down in material. Playing solidly to get to an endgame against gambits is a perfectly good goal.

Stream footage here: https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=hyAhvdIvtzQ
Game played here: https://www.chess.com/live/game/5922405198 }
1. d4 { [%clk 0:05:00] } 1... e5 { Start of the (very dubious) Englund } { [%clk 0:04:59] } 2. dxe5 { [%clk 0:04:58] } 2... Nc6 { [%clk 0:04:58] } (2... Qe7 { This is an example of a line that black can play with 2...Qe7 which can be tricky to refute. } 3. Bg5 Qb4+ 4. Nc3 Qxb2 5. Bd2 Bb4 { White is still winning here with precise play. }) (2... Bc5 { Start of the "Rosen" or GM Derek trap } 3. Nf3 d6 4. exd6 Ne7 5. dxe7 Bxf2+ { Removing the defender! } { [%csl Gd1,Re1][%cal Ge1d1,Rf2e1] } 6. Kxf2 Qxd1 { Owch. }) 3. Nf3 { [%clk 0:04:53] } 3... d6 { [%clk 0:04:55] } 4. Bf4 { We don't take because it would allow black to develop. } { [%csl Rd6][%cal Re5d6,Gf8d6] [%clk 0:04:23] } 4... Bg4 { [%clk 0:03:55] } (4... dxe5 5. Qxd8+ Nxd8 { If black takes the pawn, the queen trade is very good for white as black is very passive and soon to be down a pawn. }) 5. exd6 { [%clk 0:04:17] } 5... cxd6 { Creating an isolated pawn. } { [%csl Bd6] [%clk 0:03:48] } 6. Nc3 { [%clk 0:04:06] } 6... Nf6 { [%clk 0:03:43] } 7. e3 { [%clk 0:04:01] } 7... d5 { [%clk 0:03:37] } 8. a3 { Preventing black from developing to good squares. This slows down white's development, but black's position is passive anyway and this reduces possibilities of any active counterplay. } { [%csl Rb4][%cal Ra3b4,Gc6b4,Gf8b4] [%clk 0:03:25] } 8... Qb6 { [%clk 0:03:13] } 9. Rb1 { Simple, but effective way of defending the pawn. } { [%csl Gb2][%cal Gb1b2,Rb6b2] [%clk 0:03:00] } 9... O-O-O { [%clk 0:03:04] } 10. b4 { Sealing the last two squares off from the queen. } { [%csl Rc5,Ra5][%cal Rb4a5,Rb4c5] [%clk 0:02:29] } 10... d4?? { [%clk 0:03:00] } (10... Bd6 { What if black creates an escape square? } { [%csl Gc7][%cal Gb6c7] } 11. Bxd6 Rxd6 12. Be2 { White still has a perfectly pleasant postition. }) 11. Na4 { The queen is now trapped! } { [%csl Rb6][%cal Ra4b6] [%clk 0:02:26] } 1-0


