[ChapterName "Game 126: King's Indian, Knights Variation (Black, ELO 1868)"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/dSNQO9Uq/Cwhx5rfu"]
[Annotator "https://lichess.org/@/rudisco"]

{ THEMES and Things to Look For
- A bishop xraying an undefended bishop is an opportunity to win material if the piece in the middle is your piece and it attacks another piece.
- When an opponent develops passively, we can deviate from standard opening moves to take advantage of the resultant weaknesses.
- In some positions, we can play moves that are otherwise considered "bad" for certain setups (such as f6 in a KID) if our opponent lacks the ability to take advantage of the resultant weaknesses (in this case white lacking a light squared bishop to attack the king).

Stream footage here: https://youtu.be/hyAhvdIvtzQ?t=905
Game played here: https://www.chess.com/live/game/5922476743 }
1. d4 { [%clk 0:04:59] } 1... Nf6 { Start of the King's Indian } { [%clk 0:04:58] } 2. Nf3 { [%clk 0:04:56] } 2... g6 { [%clk 0:04:58] } 3. e3 { [%clk 0:04:52] } 3... Bg7 { [%clk 0:04:57] } 4. c3 { Really passive line from white. } { [%csl Bc3,Bd4,Be3] [%clk 0:04:48] } 4... O-O { [%clk 0:04:53] } 5. Nbd2 { [%clk 0:04:44] } 5... d6 { Preparing the e5 pawn break. } { [%csl Ge5][%cal Ge7e5] [%clk 0:04:36] } 6. h3 { [%clk 0:04:37] } 6... Nc6 { More prep, unusual in the KID, but taking advantage of white's passive development. } { [%csl Ge5][%cal Gc6e5,Ge7e5] [%clk 0:04:35] } 7. b4 { [%clk 0:04:32] } 7... e5 { Offering the pawn sac to open up the position and expose white's undeveloped pieces and pawn weaknesses. } { [%csl Bc3,Be3,Bf2] [%clk 0:04:31] } 8. Bb2 { White correctly refrains from opening up the position. } { [%clk 0:04:12] } (8. b5 Ne7 9. dxe5 dxe5 10. Nxe5 Nfd5 { This is the idea behind sacrificing the pawn, everything is hanging and white's position will crumble. } { [%csl Ra1,Rc3,Re5][%cal Rg7a1] }) 8... Re8 { Developing the rook and positioning it to take advantage of a potential semi open file. } { [%clk 0:04:15] } 9. Bb5 { Pinning the knight. } { [%csl Re8][%cal Rb5e8] [%clk 0:04:00] } 9... Bd7 { Unpin. } { [%clk 0:04:11] } 10. O-O { Bishop is x-raying the undefended bishop. } { [%csl Rb5][%cal Rd7b5] [%clk 0:03:56] } 10... Nxd4 { Winning a pawn and attacking the hanging piece. } { [%csl Rb5,Rf3][%cal Rd7b5,Rd4f3] [%clk 0:03:48] } 11. exd4 { [%clk 0:03:24] } (11. Bxd7 Nxf3+ 12. Qxf3 Qxd7 { Black, again is up a pawn in the exchange. }) (11. Nxd4 exd4 12. Bxd7 Qxd7 (12... dxc3 13. Bxe8 cxb2 14. Rb1 (14. Bxf7+! { This is the critical move for white in this line. } 14... Kxf7 { Now black is worse due to the exposed king. } { [%csl Bf7] } 15. Rb1 { [%csl Bf7] }) 14... Nxe8 { In this line black exchanges a rook for a bishop and a pawn, but is compensated with a dominate pawn on b2. } { [%csl Gb2][%cal Gg7b2] }) 13. cxd4 { An even exchange, but black's pieces are still slightly more active. }) 11... Bxb5 { Allowing black to win a pawn in the exchange. } { [%clk 0:03:44] } 12. c4 { [%clk 0:03:23] } 12... Bc6 { [%clk 0:03:35] } 13. dxe5 { We are now in a very similar tactical position with the undefended bishop being x-rayed. } { [%csl Rb2][%cal Rg7b2] [%clk 0:03:07] } 13... Nd7 { Opening up the diagonal while also applying pressure to the pawn. } { [%csl Rb2][%cal Rg7b2,Rd7e5,Rd6e5] [%clk 0:03:20] } 14. Re1 { [%clk 0:03:01] } 14... Nxe5 { Winning the pawn in the exchange. } { [%clk 0:03:16] } 15. Nxe5 { [%clk 0:02:57] } 15... dxe5 { [%clk 0:03:15] } 16. b5 { [%clk 0:02:48] } 16... Bd7 { [%clk 0:03:14] } 17. Nf3 { [%clk 0:02:39] } 17... f6 { Why is black allowed to play f6 in the KID? White lacks a light squared bishop which blunts this weakness. This also protects the e5 pawn from attack. } { [%csl Ge5][%cal Gb3f7,Gf6e5,Rf3e5,Re1e5,Rb2e5] [%clk 0:02:58] } 18. Qd5+ { [%clk 0:02:14] } 18... Be6 { [%clk 0:02:57] } 19. Qxb7 { [%clk 0:02:12] } 19... Bxc4 { Queen on b7 is undefended and the pawn is only defended by that queen, how can black take advantage? } { [%csl Bb7,Bb5] [%clk 0:02:48] } 20. Rad1 { [%clk 0:02:10] } 20... Qb8 { Forcing the trade to simplify, or white is down another pawn. } { [%clk 0:02:21] } 21. Qxb8 { [%clk 0:02:00] } 21... Raxb8 { [%clk 0:02:19] } 22. a4 { a6 or c6 to attack the b5 pawn? } { [%cal Ga7a6,Gc7c6] [%clk 0:01:59] } 22... c6 { [%clk 0:01:43] } (22... a6 { The worse line, allowing white to fight for a better position due to... } 23. Rc1 { ... allowing white to counter skewer the bishop here. } { [%csl Rc7][%cal Rc1c7] } 23... axb5 24. axb5 Bxb5 25. Rxc7) (22... Bb3 { Looks like a fork but... } { [%csl Ra4,Rd1][%cal Rb3a4,Rb3d1] } 23. Ra1 { ... white can defend one forked piece with the other while moving it out of the attack. } { [%csl Ga4][%cal Ga1a4] }) 23. Rd7 { [%clk 0:01:33] } (23. Rc1 { If white tries to skewer in this position... } { [%csl Rc6][%cal Rc1c6] } 23... cxb5 24. axb5 Bxb5 { ... black is able to solve that problem by using the pawn to attack and moving the bishop out of harms way by retaking. }) 23... cxb5 { [%clk 0:01:41] } (23... a6 { This is a better move for black, guaranteeing winning a pawn and preventing white from playing Rxa7 ideas. } { [%csl Rb2][%cal Rb8b2] }) 24. Rxa7 { [%clk 0:01:28] } 24... bxa4 { [%clk 0:01:39] } 25. Ba3? { [%clk 0:01:23] } (25. Rxa4 { White is allowed to take the pawn here and attack the bishop. } { [%csl Rc4][%cal Ra4c4] }) 25... Ra8 { [%clk 0:01:28] } 26. Rc7 { [%clk 0:01:16] } 26... Rec8 { [%clk 0:01:27] } 27. Re7?? { [%clk 0:00:59] } 27... Bf8 { Forcing a favorable exchange or white loses the bishop. } { [%csl Ra3][%cal Rf8a3] [%clk 0:01:15] } 28. Nd2 { [%clk 0:00:40] } 28... Bxe7 { [%clk 0:01:11] } 29. Bxe7 { [%clk 0:00:38] } 29... a3 { [%clk 0:01:09] } (29... Kf7 { Danya would have played this in a classical game to ensure the pawn is defended. }) 30. Nxc4 { [%clk 0:00:31] } 30... Rxc4 { [%clk 0:01:07] } 31. Bxf6 { [%clk 0:00:30] } 31... a2 { [%clk 0:01:05] } 32. Bxe5 { [%clk 0:00:28] } 32... Rb4 { Want to get the rook there to guarantee promotion. } { [%csl Gb1][%cal Gb4b1] [%clk 0:00:37] } (32... Re4 33. Rxe4 { This doesn't allow promotion because the bishop still controls the promotion square. } { [%csl Ra1][%cal Re5a1] }) 33. Kh2 { [%clk 0:00:13] } 33... Rb1 { [%clk 0:00:36] } 34. Re2 { [%clk 0:00:06] } 34... a1=Q { [%clk 0:00:19] } 35. Bxa1 { [%clk 0:00:04] } 35... Rbxa1 { [%clk 0:00:18] } 36. f4 { [%clk 0:00:01] } 36... R1a2 { 0-1 Black wins. } { [%clk 0:00:18] } 0-1


