{ THEMES and Things to Look For
- Usually the strongest piece is the best piece to attack because it will have to move given you a tempo.
- Recognize that sometimes retreating moves may be critical to stopping opponents threats and allowing one to win the game.

Stream footage here: https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=3UqPa5eV2e0
Game played here: https://www.chess.com/live/game/5922476743 }
1. e4 { [%clk 0:05:00] } 1... c5 { [%clk 0:05:00] } 2. d4 { Start of the Smith Mora Gambit } { [%clk 0:04:59] } 2... cxd4 { [%clk 0:04:59] } 3. c3 { White offering another pawn. } { [%clk 0:04:59] } 3... Nf6 { Black declines and attempts to transpose into an Alapin Sicilian. } { [%clk 0:04:32] } 4. e5 { [%clk 0:04:56] } 4... Nd5 { [%clk 0:04:29] } 5. Qxd4 { [%clk 0:04:55] } 5... e6 { Defending the knight. } { [%csl Gd5][%cal Ge6d5] [%clk 0:04:26] } 6. Nf3 { [%clk 0:04:39] } 6... Nc6 { [%clk 0:04:24] } 7. Qe4 { [%clk 0:04:34] } 7... f5 { [%clk 0:04:19] } 8. Qe2 { White has ideas of playing c4, how can black stop it? } { [%csl Gc4][%cal Gc3c4] [%clk 0:04:33] } 8... b5 { Yes, the pawn is hanging, but it stops c4. } { [%clk 0:04:08] } 9. g3 { [%clk 0:04:30] } 9... a5 { [%clk 0:03:58] } 10. Bg2 { [%clk 0:04:29] } 10... Ba6 { x-raying the queen. } { [%csl Re2][%cal Ra6e2] [%clk 0:03:56] } (10... Be7 { This probably should have been played first to prevent the knight from getting to h4. } { [%csl Gh4][%cal Ge7h4] }) 11. Nh4 { White has two ideas, to exchange pieces and to check which may allow for some nasty tactics. How can black stop both? } { [%csl Gh5,Rd5][%cal Ge2h5,Rg2d5] [%clk 0:04:27] } 11... Nde7 { Moving the knight prevents it from being taken and now the knight can move to g6 to guard against Qh5+ } { [%csl Gg6][%cal Ge7g6] [%clk 0:03:36] } 12. O-O { [%clk 0:04:18] } 12... Qc7 { Pressuring the pawn. } { [%csl Re5][%cal Rc7e5] [%clk 0:03:34] } 13. Re1 { Dark squared bishop is stuck, how can we develop it? } { [%csl Bf8] [%clk 0:04:05] } 13... g6 { Ideas of fianchettoing the bishop. } { [%csl Gg7][%cal Gf8g7] [%clk 0:03:19] } 14. b4 { [%clk 0:03:39] } (14. Bg5 Bg7 15. Bf6 { The better idea for white. }) 14... Bg7 { [%clk 0:03:13] } 15. Bb2 { [%clk 0:03:35] } 15... O-O { [%clk 0:03:02] } 16. Nf3 { Black can fairly confidently win the pawn exchange now. } { [%csl Rb4][%cal Ra5b4] [%clk 0:03:29] } 16... axb4 { [%clk 0:02:51] } 17. cxb4 { [%clk 0:03:24] } 17... Nxb4 { White doesn't appear to have any compensation for the pawn. } { [%clk 0:02:51] } 18. Nc3 { Black would like to develop the light squared bishop by opening up the diagonal with b4. However, black can't move the knight just yet to make way for the pawn move as it would leave the pawn hanging. } { [%csl Gb4][%cal Gb5b4,Ra6f1] [%clk 0:03:15] } 18... Qa5 { Preparing b4 by protecting the pawn first so the knight can move out of the way. Has the added benefit of attacking the a pawn, preventing white's rook from coming into the game. } { [%csl Gb5,Ra2][%cal Ga5b5,Ra5a2] [%clk 0:02:19] } (18... Nbd5? { Playing this immediately allows white to win back the pawn. } 19. Nxb5 Qa5 { Looks like black wins a piece but... } { [%csl Rb5][%cal Ra5b5,Ra6b5,Ge2b5] } 20. a4 { ... white has a simple defensive resource. }) 19. h4 { [%clk 0:02:59] } 19... Nbd5 { [%clk 0:02:17] } 20. Nxd5 { [%clk 0:02:50] } 20... Nxd5 { [%clk 0:02:17] } 21. h5 { [%clk 0:02:49] } 21... b4 { [%csl Re2][%cal Ra6e2] [%clk 0:02:16] } 22. Qd2 { [%clk 0:02:37] } 22... gxh5 { Okay to open up the king a little bit as white doesn't have enough attacking resources. } { [%clk 0:02:13] } 23. Qg5 { [%clk 0:02:23] } 23... Qd8 { Easily defended by moving the queen back. This is a key move to stymie a potentially overwhelming attack by white. } { [%clk 0:02:07] } 24. Qxh5 { Note that white's pawn is well positioned for black, blocking both white's bishop and rook from fully influencing the board. } { [%csl Be5][%cal Ge1e5,Gb2e5] [%clk 0:02:21] } 24... Qe8 { [%clk 0:02:06] } 25. Qh4 { [%clk 0:01:59] } 25... Qg6 { Stopping potential Ng5 attacks. } { [%csl Gg5][%cal Gf3g5] [%clk 0:01:48] } 26. Rac1 { [%clk 0:01:50] } 26... Rac8 { [%clk 0:01:46] } 27. Nd4 { White has Bxd5 ideas to ruin black's pawn structure. } { [%csl Rd5][%cal Rg2d5] [%clk 0:01:37] } 27... Nc3 { Outposting the knight and preventing Bxd5. } { [%clk 0:01:22] } 28. a3 { White attacks the defender of the knight. } { [%csl Rb4][%cal Ra3b4] [%clk 0:01:30] } 28... Bh6 { Throwing in a counter attack to stop white from immediately dislodging the knight.
How does Danya see this? The critical observations are that the knight's defense is being undermined and the knight is being attacked by the rook and bishop. Is there a way to disrupt those attackers since black cannot add another defender? } { [%csl Rb2,Rc1,Ra3][%cal Rb2c3,Rc1c3,Ra3b4] [%clk 0:00:56] } (28... Na4 { This is also an option, but puts the knight on an undesireable square. }) 29. Rc2 { [%clk 0:01:12] } (29. f4 { A possible move for white, but it blocks in white's queen and creates some possible long term weaknesses on g3. } { [%csl Bg3] } 29... Na4 { Now black's knight can escape. }) 29... Bd3 { Trapping the rook. } { [%cal Rh6c1,Rc3d1,Rd3c2] [%clk 0:00:54] } 30. Bxc3 { [%clk 0:00:57] } 30... bxc3 { The rook isn't trapped any more, but it will be forced from blockading the pawn. } { [%clk 0:00:49] } (30... Bxc2 { This allows white to get something in exchange for the rook. } 31. Bxb4 { A tempo. } { [%csl Rf8][%cal Rb4f8] }) 31. Ra2 { [%clk 0:00:48] } 31... c2 { Forcing white into a losing exchange for the pawn. } { [%clk 0:00:48] } 32. Nxc2 { [%clk 0:00:31] } 32... Bxc2 { No tempo for black here in the exchange. } { [%clk 0:00:48] } 33. f4 { [%clk 0:00:27] } 33... Be4 { [%clk 0:00:45] } 34. Bxe4 { [%clk 0:00:13] } 34... fxe4 { [%clk 0:00:45] } 35. Kh2 { [%clk 0:00:09] } 35... Rf5 { [%clk 0:00:42] } 36. Rb1 { [%clk 0:00:03] } 36... Rh5 { [%clk 0:00:40] } 37. Qxh5 { [%clk 0:00:02] } 37... Qxh5+ { [%clk 0:00:39] } 38. Kg2 { [%clk 0:00:02] } 38... Qf3+ { [%clk 0:00:39] } 39. Kh2 { [%clk 0:00:01] } 39... e3 { [%clk 0:00:39] } 0-1


