{ THEMES and Things to Look For
- When the opponent does something weird in the opening, there's no need to deviate from opening principles!
- Always worth repeating, when you have a development lead open up the position to fully use your advantage.

Stream footage here: https://youtu.be/3UqPa5eV2e0?t=989
Game played here: https://www.chess.com/live/game/5930613572 }
1. e4 c6 { Start of the Caro Kann } 2. d4 d6 { Sort of going into a weird Caro Kann / Pirc hybrid. } 3. Nc3 e6 { This is a weird opening for black, but white can continue to look for developing moves. } 4. Nf3 a6 5. Bd3 h6 6. O-O b6 7. Be3 Bb7 { Black has played very passively so white need to... } 8. d5 { ... open up the center! } (8. e5 d5 { Black can easily close the center if white plays e5. }) (8. Nd2 { Another, stronger idea here, is for white to open up the f-file to gain a direct attack against black's king. } { [%csl Gf4,Re6][%cal Gf2f4,Gf4f5,Rf5e6] } 8... Nd7 9. f4 Rb8 10. f5) 8... cxd5 9. exd5 e5 { Black trying to close the center. White would like to crack that open with f4. } { [%csl Gf4,Re5][%cal Gf2f4,Rf4e5] } 10. Nd2 { Preparing f4 and giving the knight the option to head to c3. } { [%csl Gc4,Gf4][%cal Gd2c4,Gf2f4] } 10... g5 { Stopping f4. } 11. a4 { Preventing white from playing b5, allowing Nc4. } { [%csl Gb5,Gc4][%cal Gb6b5,Gd2c4] } (11. Nc4 { Going Nc4 immediately results in... } 11... b5 { ... black easily kicking the knight out. } { [%csl Rc4][%cal Rb5c4] }) 11... Nd7 12. Nc4 Ngf6 13. Bf5 Rg8 14. Re1 { Preparing opening the e file. } { [%csl Re8][%cal Re1e8] } 14... Be7 15. a5 { Preparing an outpost for the knight. } { [%csl Gb6][%cal Gc4b6] } 15... bxa5 16. Nxa5 { Attacking the hanging bishop with the option of outposting on c6. } { [%csl Rb7,Gc6][%cal Ra5b7,Ga5c6] } 16... Qc7 17. Ra4 h5 18. Qa1 { Guarding the knight so we can move the rook. } { [%csl Ga5][%cal Ga1a5] } 18... Bxd5 { Sacrificing the pawn to further open up the center. } 19. Nxd5 Nxd5 20. Rc4 Qb8 21. Nc6 Qb7 22. Qa4 { Increasing the pressure. } { [%csl Re8][%cal Ra4e8] } 22... Nxe3 23. Rxe3 Kf8 24. Rb3 Nb6 { Looks like a fork but, black's queen is pinned. } { [%csl Ra4,Rc4,Rb7][%cal Rb6a4,Rb6c4,Rb3b7] } 25. Qb4 { White can prevent black from immediately taking the rook. } { [%csl Rb7][%cal Rb4b7,Rb3b7] } 25... d5 { Attacking the rook. } { [%csl Rc4][%cal Rd5c4] } 26. Nxe7 dxc4 27. Nxg8+ Kxg8 28. Rg3 (28. Qxb6 { Looks like a free piece, but... } 28... Qd5 { ... black can threaten back rank mate and continue to attack the rook. Black will be up an exchange here. } { [%csl Gd1,Rh1,Rb3][%cal Gd5d1,Rd1h1,Rc4b3] }) 28... g4 { Pawn is pinned. } { [%csl Rg8,Rg4][%cal Rg3g8] } 29. h3 Rb8 { [%csl Rg8,Rg4][%cal Rg3g8] } 30. hxg4 h4 31. Rh3 Qd5 32. Rxh4 Qd1+ 33. Kh2 Qc1 34. Qe7 Qf4+ 35. Kh3 Nd5 36. Qd6 { Forking the knight and rook. } { [%csl Rd5,Rb8][%cal Rd6d5,Rd6b8] } 36... Re8 (36... Rb5 { This looks like it stops the fork but... } 37. Rh8+ { ... white has a rook sacrifice for mate. } 37... Kxh8 38. Qf8#) 37. Qxd5 Qxf2 38. Qf3 { White trading while ahead (and behind on time!) } 38... Qxf3+ 39. gxf3 Kg7 40. b3 cxb3 41. Rh7+ Kf6 42. cxb3 Rb8 43. Rh6+ Ke7 44. Rxa6 Rxb3 45. Be4 Rb8 46. Ra7+ Kd6 47. Rb7 Rh8+ 48. Kg3 Rf8 49. g5 f5 50. gxf6 Rxf6 51. Rb6+ Ke7 52. Rxf6 Kxf6 53. Kg4 Ke6 54. Kg5 Ke7 55. Kf5 Kd6 56. Kf6 Kc5 57. Kxe5 Kb6 58. f4 Kc7 59. Ke6 Kd8 60. Kf7 Kd7 61. f5 Kd6 62. f6 Ke5 63. Kg7 Kxe4 64. f7 Kf5 65. f8=Q+ Ke6 66. Qf6+ Kd5 67. Qe7 Kd4 68. Kf6 Kd5 69. Qe5+ Kc6 70. Ke6 Kb6 71. Qc3 Kb5 72. Kd5 Ka4 73. Qb2 Ka5 74. Kc5 1-0


