{ THEMES and Things to Look For
- Again, when ahead in development, leverage that advantage by opening up the position so your better developed pieces can better control the board!
- Some positions will call for concrete calculation to figure out if the lines lead to one player winning an exchange.

Stream footage here: https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=bIxvPbhuTpo
Game played here: https://www.chess.com/live/game/5930689030 }
1. e4 { [%c_effect e4;square;e4;type;Book] [%clk 0:04:59] } 1... d5 { Start of the Scandinavian. } { [%clk 0:04:56] } 2. exd5 { [%clk 0:04:52] } 2... Qxd5 { [%clk 0:04:55] } 3. Nc3 { Development tempo. } { [%csl Rd5][%cal Rc3d5] [%clk 0:04:31] } 3... Qd8 { [%clk 0:04:52] } 4. d4 { Controlling the center. } { [%clk 0:04:28] } 4... Nf6 { [%clk 0:04:50] } 5. Nf3 { [%clk 0:04:27] } 5... Bg4 { Pinning the knight. } { [%csl Rd1,Rf3][%cal Rg4d1] [%clk 0:04:47] } 6. Be2 { Breaking the pin. } { [%clk 0:04:00] } 6... e6 { [%clk 0:04:44] } 7. O-O { [%clk 0:03:57] } 7... c6 { [%clk 0:04:38] } 8. Ne5 { [%clk 0:03:55] } 8... Bxe2 { [%clk 0:04:35] } 9. Qxe2 { Looks like the pawn is hanging. } { [%csl Rd4][%cal Rd8d4] [%clk 0:03:54] } 9... Bd6 { [%clk 0:04:34] } (9... Qxd4 { If black takes the pawn... } 10. Rd1 Qb6 11. Bg5 { ... white gets plenty of compensation through much better developed pieces. } { [%csl Rd8,Be5,Bg5,Be2,Bd1][%cal Rd1d8] }) 10. Bg5 { White getting their own pin in. } { [%csl Rf6,Rd8][%cal Rg5d8] [%clk 0:03:33] } 10... O-O { White's pieces are well placed and developed, while black is stuck on the back 3 rows. White should have attacking ideas! } { [%csl Bg5,Be5,Gd6,Gf6] [%clk 0:04:26] } 11. f4 { White has plans of opening up the f file by playing f5 to take advantage of better development. } { [%csl Gf5][%cal Gf4f5,Rf1f8] [%clk 0:02:47] } (11. Ne4 { This idea works in blacks favor as... } 11... Be7 { ... black easily defends the knight and disrupts the pin and... } 12. Bxf6 Bxf6 13. Nxf6+ Qxf6 { ... after the dust settles this is an even material exchange, but white no longer has as much of a developmental advantage. }) 11... Nbd7 { Should white play f5 immediately or further prepare? } { [%clk 0:03:22] } 12. f5 { [%clk 0:02:25] } (12. Rad1 { Danya's preferred move order in a classical game is to delay f5 in favor of defending the d pawn first before playing f5. } { [%csl Gd4][%cal Gd1d4] }) 12... exf5 { [%clk 0:03:16] } (12... Qb6 { Danya would not play f5 immediately in a tournament game or against a stronger player as it allows black to attack the hanging pawn. } { [%csl Rd4][%cal Rb6d4] }) 13. Rxf5 { The f file is opened. } { [%cal Rf5f8] [%clk 0:02:18] } 13... Re8 { [%clk 0:03:13] } 14. Raf1 { Simple development idea by bringing the other rook into the game, doubling the rooks on the half open f file. } { [%clk 0:01:50] } 14... Nxe5 { [%clk 0:03:03] } 15. dxe5 { The knight is in trouble. } { [%csl Rf6][%cal Re5f6,Rf5f6,Rg5f6,Rf1f6] [%clk 0:01:48] } 15... Bxe5 { Sacrificing the bishop for an interesting idea that doesn't quite work. } { [%clk 0:02:42] } 16. Rxe5 { [%clk 0:01:36] } 16... Qd4+ { Looks like white is forked here but... } { [%csl Re5,Rg1][%cal Rd4e5,Rd4g1] [%clk 0:02:40] } 17. Re3 { Drawing on a defensive resource by blocking the check and defending the rook with the bishop. } { [%csl Ge3][%cal Gg5e3,Ge2e3] [%clk 0:01:20] } 17... Nd5 { [%clk 0:02:38] } (17... Ng4 { This is a better try for black because white doesn't control the square from which black's knight is attacking from. What is white's response? } { [%csl Gd5][%cal Gc3d5] } 18. Rd1 (18. Qf3 { Going for the same mate in the game doesn't work from this position as... } 18... Nxe3 19. Qxf7+ { ... black has two rooks defending the back rank so the back rank mate doesn't work. } { [%csl Be8,Ba8] }) (18. Rf4 { Similar idea, but doesn't work since the f4 rook is now blocking the defense of the e3 rook. } { [%cal Gg5f4] } 18... Qxe3+ 19. Qxe3 Nxe3 { Black wins the exchange. }) 18... Rxe3 (18... Qb6 { This looks like it is okay for black, however white can now take the hanging knight and the exchange results in... } { [%csl Rg4][%cal Re2g4] } 19. Qxg4 Rxe3 20. Bxe3 Qxe3+ 21. Kh1 { ... yet again white is up a minor piece. }) 19. Rxd4 Rxe2 20. Nxe2 { White wins a minor piece in the exchange should black attempt to trade. }) 18. Nxd5 { [%clk 0:01:12] } 18... cxd5 { [%clk 0:02:37] } 19. Qf2 { Black resigns. Game may have continued... } { [%clk 0:01:06] } 19... Rxe3 20. Qxf7+ Kh8 21. Qf8+ Rxf8 22. Rxf8# { ... a nice backrank mate. } 1-0


