{ THEMES and Things to Look For
- If you have a defended piece x-raying an undefended piece, if the piece that is blocking is also your piece, there are potential ideas of moving the blocking piece to take another piece and simultaneously attack your opponents now hanging piece. (I need a better way to word this.)
- Be discriminating on how you go about trading when up material.

Stream footage here: https://youtu.be/bIxvPbhuTpo?t=801
Game played here: https://www.chess.com/live/game/5930756903 }
1. e4 { [%c_effect e4;square;e4;type;Book] [%clk 0:05:00] } 1... c5 { Start of the Sicilian. } { [%clk 0:04:58] } 2. Nf3 { [%clk 0:04:59] } 2... Nc6 { [%clk 0:04:55] } 3. c3 { Start of the Alapin. White prepares d4 to control the center. } { [%csl Gd4][%cal Gd2d4] [%clk 0:04:57] } 3... Nf6 { Attacking the pawn to prevent d4. } { [%csl Re4][%cal Rf6e4] [%clk 0:04:33] } 4. Bd3? { White has blocked the development of the dark squared bishop. } { [%csl Bd2,Bc1] [%clk 0:04:54] } 4... d5 { Contesting the center. } { [%clk 0:04:25] } 5. e5 { [%clk 0:04:48] } 5... Ng4 { Attacking the pawn. } { [%csl Re5][%cal Rg4e5,Rc6e5] [%clk 0:04:10] } 6. Bb5 { Pinning the knight. } { [%csl Re8,Rc6][%cal Rb5e8] [%clk 0:04:44] } 6... Bd7 { [%clk 0:04:04] } 7. d4 { Bishop is xraying an undefended piece. What tactical idea does black have? } { [%csl Rb5][%cal Rd7b5] [%clk 0:04:41] } 7... Ncxe5 { Winning material by attacking the hanging bishop! } { [%csl Rb5][%cal Rd7b5] [%clk 0:03:46] } 8. Nxe5 { [%clk 0:04:36] } 8... Nxe5 { [%clk 0:03:45] } 9. Bxd7+ { [%clk 0:04:23] } 9... Nxd7 { Black wins a pawn in the exchange. } { [%clk 0:03:43] } 10. O-O { [%clk 0:04:21] } 10... e6 { [%clk 0:03:43] } 11. f4 { White is trying to open up the f file with f4-f5, how can black prevent this? } { [%csl Gf5][%cal Gf4f5] [%clk 0:04:19] } 11... g6 { [%clk 0:03:14] } 12. Be3 { [%clk 0:03:58] } 12... c4 { Closing the position so black can focus on completing development. } { [%clk 0:03:09] } 13. Nd2 { [%clk 0:03:56] } 13... Bg7 { [%clk 0:03:03] } 14. Nf3 { [%clk 0:03:47] } 14... O-O { Completing development. } { [%clk 0:02:59] } 15. Ne5 { Note that there is no need to trade here even though black is up material. Black has an even better idea of outposting the knight. } { [%csl Re5,Gf6,Ge4][%cal Rd7e5,Gd7f6,Gf6e4] [%clk 0:03:42] } 15... Nf6 { Going for the e4 outpost. } { [%csl Ge4][%cal Gf6e4] [%clk 0:02:37] } 16. Bf2 { [%clk 0:03:37] } 16... Ne4 { [%clk 0:02:35] } 17. Be1 { [%clk 0:03:29] } 17... f6 { White's knight however, is not on an outpost and black can kick it with the pawn. } { [%csl Re5][%cal Rf6e5] [%clk 0:02:19] } 18. Ng4 { [%clk 0:03:19] } 18... f5 { [%clk 0:02:18] } 19. Ne5 { [%clk 0:03:15] } 19... Bxe5 { Trading the bishop because the bishop is poorly positioned having the long diagonal closed. } { [%clk 0:02:10] } 20. fxe5 { Now black has a "good" knight vs white's "bad" bishop. } { [%csl Be4,Be1] [%clk 0:03:11] } 20... Qg5 { Black now has ideas of attacking on the kingside. } { [%clk 0:01:41] } 21. Qf3 { [%clk 0:03:06] } 21... b5 { Black also playing on the queenside. } { [%clk 0:01:38] } 22. Rd1 { [%clk 0:02:49] } 22... a5 { Preparing the b4 pawn break. } { [%csl Gb4][%cal Gb5b4] [%clk 0:01:36] } 23. Kh1 { [%clk 0:02:32] } 23... Rab8 { [%clk 0:01:34] } 24. Rg1 { [%clk 0:02:30] } 24... b4 { Trying to open up the file to get the rook to the 2nd rank. } { [%csl Rb2][%cal Rb8b2] [%clk 0:01:30] } 25. g4 { [%clk 0:02:10] } 25... fxg4 { White allowing the f file to open. } { [%csl Rf1][%cal Rf8f1] [%clk 0:01:25] } 26. Qxg4 { [%clk 0:02:06] } 26... Qxg4 { Trading queens here is okay because black has a strong positional advantage controlling the f file and possibly the b file. } { [%clk 0:01:23] } 27. Rxg4 { [%clk 0:02:05] } 27... bxc3 { [%clk 0:01:20] } 28. bxc3 { [%clk 0:02:02] } 28... Rb2 { [%clk 0:01:08] } 29. Rg2 { [%clk 0:01:49] } 29... Rf1+ { [%clk 0:01:06] } 30. Rg1 { [%clk 0:01:47] } 30... Rxg1+ { [%clk 0:01:01] } (30... Nf2+ { Another winning idea. } 31. Bxf2 Rfxf2 32. Rge1 Rxh2+ 33. Kg1 Rxa2) 31. Kxg1 { [%clk 0:01:46] } 31... Rxa2 { [%clk 0:01:01] } 32. Rb1 { The bishop is effectively stuck, giving black ideas of promoting the pawn. } { [%csl Be1][%cal Ga5a4,Ga4a3] [%clk 0:01:44] } 32... a4 { [%clk 0:00:52] } 33. Kf1 { [%clk 0:01:32] } 33... a3 { [%clk 0:00:48] } 34. Rb8+ { [%clk 0:01:30] } 34... Kg7 { [%clk 0:00:47] } 35. Ra8 { Black has no need to rush things, they should continue to develop... } { [%clk 0:01:26] } 35... g5 { ... in this case the pawn! } { [%clk 0:00:42] } 36. h4 { [%clk 0:01:11] } 36... g4 { [%clk 0:00:40] } 37. h5 { [%clk 0:01:08] } 37... g3 { [%csl Rg3][%cal Ge4g3,Re1g3] [%clk 0:00:38] } 38. Ra6 { [%clk 0:00:52] } 38... g2+ { [%clk 0:00:35] } 39. Kg1 { [%clk 0:00:50] } 39... Ng5! { A very nice idea to dislodge the king and promote! } { [%csl Gf3][%cal Gg5f3] [%clk 0:00:22] } 40. h6+ { [%clk 0:00:37] } 40... Kf7 { [%clk 0:00:17] } 41. Ra7+ { [%clk 0:00:32] } 41... Kg6 { [%clk 0:00:16] } 42. Rg7+ { [%clk 0:00:30] } 42... Kxh6 { [%clk 0:00:15] } 43. Rxg5 { [%clk 0:00:22] } 43... Kxg5 { [%clk 0:00:15] } 44. Bf2 { [%clk 0:00:19] } 44... Rxf2 { [%clk 0:00:14] } 45. Kxf2 { [%clk 0:00:13] } 45... a2 { [%clk 0:00:13] } 46. Kxg2 { [%clk 0:00:11] } 46... a1=Q { [%clk 0:00:12] } 47. Kf2 { [%clk 0:00:10] } 47... Qxc3 { [%clk 0:00:11] } 48. Ke2 { [%clk 0:00:05] } 48... Kf4 { [%clk 0:00:11] } 0-1


