{ THEMES and Things to Look For
- Philosophy of the King's Indian. Allow white to control the center with their pawns, but setup pieces to contest it from a distance in the future.
- Three types of pieces: 1) undefended piece (excl the King and Queen), 2) a piece that is defended by only one other non pawn piece, 3) defended piece protected by a pawn or more than one non-pawn piece.

Stream footage here: https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=7uxRcomJo7I
Game played here: https://www.chess.com/live/game/5930854169 }
1. d4 { [%clk 0:05:00] } 1... Nf6 { Start of the King's Indian } { [%clk 0:04:59] } 2. c4 { [%clk 0:05:00] } 2... g6 { [%clk 0:04:52] } 3. Nc3 { [%clk 0:04:59] } 3... Bg7 { [%clk 0:04:51] } 4. e4 { [%clk 0:04:58] } 4... d6 { [%clk 0:04:38] } 5. Nf3 { [%clk 0:04:58] } 5... O-O { [%clk 0:04:34] } 6. Be2 { [%clk 0:04:56] } 6... e5 { [%clk 0:04:32] } 7. Be3 { [%clk 0:04:47] } (7. dxe5? { This is not a free pawn for white. } 7... dxe5 8. Nxe5 Nxe4 { Black equalizes materials with a discovered attack on the hanging knight and opens up the long diagonal for the fianchetto'd bishop. } { [%csl Re5][%cal Rg7e5] }) (7. d5 { Another common line where white has ideas of attacking on the queenside and black has ideas of attacking on the kingside. } 7... Nbd7 8. O-O) 7... exd4 { [%clk 0:04:29] } 8. Nxd4 { Standard King's Indian moves up to this point. Black has ideas of applying more pressure on the e4 pawn. } { [%csl Re4][%cal Rf6e4] [%clk 0:04:44] } 8... Re8 { [%csl Re4][%cal Re8e4,Rf6e4] [%clk 0:04:15] } 9. f3 { Note that this continues to be main line theory. The bishop is currently hanging and black would like to prepare d5 to open up the center. } { [%csl Be3,Gd5][%cal Gd6d5] [%clk 0:04:42] } 9... c6 { Preparing d5. } { [%clk 0:03:41] } 10. Qd2 { Defending the bishop. } { [%csl Ge3][%cal Gd2e3] [%clk 0:04:39] } 10... d5 { [%clk 0:03:40] } 11. cxd5 { [%clk 0:04:37] } 11... cxd5 { [%clk 0:03:39] } 12. e5? { White goes out of theory and hangs the pawn. } { [%csl Re5][%cal Re8e5] [%clk 0:04:31] } 12... Rxe5 { [%clk 0:03:36] } 13. O-O { [%clk 0:04:26] } 13... Nc6 { [%clk 0:03:33] } 14. Rad1 { Black should continue developing pieces. } { [%clk 0:04:14] } 14... Bd7 { A modest move, but it gets the bishop into the action. } { [%clk 0:02:57] } (14... Rxe3 { This doesn't work because... }) 15. Bf4 { [%clk 0:04:08] } 15... Re8 { [%clk 0:02:52] } 16. Kh1 { White's d4 knight is defended by only one piece and that square is being x-rayed by the bishop, can black draw the queen onto that square?
This idea goes deeper, observe that white's e2 bishop is only defended by the c3 knight and the c3 knight is also x-ray'd by the bishop. There is the possibility of black winning material here if the e2 bishop is left defended solely by the knight. } { [%csl Rd4,Rc3,Re2][%cal Gd2d4,Rg7c3,Re8e2] [%clk 0:04:04] } 16... Nxd4 { Yes. } { [%clk 0:02:38] } 17. Qxd4 { [%clk 0:03:59] } 17... Nh5 { [%csl Rd4,Rf4][%cal Rg7d4,Rh5f4] [%clk 0:02:24] } 18. Qd2 { [%clk 0:03:57] } 18... Nxf4 { [%clk 0:02:22] } 19. Qxf4 { Again, white has a piece that is being attacked and solely defended by another piece. How can black win material? } { [%csl Ge2][%cal Gc3e2,Re8e2] [%clk 0:03:53] } 19... Bxc3 { Removing the defender! } { [%csl Re2][%cal Re8e2] [%clk 0:02:10] } 20. bxc3 { [%clk 0:03:33] } 20... Rxe2 { [%clk 0:02:08] } 21. Rxd5 { [%clk 0:03:33] } 21... Qe7 { [%clk 0:02:06] } 22. h4 { White has ideas of h5 } { [%csl Gh5][%cal Gh4h5] [%clk 0:03:29] } 22... h5 { Stopping h5. } { [%clk 0:01:59] } 23. Rfd1 { [%clk 0:03:23] } 23... Bc6 { Nice solid square for the light squared bishop being defended by the pawn and covering the long diagonal. } { [%csl Rh1][%cal Rc6h1] [%clk 0:01:46] } 24. Rd6 { [%clk 0:03:02] } 24... Re8 { Developing black's final piece. } { [%clk 0:01:37] } 25. Qh6 { [%clk 0:02:42] } 25... Qxh4+ { <br /><br />Game may have
continued... } { [%clk 0:01:21] } (25... Kg1 26. Re1+ Rxe1 27. Rxe1# { -M2 }) 26. Kg1 { A lot of possible mates here, Danya shows a line to really rub it in. } 26... Rxg2+ 27. Kxg2 Bxf3+ 28. Kxf3 Qg4+ 29. Kf2 Re2+ 30. Kf1 Qg2# 0-1


