{ THEMES and Things to Look For
- Sometimes weakening the kingside by moving pawns is justified especially if it accomplishes a specific goal and your opponents have no threats.
- Some pawn structures can be aesthetically ugly, but can still be used to control the center.
- In endgames with a bishop, try to fix your opponents pawns on the same color as your bishop so they are more easily attacked.

Stream footage here: https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=VEwKZo8l7yY
Game played here: https://www.chess.com/live/game/5938375354 }
1. e4 { [%clk 0:05:00] } 1... c5 { [%clk 0:04:59] } 2. c3 { Danya finally plays the Alapin with the white pieces. } { [%clk 0:04:58] } 2... d6 { [%clk 0:04:58] } 3. d4 { [%clk 0:04:51] } 3... cxd4 { [%clk 0:04:57] } 4. cxd4 { Black inaccurately allows white to control the center and giving white a great development square for the knight. } { [%csl Bd4,Be4,Gc3][%cal Gb1c3] [%clk 0:04:46] } 4... Nf6 { [%clk 0:04:57] } 5. Nc3 { [%clk 0:04:45] } 5... Nc6 { [%clk 0:04:55] } 6. Nf3 { [%clk 0:04:42] } 6... g6 { Black wants Bg4, how can white stop it? } { [%csl Gg4][%cal Gc8g4] [%clk 0:04:47] } (6... Bg4 7. d5 Ne5 { Seems like the knight is pinned, but... } { [%csl Rf3,Rd1][%cal Rg4d1] } 8. Nxe5 Bxd1 9. Bb5+ Nd7 10. Bxd7+ Qxd7 11. Nxd7 Kxd7 12. Kxd1 { ... white has a common tactical idea which results in white being up a knight at the end of the exchange. This is also a common line in the queen's gambit. }) 7. h3 { Yes, this doesn't develop a piece, but denies black's access to a critical square. } { [%clk 0:04:10] } 7... Bg7 { [%clk 0:04:44] } 8. Be3 { [%clk 0:04:08] } 8... O-O { [%clk 0:04:41] } 9. Bd3 { Supporting the center and development. } { [%csl Ge4][%cal Gd3e4] [%clk 0:03:45] } (9. Bc4 { This blunders white into... } 9... Nxe4 10. Nxe4 d5 { ... a pawn fork or loss of a pawn. } { [%csl Re4,Rc4][%cal Rd5c4,Rd5e4] }) 9... Nb4 { [%clk 0:04:40] } 10. Be2 { White can easily push the knight away with a3 at some point. } { [%csl Ga3][%cal Ga2a3] [%clk 0:03:41] } 10... a6 { [%clk 0:04:26] } 11. O-O { [%clk 0:03:34] } 11... d5 { [%clk 0:04:07] } 12. e5 { [%clk 0:03:31] } 12... Ne4 { [%clk 0:04:05] } 13. Nxe4 { [%clk 0:03:14] } 13... dxe4 { Trading knights and creating a weak pawn for black. } { [%csl Be4] [%clk 0:04:02] } 14. Nd2 { [%clk 0:03:08] } 14... Bf5 { [%clk 0:03:41] } (14... Qd5 { Another attempt to defend the pawn. } { [%csl Ge4][%cal Gd5e4] } 15. Bc4 { Met with Bc4. } { [%csl Rd5][%cal Rc4d5] }) (14... f5 15. exf6 (15. Qb3+ { This "fork" doesn't work because... } { [%csl Rb4,Rg8][%cal Rb3b4,Rb3g8] } 15... Nd5 { Black can defend the knight and block the check. } 16. Bc4 e6 { And meets Bc4 with e6. }) 15... exf6 16. Nxe4 { White winning a pawn. }) 15. g4 { Yes, this weakens the the kingside, but white would like to chase away the bishop to win the pawn and there are no threats on the kingside. } { [%csl Re4][%cal Rd2e4] [%clk 0:03:07] } 15... Be6 { [%clk 0:03:16] } 16. Nxe4 { [%clk 0:02:52] } 16... Rc8 { [%clk 0:03:00] } (16... Nxa2 { Why can't black take the pawn? } 17. Qa4 { White threatens to trade the rook for two minor pieces. } { [%csl Ra2][%cal Ra4a2,Ra1a2] } 17... b5 18. Qa3 Qd5 19. Bf3 { Black will lose material here. } { [%csl Gf6,Rd5,Ra2][%cal Ge4f6,Rf3d5,Ra3a2,Ra1a2] }) 17. a3 { Now white plays a3 to force black to make a decision on where to place the knight. } { [%clk 0:02:23] } 17... Nc2 { [%clk 0:02:47] } 18. Rc1 { [%clk 0:02:20] } 18... Nxe3 { [%clk 0:01:58] } 19. fxe3 { Pawn structure looks sort of ugly, but white has a strong pawn chain to control the center. } { [%csl Be3,Bd4,Be5] [%clk 0:02:19] } 19... Bd5 { [%clk 0:01:47] } 20. Bf3 { Note that black's bishop is a "type 2" defended piece. It is only defended by the queen - white may look for opportunities to remove the defender and win it with the x-ray. } { [%csl Gd5][%cal Gd8d5,Rf3d5] [%clk 0:02:03] } 20... f5 { [%clk 0:01:29] } 21. exf6 { [%clk 0:01:47] } 21... exf6 { Black allowing white to win an exchange using the previously mentioned idea. } { [%clk 0:01:28] } 22. Rxc8 { [%clk 0:01:40] } 22... Qxc8 { Removing the defender. } { [%csl Bd5] [%clk 0:01:24] } 23. Nxf6+ { Winning a pawn with check and a discovered attack on the now hanging bishiop. } { [%csl Rd5][%cal Rf3d5] [%clk 0:01:37] } (23. Nd6 { This discovered attack doesn't work. } { [%csl Rd5,Rc8][%cal Rf3d5,Rd6c8] } 23... Qc6 { Black can simply use one of the attacked pieces to defend the other. }) 23... Rxf6 { [%clk 0:01:20] } 24. Bxd5+ { [%clk 0:01:37] } 24... Kh8 { [%clk 0:01:18] } 25. Rxf6 { [%clk 0:01:36] } 25... Bxf6 { [%clk 0:01:18] } 26. Kg2 { Stopping any of black's ideas of taking the pawn with check. } { [%csl Rg1][%cal Rg5e3,Re3g1] [%clk 0:00:45] } 26... Bg5 { [%clk 0:01:13] } 27. Qf3 { [%clk 0:00:36] } 27... b5 { [%clk 0:01:02] } 28. b4 { Freezing black's a and b pawns on light squares. } { [%csl Ba6,Bb5,Bd5] [%clk 0:00:32] } 28... Qc2+ { [%clk 0:00:39] } 29. Qf2 { [%clk 0:00:31] } 29... Qc8 { [%clk 0:00:33] } 30. e4 { [%clk 0:00:29] } 30... Qd8 { [%clk 0:00:24] } 31. Qf7 { [%clk 0:00:18] } 31... Bh4 { [%clk 0:00:19] } 32. e5 { [%clk 0:00:16] } 32... Be7 { [%clk 0:00:15] } 33. Kf3 { Bringing in the king as a defensive resource. } { [%clk 0:00:14] } 33... Bg5 { [%clk 0:00:12] } 34. Ke4 { [%clk 0:00:14] } 34... Bh4 { [%clk 0:00:08] } 35. e6 { [%clk 0:00:12] } 35... Be7 { [%clk 0:00:06] } 36. Bc6 { [%clk 0:00:10] } 36... Bf6 { [%clk 0:00:04] } 37. d5 { [%clk 0:00:09] } 37... Be7 { [%clk 0:00:04] } 38. Qe8+ { [%clk 0:00:08] } 38... Qxe8 { [%clk 0:00:02] } 39. Bxe8 { Forcing the trade of queens into a winning endgame for white. } { [%clk 0:00:08] } 39... Bd6 { [%clk 0:00:01] } 40. Bc6 { 1-0 White wins. } { Now white can easily win the light squared pawns. This is the idea that allows white to win, there are many similar endgame setups with opposite colored bishops where it is simply a draw. } { [%csl Bc6,Bb5,Ba6] [%clk 0:00:07] } 1-0


