{ THEMES and Things to Look For
- Just because an opponent has taken a weird line, doesn't mean our fundamental opening plans change. Develop!
- Inaccessability, if a move creates a weakness on a square that your opponent can't access, it is okay to create that weakness.

Stream footage here: https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=uVQKL83tZb0
Game played here: https://www.chess.com/live/game/5986986732 }
1. e4 { [%clk 0:05:00] } 1... e5 { [%clk 0:04:59] } 2. Nf3 { [%clk 0:04:58] } 2... Nc6 { [%clk 0:04:58] } 3. Bb5 { Start of the Ruy Lopez } { [%clk 0:04:57] } 3... Nd4 { Start of Bird's Defense, attacking the bishop.
Named after Harry Bird, a 19th century chess player. } { [%csl Rb5][%cal Rd4b5] [%clk 0:04:56] } 4. Nxd4 { [%clk 0:04:45] } 4... exd4 { The idea here is to stop white's b knight from developing. } { [%clk 0:04:54] } 5. O-O { [%clk 0:04:30] } (5. c3 Qg5 { This is why white castles, that prevents this annoying fork. } { [%csl Rb5,Rg2][%cal Rg5b5,Rg5g2] }) 5... Bc5 { White doesn't have to immediately remove this pawn, but instead use it as a way to limit the influence of black's dark squared bishop. The pawn is, however, stopping white's knight from developing. Is there another way for white's knight to develop? } { [%csl Bd4,Gc3][%cal Gb1c3,Rd4c3] [%clk 0:04:53] } 6. d3 { Preparing Nd2. } { [%csl Gd2][%cal Gb1d2] [%clk 0:03:51] } (6. c3 { If white chooses to try to remove the pawn so the knight can develop to c3... } 6... Nf6 7. cxd4 Bxd4 8. Nc3 { ... black gains a well placed, outposted bishop which will be difficult to remove. } { [%csl Rg1][%cal Rd4g1] }) 6... Qh4 { Looks threatening, but white can continue to develop. } { [%clk 0:04:50] } 7. Nd2 { [%clk 0:03:40] } 7... c6 { [%clk 0:04:47] } 8. Bc4 { [%clk 0:03:26] } 8... d6 { [%clk 0:04:45] } 9. Nf3 { [%csl Rd4,Rh4][%cal Rf3d4,Rf3h4] [%clk 0:03:23] } (9. f4 { This is also fine, but gives black a possible outpost on e3. } { [%csl Be3] } 9... Nf6 10. h3 { Black may have ideas of getting the knight to e3 and white can try to prevent it, but it weakens the kingside. }) 9... Qh5 { Black would like to battery up on the diagonal, how can white stop this? } { [%csl Gg4,Rd1,Rf3][%cal Gc8g4,Rh5d1] [%clk 0:04:43] } 10. Ng5 { More or less forcing the queen trade. } { [%clk 0:02:47] } 10... Qxd1 { White does not have to immediately retake, how can white win material? } { [%clk 0:04:42] } 11. Bxf7+ { Taking the pawn first. } { [%csl Gf7][%cal Gg5f7] [%clk 0:02:37] } 11... Ke7 { [%clk 0:04:41] } 12. Rxd1 { [%clk 0:02:37] } 12... h6 { It looks like black wins a piece, but... } { [%csl Rg5,Rf7][%cal Rh6g5,Re7f7] [%clk 0:04:39] } 13. Bxg8 { ... white can take the knight. } { [%clk 0:02:25] } 13... Rxg8 { [%clk 0:04:37] } 14. Nf3 { [%clk 0:02:24] } 14... Bg4 { [%clk 0:04:34] } 15. Re1 { [%clk 0:02:12] } 15... g5 { [%clk 0:04:22] } (15... Bxf3 { White should not be worried here because queens are off the board and the files are still closed. } 16. gxf3 Raf8 17. Kg2 { Black will have a difficult time attacking white's king. }) 16. Nd2 { [%clk 0:02:09] } 16... Raf8 { Black preparing a kingside attack, but it is not as threatening as it looks because queens are off the board. } { [%cal Rf8f2,Rg8g2] [%clk 0:04:20] } 17. Nb3 { [%clk 0:01:56] } 17... Bb6 { Note that black's king and rook are on the same diagonal, how can white take advantage of this? } { [%csl Be7,Bf8] [%clk 0:04:17] } 18. Bd2 { [%clk 0:01:55] } 18... Bc7 { [%clk 0:03:51] } 19. e5 { A couple of nasty ideas by white, threatening to open up the center and win an exchange. } { [%csl Re7,Gb4,Rf8][%cal Re1e7,Gd2b4,Rb4f8] [%clk 0:01:53] } (19. Nxd4 { Could also capture the hanging pawn, but the line as played is a nice harmony of different tactical ideas. }) 19... dxe5 { [%clk 0:03:49] } 20. Bb4+ { [%clk 0:01:44] } 20... Kd7 { Black simply gives up the exchange. } { [%clk 0:03:37] } (20... Bd6 { This seems to be a more solid defensive idea, however it doesn't work. } 21. Rxe5+ { Bishop is pinned so it cannot retake. } { [%csl Re7,Rd6][%cal Rb4e7] } 21... Kd7 22. Bxd6 Kxd6 23. Re4 { A nice fork, white will be up two pawns and is winning. } { [%csl Rd4,Rg4][%cal Re4d4,Re4g4] }) 21. Bxf8 { [%clk 0:01:43] } 21... Rxf8 { [%clk 0:03:36] } 22. Nc5+ { [%clk 0:01:31] } 22... Kc8 { [%clk 0:03:34] } 23. Ne4 { [%clk 0:01:28] } 23... b6 { [%clk 0:03:31] } 24. f3 { Solidifying the outpost. } { [%csl Be4][%cal Gf3e4,Gd3e4] [%clk 0:01:28] } 24... Be6 { White is up material and will continue developing to leverage that advantage. } { [%clk 0:03:28] } 25. Re2 { Ideas of doubling rooks. } { [%csl Ge1][%cal Ga1e1,Re1e7] [%clk 0:01:26] } 25... Bd5 { [%clk 0:03:22] } 26. Rae1 { [%clk 0:01:25] } 26... Bxe4 { [%clk 0:03:19] } 27. Rxe4 { These rooks are strong in an open position. } { [%csl Be1,Be4] [%clk 0:01:24] } 27... Kd7 { [%clk 0:03:14] } 28. h4 { White trying to open up the position so the rooks can exert maximum influence. } { [%clk 0:01:15] } 28... c5 { [%clk 0:03:03] } 29. hxg5 { [%clk 0:01:14] } 29... hxg5 { [%clk 0:03:01] } 30. Kf2 { [%clk 0:01:12] } 30... Rf4 { Black continuing to play into white's hands by offering trades. } { [%clk 0:03:00] } 31. Rxf4 { [%clk 0:01:10] } 31... gxf4 { [%clk 0:02:59] } 32. Rh1 { White looks to establish maximum control of the board by getting the rook to the 7th rank. } { [%csl Gh7][%cal Gh1h7] [%clk 0:01:08] } 32... Kc6 { [%clk 0:02:53] } 33. Rh7 { [%clk 0:01:07] } 33... a6 { [%clk 0:02:48] } 34. g4 { [%clk 0:01:00] } (34. Kg1 { White could also take the long way and win. } { [%cal Gg1h2,Gh2h3,Gh3g4,Gg4f5] }) 34... fxg3+ { [%clk 0:02:46] } 35. Kxg3 { [%clk 0:00:59] } 35... e4+ { [%clk 0:02:43] } 36. Rxc7+ { White demonstrates a nice concept here by sacing the exchange, simplifying the game to a winning endgame. } { [%clk 0:00:46] } (36. f4 { This is also winning. }) (36. Kg4 e3 { Not sacrificing the rook allows black to establish a protected passer. White is still winning here, but it is more unclear. } { [%csl Be3] }) 36... Kxc7 { [%clk 0:02:42] } 37. fxe4 { [%clk 0:00:45] } 37... Kd6 { [%clk 0:02:40] } 38. Kf4 { The protected passer is devastating. } { [%csl Be4] [%clk 0:00:44] } 38... Ke6 { [%clk 0:02:39] } 39. e5 { [%clk 0:00:39] } 39... b5 { [%clk 0:02:36] } 40. Ke4 { [%clk 0:00:38] } 40... a5 { Black will eventually be in zugswang and be forced to move the king once they exhaust their pawn moves. } { [%clk 0:02:33] } 41. b3 { [%clk 0:00:36] } 41... a4 { [%clk 0:02:21] } 42. bxa4 { [%clk 0:00:34] } 42... bxa4 { [%clk 0:02:18] } 43. a3 { Now black has no choice but to move the king back. } { [%clk 0:00:33] } 43... Kd7 { [%clk 0:02:06] } (43... c4 44. dxc4 { The other legal move results in another passed pawn for white. }) 44. Kd5 { [%clk 0:00:24] } 44... Ke7 { [%clk 0:02:02] } 45. Kxc5 { [%clk 0:00:23] } 45... Ke6 { [%clk 0:02:01] } 46. Kxd4 { White now has a straightforward win with 3 passed pawns. } { [%clk 0:00:22] } 46... Ke7 { [%clk 0:01:58] } 47. Kd5 { [%clk 0:00:22] } 47... Kd7 { [%clk 0:01:57] } 48. d4 { [%clk 0:00:21] } 48... Ke7 { [%clk 0:01:55] } 49. e6 { [%clk 0:00:20] } 49... Kf6 { [%clk 0:01:52] } 50. Kd6 { [%clk 0:00:19] } 50... Kf5 { [%clk 0:01:51] } 51. e7 { [%clk 0:00:18] } 51... Ke4 { [%clk 0:01:50] } 52. e8=Q+ { [%clk 0:00:17] } 52... Kxd4 { [%clk 0:01:50] } 53. Qe5+ { [%clk 0:00:05] } 53... Kc4 { [%clk 0:01:46] } 54. Qc5# { 1-0 White wins by checkmate. } { [%clk 0:00:04] } 1-0


