{ THEMES and Things to Look For
- Winning two minor pieces for a rook and a pawn in the middle game is considered winning. The rook cannot be fully leveraged in closed positions where as the minor pieces can.
- Seeing good ideas in chess is not all about calculating lines, sometimes it can be pure pattern recognition to find ideas that work in similar positions.

Stream footage here: https://youtu.be/uVQKL83tZb0?t=1460
Game played here: https://www.chess.com/live/game/5987138806 }
1. e4 { [%clk 0:04:56] } 1... Nf6 { [%clk 0:05:00] } 2. e5 { [%clk 0:04:55] } 2... Nd5 { Start of the Alekhine. } { [%clk 0:04:59] } 3. d4 { [%clk 0:04:54] } 3... d6 { Going down the main line. } { [%clk 0:04:58] } 4. c4 { [%clk 0:04:39] } 4... Nb6 { [%clk 0:04:57] } 5. exd6 { [%clk 0:04:36] } 5... cxd6 { White should develop and find moves that support the existing control of the center. } { [%clk 0:04:57] } 6. Nc3 { [%clk 0:04:30] } 6... g6 { [%clk 0:04:56] } 7. Be3 { Continuing with the theme of supporting the center. } { [%csl Gd4][%cal Ge3d4] [%clk 0:04:12] } 7... Bg7 { [%clk 0:04:55] } 8. h3 { White chooses to stop the pin. This is done for a very concrete reason to prevent some nasty pinning ideas that black has. } { [%clk 0:03:50] } (8. Nf3 { Playing Nf3 allows an annoying idea for black. } 8... Bg4 9. Be2 (9. b3 { This defends the c pawn, but leaves the knight hanging and black's dark squared bishop could have even greater control over the long diagonal. } { [%csl Rc3,Ra1][%cal Rg7a1] }) 9... Nc6 10. O-O { See how white's bishop is the only defender for two pieces? } { [%csl Gc4,Gf3][%cal Ge2f3,Ge2c4] } 10... Bxf3 11. Bxf3 Nxc4 { Black overloads the bishop and wins the pawn. }) 8... O-O { [%clk 0:04:54] } 9. Nf3 { [%clk 0:03:46] } 9... Nc6 { [%clk 0:04:54] } 10. Be2 { [%clk 0:03:42] } 10... e5 { [%clk 0:04:54] } 11. O-O { White can ignore the central tension as the pawn is sufficiently defended. } { [%csl Rd4][%cal Re5d4] [%clk 0:03:21] } 11... e4?? { Black blunders the pawn. } { [%clk 0:04:45] } (11... Bf5 { Better move is to keep the tension and develop the bishop. }) (11... exd4 12. Nxd4 { Severely weakened isolated pawn. } { [%csl Bd6] }) 12. Nxe4 { Black sacrificing the pawn... } { [%clk 0:03:18] } 12... Bf5 { ... for a development tempo. } { [%clk 0:04:38] } 13. Nc3 { [%clk 0:03:15] } 13... Nb4 { Black wants Nc2, how does white stop this? } { [%csl Gc2][%cal Gb4c2,Gf5c2] [%clk 0:04:35] } 14. Rc1 { Stopping Nc2. } { [%clk 0:02:57] } 14... Rc8 { [%clk 0:04:33] } 15. a3 { [%clk 0:02:54] } 15... Nc2 { [%clk 0:04:29] } 16. Rxc2 { [%clk 0:02:52] } 16... Bxc2 { [%clk 0:04:27] } 17. Qxc2 { [%clk 0:02:52] } 17... Nxc4 { White is about even in material for the exchange, trading two minor pieces and a pawn for the rook. However, this is very advantageous in the middle game where the rook cannot be fully leveraged.
Does white want to take the knight? } { [%csl Rc4][%cal Re2c4] [%clk 0:04:27] } 18. Bxc4 { Yes, the knight is black's only active piece and trading it for white's passive bishop is a win. } { [%clk 0:02:19] } 18... Rxc4 { How to unpin the knight? } { [%csl Rc3][%cal Rc4c2] [%clk 0:04:26] } 19. Qb3 { Putting the queen on a very active square with tempo. } { [%csl Rg8,Rf7,Rc4,Rb7][%cal Rb3g8,Rb3b7] [%clk 0:02:06] } (19. Qd2 { Also a fine idea to trade the dark squared bishops. } { [%csl Gh6,Rg7][%cal Gd2h6,Rh6g7] }) 19... Qc8 { [%clk 0:04:16] } 20. Nd5 { White posing a forking thread with Ne7. } { [%cal Gd5e7,Re7g8,Re7c8] [%clk 0:02:04] } 20... Re8 { [%clk 0:04:06] } 21. Bg5 { Threatening to fork and win the exchange. The bishop also controls the dark squares around the king. } { [%csl Ge7,Bf6,Bh6][%cal Gg5e7,Gd5e7] [%clk 0:01:48] } 21... Kf8 { [%clk 0:03:14] } 22. Nf6 { [%clk 0:01:42] } 22... Bxf6 { [%clk 0:03:05] } 23. Bxf6 { Trying to setup a lobster pincer mate. } { [%clk 0:01:42] } 23... Re6 { [%clk 0:02:51] } (23... Rc1 { Strong move for black. } 24. Rxc1 (24. Qb4 { Ignoring the threat of the rook allows white to deliver a nasty check that slows down white's attack. } 24... Rxf1+ 25. Kxf1 Qc1+ 26. Ne1) (24. Bg5 { White's strongest move to defend against black's counterplay by betting to the root of the problem which is allow black to get the queen to c1. Bg5 defends the square and stops those ideas. }) 24... Qxc1+ 25. Kh2 Qf4+ { [%csl Rf6,Rh2][%cal Rf4f6,Rf4h2] }) 24. Bg5 { [%clk 0:01:32] } 24... f6 { [%clk 0:02:48] } 25. Be3 { [%clk 0:01:21] } 25... d5 { Queen x-ray's the hanging pawn. } { [%csl Rd5][%cal Rb3d5] [%clk 0:02:42] } 26. Nd2 { [%clk 0:01:11] } 26... Rb6 { [%clk 0:02:37] } 27. Qa2 { [%clk 0:01:10] } 27... Rc2 { [%clk 0:02:35] } 28. Qxd5 { [%clk 0:01:08] } 28... Rbxb2 { Yes, black wins the pawn back, but white's queen dominates the center. } { [%clk 0:02:31] } 29. Bh6+ { <br /><br />Game may have continued... } { Mate in 3 from this position. } { [%clk 0:00:58] } (29. Ke7 Re1+ 30. Qe6 Rxe6+ 31. Kf7 Re1# { +M3 }) 1-0


