1. e4 { [%clk 0:05:00] } 1... e5 { [%clk 0:05:00] } 2. Nf3 { [%clk 0:04:59] } 2... Nc6 { [%clk 0:04:59] } 3. Nc3 { [%clk 0:04:58] } 3... Nf6 { The four knights game. } { [%clk 0:04:58] } 4. Bb5 { [%clk 0:04:55] } 4... Nd4 { [%clk 0:04:52] } 5. Nxd4 { [%clk 0:04:54] } 5... exd4 { [%clk 0:04:51] } 6. e5 { Very drawish line which results in a mass liquidation of pieces. } { [%clk 0:04:54] } 6... dxc3 { [%clk 0:04:43] } 7. exf6 { [%clk 0:04:53] } 7... Qxf6 { [%clk 0:04:38] } 8. dxc3 { [%clk 0:04:51] } 8... Bc5 { [%clk 0:04:31] } 9. O-O { [%clk 0:04:49] } 9... O-O { [%clk 0:04:11] } 10. Bd3 { [%clk 0:04:47] } (10. Bxd7 { This is not a free pawn for white. } 10... Rd8 { Black gets a nasty pin on the light squared bishop and will win it. } { [%csl Rd1,Rd7][%cal Rd8d1,Rc8d7] }) 10... d6 { [%clk 0:03:51] } 11. Qh5 { [%clk 0:04:46] } 11... g6 { [%clk 0:03:50] } (11... h6 { This is the correct move in the line to defend against mate. }) 12. Qh6 { [%clk 0:04:44] } 12... Re8 { [%clk 0:03:42] } 13. Bg5 { [%clk 0:04:42] } 13... Qg7 { [%clk 0:03:41] } 14. Qh4 { Danya walks into a line that is advantageous for white. White is threatening Bf6 } { [%csl Gf6][%cal Gg5f6] [%clk 0:04:42] } 14... h5 { Giving the queen a more active square in case white plays bf6. } { [%csl Gh6][%cal Gg7h6] [%clk 0:03:24] } (14... Bd7 15. Bf6 Qf8 { This forces black's queen into a passive position and Danya wanted to prevent this. }) 15. a4 { Threatening b4 and a5. } { [%csl Gb4,Ga5][%cal Gb2b4,Ga4a5] [%clk 0:04:25] } 15... a6 { Creating a hole for the bishop to hide. } { [%csl Ga7][%cal Gc5a7] [%clk 0:03:14] } 16. Rfe1 { [%clk 0:03:47] } 16... Be6 { [%clk 0:03:10] } 17. Bc4 { Should black trade bishops here? } { [%clk 0:03:12] } 17... Bxc4 { Yes, to draw the queen off of h4. } { [%clk 0:02:52] } 18. Qxc4 { [%clk 0:03:12] } 18... Re5 { Ideas of doubling rooks to control the e-file. } { [%csl Ge8][%cal Ga8e8] [%clk 0:02:34] } 19. Rxe5 { [%clk 0:02:42] } (19. Bf4 Rf5 { Allowing for similar ideas in the actual game, except this time with the rook attacking f2 rather than the queen. } { [%csl Rf2,Rf4][%cal Rf5f2] }) 19... Qxe5 { [%clk 0:02:33] } 20. Bf4 { [%clk 0:02:40] } 20... Qf5 { Pressuring the bishop and f2 pawn. } { [%csl Rf2,Rf4,Rc2][%cal Rf5f2,Rf5c2] [%clk 0:02:20] } (20... Qe7 { This allows black to continue ideas of controlling the e file. }) 21. b4 { [%clk 0:02:35] } 21... Ba7 { [%clk 0:02:13] } 22. Bg3 { [%clk 0:02:18] } 22... Re8 { Sacrificing the c7 pawn to seize control of the e file. } { [%csl Bc7] [%clk 0:02:00] } (22... c6 { Perhaps a better move to defend the pawn rather than sacrifice. }) 23. Qxc7 { Black has ideas of creating a backrank mate on e1. How can black do this without telegraphing the idea? } { [%csl Be1] [%clk 0:01:57] } 23... h4 { [%clk 0:01:31] } (23... Qe4 { This makes the mate obvious and white has an obvious response. } 24. h3 { Avoiding the backrank. }) 24. Qxb7! { [%clk 0:01:08] } (24. Bxh4 Qe4 { The idea here is to force the bishop onto a square that allows black to attack it and threaten checkmate. } { [%csl Rh4,Re1][%cal Re4h4,Re4e1] } 25. g3 Qf3) 24... Qe6 { [%clk 0:01:00] } (24... hxg3 25. Qxa7 { White's idea is to exchange bishops and position the queen such that it defends f2. } { [%csl Gf2][%cal Ga7f2] }) (24... Bb8 25. Bxh4 { This is problematic for black as now black is unable to bring the queen to e4 to attack the bishop and threaten mate. } { [%csl Ge4][%cal Gb7e4] }) 25. Rf1 { Strong defending move, preventing black's mating threats. } { [%clk 0:00:59] } 25... hxg3 { [%clk 0:00:52] } 26. Qxa7 { [%clk 0:00:58] } 26... gxh2+ { [%clk 0:00:51] } 27. Kxh2 { [%clk 0:00:57] } 27... Qe5+ { [%clk 0:00:49] } 28. Kg1 { [%clk 0:00:49] } 28... Qxc3 { [%clk 0:00:49] } 29. Qxa6 { [%clk 0:00:36] } 29... Qxb4 { [%clk 0:00:48] } 30. Qb5 { [%clk 0:00:35] } 30... Qe4 { [%clk 0:00:44] } 31. c4 { [%clk 0:00:31] } 31... Re5 { [%clk 0:00:33] } 32. Qb8+ { [%clk 0:00:30] } 32... Kg7 { [%clk 0:00:32] } 33. Qxd6 { [%clk 0:00:29] } 33... Rg5 { Threatening mate. } { [%csl Rg2][%cal Re4g2,Rg5g2] [%clk 0:00:32] } 34. g3 { Forcing white to weaken the kingside pawns. } { [%clk 0:00:24] } (34. Qh2 { Perhaps a better defending move that doesn't allow black to play for a win. }) 34... Rh5 { Threatening anohter mate. } { [%csl Rh1][%cal Rh5h1,Re4h1] [%clk 0:00:24] } 35. f3 { [%clk 0:00:22] } 35... Qxc4 { Trying to equalize material. } { [%clk 0:00:22] } 36. Rd1 { [%clk 0:00:19] } 36... Qe2 { [%clk 0:00:20] } 37. Qd2 { [%clk 0:00:16] } 37... Qxf3 { [%clk 0:00:17] } 38. Qd4+ { [%clk 0:00:16] } 38... Kh7 { [%clk 0:00:16] } 39. Qd5 { [%clk 0:00:14] } 39... Rh1# { 0-1 Black wins by checkmate. } { [%clk 0:00:15] } 0-1


