{ THEMES and Things to Look For
- The difference between having "potential energy" and "biting on granite" has to do with what is behind the blocked piece. If the blocking piece were removed, does the blocked piece now participate in some meaningful way? If yes, then it has "potential energy", if no then "biting on granite".

Stream footage here: https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=NQYFSC5TCnE
Game played here: https://www.chess.com/analysis/game/live/6036738198 }
1. e4 { [%clk 0:05:00] } 1... g6 { Black plays the modern. } { [%clk 0:05:00] } (1... d6 { The Modern can be compared and contrasted with another hypermodern opening, the Pirc. } 2. d4 Nf6 3. Nc3 g6) 2. d4 { [%clk 0:04:59] } 2... Bg7 { [%clk 0:05:00] } 3. Nc3 { [%clk 0:04:58] } 3... d6 { [%clk 0:04:59] } 4. Be3 { Nicely reinforcing the center. This is consistent with Danya's philosophy when playing against hypermodern openings to not over-extend and, instead, worry about the "quality" of control over the center. } { [%csl Gd4][%cal Ge3d4] [%clk 0:04:51] } 4... a6 { This is the main line. Black preparing b5.
